export const SKILL_FILES: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
  "mutinybim": "---\nname: mutinybim\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM core: read and change a MutinyBIM building model by writing scripts with the open-source kit. The kit's reference (organised by task, each with a worked example), checking a script, a preview run that saves nothing, landing the result after the person approves, saving a part script's parts; and four reads (model summary, element queries, networks, validation issues) to look before writing. Every change pauses for the person's approval and is decided by the model server. Activate it first; it names the opt-in tool family for each job that has no script yet (model, drafting, ducts and pipes, electrical, plumbing, views and sheets, coordination, groups and phases, parts, calculations, schedules). Trigger on: building model, MutinyBIM, BIM model, script, place a part, route a duct, what's in the model, validation issues, make sheets.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server from this package (/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"core\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"core\"\n  token-cap: \"3500\"\n  tools: \"bim_script_reference bim_check_output bim_run_script bim_apply_output bim_save_part_type bim_model_summary bim_query_elements bim_inspect_network bim_validation_issues\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM core\n\nMutinyBIM is an open-source, browser-based building model for every trade:\narchitecture, structure, mechanical, electrical, plumbing, fire protection,\nand the owner and contractor who price what is built. People edit a model\nlive in the browser; you edit the same model through the same model server,\nwith the same rules. The server decides every change against the latest\nrevision. Your job: read carefully, plan from what you read, show the person\nthe change, and report exactly what the server answered.\n\n## Script first: the script API is the product\n\nDo the job with a script. A script is TypeScript that imports only\n`@permitzip/bim-script` and returns either **edits** (ordinary model\ncommands, one undo step) or **part types**. The core tools are:\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_script_reference` | First in a thread, **once**: the index (no arguments) says whether a script machine is ready here (`script_machine.ready`) and lists the kit. Then only what the task needs: `search` a topic (sheets, schedules, rooms, levels, walls, duct and pipe routing, equipment, tags, dimensions, checks, imports, calculations; the two best matches come whole, the rest by name), `topic` for one function or recipe by name, `command` for one command's exact fields, `example` for a whole script, and the saved and starter scripts (`library: \"list\"` / `\"read\"` with `key`). Reads only |\n| `bim_check_output` | Lint and syntax findings for a `script`, each with its line, plus kit functions and commands that don't exist (with `model_id`, also what its reads would send); or the kernel's checks on an `output` change set. Saves nothing. Its `script_id` names the script from then on |\n| `bim_run_script` | Run the script as a **preview** on a sealed machine: what it creates, changes and deletes, or the part types it makes, plus `apply_with` and `script_id`. Saves nothing |\n| `bim_apply_output` (approval) | Land a previewed edits result: call it with `apply_with.args` unchanged (it names the script by id). The script runs again, with what the preview ran with, and lands only if its output is exactly what was previewed, as one undo step. An agent that ran the open kit in its own sandbox sends `output` and `provenance` (`script_hash`, `kit_version`, `client`) instead of `script`; the kernel checks every step again and the person still approves |\n| `bim_save_part_type` (approval) | Save previewed part types to the firm library (with `save_as`, also the script version that made them, unapproved). Asks every time |\n| `bim_model_summary` | Start here: counts by kind, levels, zones and disciplines, systems with total airflow, open issues, `notPlacedYet` (parts that exist but are not drawn yet), open questions and the **revision** |\n| `bim_query_elements` | Elements by kind (any kernel kind, `space`, `zone`, `drawing_underlay` and `question` included), `class` (a registry class id and its subclasses), level, zone, discipline or ids, with sizes, positions, airflow and connector ids. An unknown class is refused with close ids |\n| `bim_inspect_network` | One element's connections and its path up to the equipment, with airflow per piece |\n| `bim_validation_issues` | Open issues (disconnected connectors, undersized branches…) and the elements involved |\n\n### The workflow: reference → write → check → preview → approve\n\n1. **`bim_script_reference`** with no arguments, **once per thread**: never\n   ask for the index again. If it says `ready: false`, **do not write a\n   script**: tell the person why, and do the job with a family's tools\n   (below). Then search it for the task:\n   `bim_script_reference({ search: \"rooftop unit\" })` returns the recipe and\n   the best kit functions with a working example each; read another by name\n   with `topic`. Don't search the same topic twice.\n2. **Read the model** with the four reads: `bim_model_summary`, then\n   `bim_query_elements` on one level or zone. On large models always pass\n   `level` (and `zone`); never load a whole model to answer a question about\n   one room.\n3. **Write the script.** Start from the example of each function you use.\n   Declare exactly the `reads` it needs (levels, kinds, ids or `\"selection\"`,\n   part types). Every length is a measure (`units.ft(10)`), never a bare\n   number.\n4. **`bim_check_output`** with `script`, whenever you are not sure of a\n   function, a command or the reads: it costs far less than a failed\n   preview. Fix every finding and read the warnings.\n5. **`bim_run_script`**. A failure names the script's line (\"Line 14: Step 3\n   (route_run) was refused: …\"). Tell the person in plain words what the\n   preview does: how many things it creates, changes and deletes, the label,\n   any check that failed; for part types, each part and its connectors.\n6. **Approve.** Only when the status is `previewed`, call the tool in\n   `apply_with.tool` with `apply_with.args` **unchanged** (`bim_apply_output`\n   for edits, `bim_save_part_type` for parts, with `save_as` filled in). It\n   waits for the person's approval. Report the saved revision, or the\n   refusal, word for word.\n\n**Send a script's text once.** The first call carries\n`script: { source, reads }`. Every answer about it has a `script_id`; after\nthat, name it:\n\n- the preview after a check: `script: { id }`;\n- a fix after an error: `script: { id, replace: [{ old: \"<exact text>\",\n  new: \"<fixed text>\" }] }`. Change only what the error names, with one or\n  two replacements; `old` must be in the script exactly once. Never send the\n  whole script again to change a line;\n- landing: `apply_with.args` already names the id, and runs with the values\n  and selection the preview ran with.\n\n`code: \"runner_error\"` is the script machine, not the script: run it once\nmore by id (`retry_with`) and don't rewrite it. \"That preview has expired\"\nmeans the id is too old: send the script again. A server that answers with no\n`script_id` keeps no scripts; send `{ source, reads }` each time there.\n\n`has_issues` or `refused`: nothing changed; fix the script or the values and\npreview again. `stale_preview`: the model moved; run it again and show the\nnew preview. One result is one undo step of at most 500 steps; split bigger\njobs.\n\n**Prefer one script for repeated steps.** Connect twelve fixtures to a drain,\nplace and duct six units, a sheet and a plan view per level, walls with\ntheir doors and windows: one script that reads once and loops gives one\npreview, one approval and one undo, for a fraction of the tokens of dozens of\nsingle calls. Use a family's single tools for one or two edits.\n\n### The kit\n\n`import { defineScript, units, routes, parts, building, equipment, documents } from \"@permitzip/bim-script\"`\n\n- `units.ft / in / mm / m / of`: every length is a measure.\n- `parts.part(name, { type, category })` → `box` / `cylinder` / `transition`\n  → `connector(face, { id, label, domain, systemKind, shape, size…, endType })`\n  → `ctx.parts.define(typeId, builder.build())`, in a **part** script only. A\n  layout or route script uses part types by listing their ids in\n  `reads.partTypes` and placing them with `equipment.place`; it never calls\n  `ctx.parts.define`. Duct connectors need an end\n  type (`mechanical.slip`, `mechanical.flanged`, `mechanical.formed_flange`);\n  pipe ones too (`plumbing_gas.sweat`, `plumbing_gas.threaded`, …).\n- `building.level`, `building.walls`, `building.floor`, `building.door`,\n  `building.window`, `building.space`, `building.spaceInWalls`,\n  `building.ceiling` (outline and height as `units.*` values).\n- **Level arguments are level ids** (from `building.level` or\n  `ctx.model.levels()` with `reads.levels: true`). The kit's helpers also take\n  the name of exactly one level the script read; raw `ctx.edit` commands take\n  only the id.\n- **Read what a helper works from.** `building.spaceInWalls` traces walls: put\n  `\"wall\"` in `reads.kinds` unless the script draws them first.\n  `bim_check_output` warns when a call needs data the reads don't send.\n- **See a value with `ctx.report(label, value)`**, never by throwing an\n  error: it comes back as `report` in `bim_run_script`'s answer (50 reports,\n  16 KB). A script that only reports still runs.\n- `units.*` values are measures, not numbers. Give them to kit helpers; a raw\n  `ctx.edit` command takes plain millimetres (`units.in_(length, \"mm\")`).\n- `equipment.place(ctx, { level, type, at })` → `placed.connector(\"supply\")`\n  is a route end.\n- `routes.along` (with `slope: { rise, per }` for drains), `routes.branch`,\n  `routes.drop`, `routes.connectInto`; sizes `routes.rect / round / oval / pipe`.\n- `documents.sheet`, `planView`, `ceilingView`, `duplicateView`, `place` (a\n  view on a sheet), `editSchedule`. There is no sheet set function: number\n  the sheets in print order; the person picks the sheets when printing or\n  issuing.\n- Anything else: `ctx.edit(command)` with any model command; its fields come\n  from `bim_script_reference({ command })`. Circuits and loads are the\n  add-ons' commands; search for them.\n\nScripts never print or issue a set: the person does that, or the `model`\nfamily's print and issue tools after the person says to.\n\n### The sealed machine and its caps\n\nA script runs on its own sealed machine: **no network, at most 1 GB of\nmemory, 30 s and 2 MB of output**, a fresh process per run. It reads only the\nslice of the model it declares and never writes to the model itself; the\nkernel checks every output before anyone sees it. A run that passes a cap is\nstopped (`status: \"stopped\"`, `code: \"cap_memory\"`, `\"cap_time\"` or\n`\"cap_output\"`) with `nothing_changed: true`: say which cap stopped it.\n**Never run the same script with the same values on the same revision\nagain** (it answers `not_run: true`); read less, make less per run, or split\nthe job.\n\n### Approve for this session (low risk only)\n\n`approval` on the preview says how the result is asked for:\n\n- `session_allowed`: a low-risk result. The person may approve it for the\n  rest of the session; later low-risk results then land without asking.\n- `ask_every_time`: deletes, large batches (more than 50 changes), edits\n  across systems, anything touching approvals or sign-offs. Say why\n  (`approval_reasons`).\n\nSaving to the firm library always asks. Never change `approval` in\n`apply_with.args`. A saved script version may be run by other agents only\nafter a person approves that version; you never approve a script version.\n\n## Rules for every MutinyBIM tool\n\nThese hold here and in every family.\n\n- **Plan from what you read.** Reads return the revision they read. Pass it\n  as `base_revision` on the change you plan from it; after a saved change,\n  the next one's `base_revision` is the saved revision.\n- **Preview first.** `bim_run_script` and the families' previews\n  (`bim_preview_part_edit`, `bim_preview_route_edit`, …) say what a change would do and save nothing, or\n  `would_refuse` with the reason. Fix the plan before asking anyone to\n  approve.\n- **Say the change in plain words**, then make it. Every change pauses for\n  the person's approval. One clear change per call.\n- **The server's answer is the result.** Report it verbatim:\n  - `saved` → \"Saved as Rev N. Changed: …\". Only now is the change done.\n    `open_issues: N` means the model already had N issues your change didn't\n    touch: say so, don't call the model clean.\n  - `conflict` → someone changed the same thing after your read. Read again\n    at `latestRevision`. If your change no longer collides, redo it on the\n    new revision; if it would replace their value, say who changed what and\n    ask. Never retry blindly.\n  - `refused` → the message is the rule broken (for example \"branch is as\n    wide as its host\"), with `code` and the `element_ids` involved. Explain\n    it, suggest a valid alternative; do not try random variations. \"doesn't\n    take <key>\" lists the keys and units it does take: resend with those,\n    never drop the value silently.\n  - `not_saved_yet` → the server could not confirm. It retries with the same\n    key. Do not send a different change to compensate; read again to check.\n- Never say a change was made because you called the tool, or because it was\n  approved.\n- **Look at what you drew.** After anything that changes a drawing, look at\n  it (the `model` family's `bim_render_view_image` and\n  `bim_render_sheet_image`) before saying it is done.\n- **Locked groups.** Nothing in a locked group can be created, changed,\n  moved or deleted, by anyone, agents included. Report the refusal word for\n  word and ask whether to unlock it; never unlock a group unless the person\n  asked for exactly that, and never route around a lock.\n- **Never claim \"clash-free\", \"coordinated\" or \"complete\"** unless the\n  `model` family's `bim_lint_check` ran on the scope you describe, with the\n  linked models included, and returned zero errors.\n- A tool whose arguments are a family of commands advertises a short form.\n  `bim_describe_commands` (model family) returns the exact fields of one\n  command; a call that doesn't fit is answered with the field and what is\n  wrong.\n\n## One vocabulary in every tool\n\n- **`element_id`** names the element in every tool that takes one. Older\n  keys (`tap_id`, `terminal_id`, `segment_id`, `equipment_id`, `wall_id`…)\n  still work; give one, not both.\n- **A number with its unit:** values take `{ \"value\": 22, \"unit\": \"kA\" }`\n  beside a bare number. The model stores the parameter's own unit and\n  refuses a unit of the wrong kind, listing the ones it takes. A bare length\n  is feet; answers give heights and distances as `{ value, unit }`.\n- **Levels** take the level's id or its name; a loose spelling works when\n  only one level matches (\"LEVEL 01\" finds \"Level 1\"). An unknown or\n  ambiguous level is refused with the candidates.\n- **Units:** distances, offsets and elevations in feet; duct sizes and paper\n  positions in inches; airflow in CFM; pipe by nominal inches plus material.\n\n## Families: single-purpose tools, opt-in\n\nThe single-purpose tools come in families. Turn one on only when the\nreference says a job has no script yet, or for a one-off edit: on a\nMutinyBIM tool server that is `bim_enable_pack` (or connecting that family's\nown endpoint); a host that loads skills turns a family on with the skill of\nthe same name. **One family at a time, and only the one the task in front of\nyou needs. Never turn on every family, and never turn one on \"in case\"**:\nevery tool is re-sent on every step (four families is about 200 tools), and\nlong tool lists confuse models. Turn one off when the task moves on.\n\n| Family (skill) | Turn it on for |\n| --- | --- |\n| `mutinybim-model` | Schedule rows, levels, views and sheets, linked models, what is under or over a point; previews of part, route and views/sheets edits; placing, moving, copying and deleting parts; pictures, printing and issuing a set; coordination lint |\n| `mutinybim-views-sheets` | A drawing set: views, crops, 3D views, sheets, title blocks, viewports and layout, sheet notes |\n| `mutinybim-drafting` | Dimensions, tags and tag styles, notes with leaders, detail linework and components |\n| `mutinybim-ducts-pipes` | Routing, joining, resizing, sloping and insulating duct and pipe runs; fittings; terminal airflow |\n| `mutinybim-electrical` | Panels, circuits, feeders, receptacle layouts, one-line and riser diagrams |\n| `mutinybim-plumbing` | Load points and connection types for every trade, pipe types, each pipe system's look and sizing |\n| `mutinybim-calcs` | Code calculations with citations, duct size advice, pressure and pipe reports, pipe sizing proposals, service size |\n| `mutinybim-coordination` | Linking the architect's model, levels from it, coordinates, accepting findings, tracing rooms |\n| `mutinybim-groups-phases` | Groups, locks, phases, demolition, phase filters; a typed element's class, type and values |\n| `mutinybim-parts` | Parts from a cut sheet or a description, the firm's part library, clearance zones, hosts, mounting planes |\n| `mutinybim-schedules` | Defining schedules, typing into their cells, the library of shared fields and templates |\n\nA whole drawing set is `mutinybim-model` plus `mutinybim-views-sheets`, with\n`mutinybim-drafting` when the sheets need dimensions, tags or details.\nCircuiting and a one-line need `mutinybim-electrical`, and `mutinybim-calcs`\nto size what they show.\n\n## How to start\n\n1. `bim_script_reference` (is a script machine ready?), then\n   `bim_model_summary` for the revision and what is there.\n2. The read that matches the job: elements on one level\n   (`bim_query_elements`), a run's path (`bim_inspect_network`), problems\n   (`bim_validation_issues`). Anything that depends on the architect's model\n   (levels, roof heights, line-up) starts with the `model` family's\n   `bim_list_linked_models`.\n3. Search the reference for the task and write the script. If the reference\n   has no recipe for it, turn on the one family that does.\n\n**List first, draw second.** A part can exist before it is drawn: it waits in\nthe Not placed yet list (`notPlacedYet` in `bim_model_summary`) with its\ncircuits, schedule rows and one-line place. Check that list before saying a\njob is drawn, and never invent a location to get a part onto the drawing: ask\nwhere it goes.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this core\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_script_reference` |\n| `bim_check_output` |\n| `bim_run_script` |\n| `bim_apply_output` |\n| `bim_save_part_type` |\n| `bim_model_summary` |\n| `bim_query_elements` |\n| `bim_inspect_network` |\n| `bim_validation_issues` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-model": "---\nname: mutinybim-model\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM family for model reads and everyday edits: schedule rows, part types, levels, views and sheets, linked models, surfaces at a point; previews of part, route and views/sheets edits; placing, moving, rotating, copying and deleting parts, parameters, moving walls; pictures of views and sheets; printing and issuing a set; coordination lint and status snapshots. Use with the mutinybim core. Trigger on: list levels, list views, list sheets, schedule rows, preview an edit, place a part, move equipment, delete elements, render a view, print the set, issue the set, lint.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the model family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs model, or /packs/model/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"model\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"model\"\n  token-cap: \"9600\"\n  tools: \"bim_schedule_read bim_list_part_types bim_list_levels bim_list_views_and_sheets bim_list_linked_models bim_surfaces_at bim_describe_commands bim_preview_part_edit bim_preview_route_edit bim_preview_views_sheets_edit bim_apply_views_sheets_edit bim_render_view_image bim_render_sheet_image bim_print_sheet_pdf bim_print_view_pdf bim_print_set_pdf bim_issue_set bim_place_part bim_move_elements bim_rotate_elements bim_copy_elements bim_delete_elements bim_set_parameters bim_move_wall bim_lint_configure bim_lint_check bim_status_snapshot\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: model reads and everyday edits\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the four starting reads and the rules for every change. Turn\nthis family on for the reads a script plans from (levels, views and sheets,\nschedule rows, linked models, what is under a point), the previews of the\nfamily edits, single part edits, pictures, printing and issuing, and the\ncoordination lint. For more than one or two changes, prefer one script.\n\n## Coordination lint: you are not working blind\n\nEvery saved modeling, drafting, view and sheet edit comes back with a `lint`\nblock from the same engine people use in Manage › Coordination: `new`\nfindings the edit caused (rule, severity, element ids with `linked:<GlobalId>`\nfor the architect's model, a plain message, a `fix` tool and hint),\n`resolved` finding ids, `near` (level, room, the surface under it, things\nwithin the scope radius), `open_errors` around the edit, and `truncated`.\n`status: \"deferred\"` means it ran past its time; call `bim_lint_check` for\nthat area. A refused edit carries `lint.blocking`: the open issues on what it\nblames.\n\n1. **Configure once.** Call `bim_lint_configure` at the start of a modeling\n   session (ruleset, tolerance; `snapshot: \"auto_if_vision\"` adds a small\n   picture every few edits when this thread's model reads images, and none\n   when it doesn't). Never pass lint options per edit.\n2. **Read the `lint` block after every edit.** A new `error` is fixed (with\n   the tool its `fix` names, or another plan) or explained to the person\n   before the next edit. Never stack edits on a new hard clash.\n3. **Never claim \"clash-free\", \"coordinated\" or \"complete\"** unless\n   `bim_lint_check` ran with `include_linked: true` on the scope you describe\n   and returned zero errors. Quote its ruleset and tolerance. Validation,\n   open issues, network, pressure and part checks don't look at shape.\n4. **Render and look.** After placing or making parts, finishing an\n   assembly, or laying out a sheet, call `bim_status_snapshot` (or a cropped\n   `bim_render_view_image` plus the sheet render) and say what you see. If\n   the picture and the model disagree (a part that doesn't draw), stop and\n   report it.\n5. **Issue sets.** Run `bim_lint_check` with ruleset `issue` before\n   `bim_issue_set`, and print again if the schedules changed since.\n6. **Accepting a finding is the person's call.** When the person says a\n   finding is acceptable, `bim_lint_accept_finding` (in\n   `mutinybim-coordination`) records it with their reason, using an `id`\n   from `bim_lint_check` (other ids are refused). Never accept one to make a\n   check pass, or to try the tool out. `bim_lint_retract_acceptance` takes\n   one back, with the person's reason.\n7. **Don't change a person's own view** (`{3D - name}`): duplicate it.\n\nUnits: distances, offsets and elevations in feet; duct sizes and paper\npositions in inches; airflow in CFM; pipe by nominal inches plus material.\nKernel commands inside `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` are millimetres\nunless you use the standalone tools' names (`crop_min_ft`, `cut_height_ft`,\n`center_in`, top-level `level`…), which are converted.\n\nLevels: every level input (`level`, `level_id`, area `level` on reads and\nedits, lint, schedules, views, `bim_update_level`) takes the level's id or its\nname. A loose spelling works when only one level matches (\"LEVEL 01\" finds\n\"Level 1\"). An unknown or ambiguous level is refused with the candidates; pick\none or call `bim_list_levels`. Views and schedules store the level's real name,\nso renaming the level carries them along.\n\n## Tools\n\n### Reads (no approval)\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_schedule_read` | Rows of any schedule view (or an ad hoc definition): columns with unit and whether a cell edits per instance or per type (or why it's locked), rows as `[row_key, ...cells]` (≤200 per call from `offset`), totals, `explain` for locked cells. No `schedule`: lists the schedule views. Panel and feeder form schedules return what prints |\n| `bim_list_part_types` | Placeable types in every discipline (model's own first, then the starter library), their parameters, choices, connectors, plus levels |\n| `bim_list_levels` | The model's levels (name, elevation in feet, the linked storey each was made from) and every linked model's storeys at their height in this model, with what `bim_sync_linked_levels` would do to each (`in_step` when nothing would change) |\n| `bim_list_views_and_sheets` | Every view (plan, ceiling plan, section, 3D, detail) with level, scale, crop and sheet; every sheet with viewports, notes and live `blocks` (id, title, what it says now, `unresolved` fields) |\n| `bim_surfaces_at` | **What is under and over a part or a point**: the floor (or roof) it stands on and the ceiling, roof and structure above, each `height` as `{ value, unit: \"ft\" }` (with `above_floor`) and its `source` in words (the host it stands on, a space's ceiling height, our slabs, a linked model's slab, roof or ceiling). Give `element_id` (RTU-1's id), or `x_ft`, `y_ft` and `level`. Use it for \"how high is the roof where RTU-1 sits\", ceiling heights for devices, roof curbs. If the linked models can't be read it answers from this model and says so in `notes` |\n| `bim_list_linked_models` | Every linked model: source (IFC upload, our Revit export, a Docs file), the version shown and every version with its status (`queued`, `converting`, `ready`, `failed` with the reason), `alignment` and what fixes it (`fix`), the position adjustment in feet, the latest export from Revit and Docs files still coming in |\n| `bim_print_sheet_pdf` | Vector PDF of one sheet at true scale from the **last saved** revision; 5-minute link with revision and sha256. Does not issue the sheet |\n| `bim_print_view_pdf` | Vector PDF of one view on its own (plan, section, 3D, detail or drafting view, schedule), sized to the view at its scale; 5-minute link with revision and sha256. For several sheets in one file use `bim_print_set_pdf` |\n| `bim_render_view_image` | **Look at a view**: a PNG of any view exactly as it prints, returned as an image you can see. `region_ft` (view feet) or `region_in` (paper inches) to zoom, `focus_ids` to frame elements, `highlight_ids` to outline them in red, `dpi` / `max_px` (1,280 px on the long side unless you ask; zoom into a region before raising it). `keep_copy` stores the PNG and returns a link for the person |\n| `bim_render_sheet_image` | **Look at a sheet**: a PNG of one sheet as it prints (viewports, notes, schedules, title block). A whole 36 x 24 sheet is an overview; zoom with `region_in` (paper inches from the top-left) to read notes and tags |\n\n### Coordination lint\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_lint_configure` (no approval) | Once per session: ruleset (`default`, `coordination`, `documents`, `issue`), tolerance and clearances, scope radius, linked models on or off, `snapshot`. Saved for this thread (`for: \"me\"`: your default on this model). Returns the settings and the rules this model can't run yet |\n| `bim_lint_check` (no approval) | Every finding on the model, a `level`, or around `ids`, with the ruleset and tolerance used; `include_linked`, `include_accepted`; 25 a page (`offset`). The only basis for saying something is clash-free. With linked models, read `linked_coverage`: `complete` (every linked model checked), `none` (the project links none), `indexing` / `incomplete` (status `partial`, \"linked coverage INCOMPLETE: N of M\"): clashes with linked models are missing, so re-run in a minute and never call it clash-free until `complete` |\n| `bim_status_snapshot` (no approval) | End of a batch: counts, open findings by rule, this thread's last edits, up to 3 small pictures (the last-edited plan, sheet, a 3D view, or the `views` / `sheets` you name). Look at them and say what you see. Findings carry the same `linked_coverage`; the `lint` block on a saved edit never waits for linked models, so the first edits of a session may say they are still being indexed |\n\n### Previews and the views/sheets apply\n\n| Tool | Covers |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_preview_part_edit` | `bim_place_part`, `bim_move_elements`, `bim_rotate_elements`, `bim_copy_elements`, `bim_delete_elements`, `bim_set_parameters`, and from packs `bim_create_level`, `bim_save_part_definition` and `bim_set_element` |\n| `bim_preview_route_edit` | Every duct and pipe edit in `mutinybim-ducts-pipes`: `bim_route_run`, `bim_connect_runs`, `bim_disconnect_run`, `bim_resize_run`, `bim_slide_branch`, `bim_swap_fitting`, `bim_offset_run`, `bim_change_elevation`, `bim_set_system_design`, `bim_set_slope`, `bim_place_pipe_accessory`, `bim_split_run`, `bim_resize_stretch`, `bim_drag_run`, `bim_set_duct_insulation`; also `bim_set_fitting_coefficient_override` (in `mutinybim-calcs`) |\n| `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` | Any views/sheets command (create / rename / duplicate / delete view, set view properties or crop, sheets and title blocks incl. `revisions`, viewports, drawing items: line, rect, cloud, dimension, and notes: `create_text_note`, `edit_text_note`, `resize_text_note`, `move_text_note`, `delete_text_note`), **or a `batch_documents` of up to 500 of them** (all or nothing, one approval). Also, one at a time: live sheet blocks (`add_field_block`, `edit_field_block`) and spaces, zones and underlays (`create_space`, `edit_space`, `delete_spaces`, `make_spaces_from_rooms`, `create_zone`, `edit_zone`, `delete_zones`, `place_underlay`, `calibrate_underlay`, `edit_underlay`, `delete_underlays`), loads, and questions (`add_question`, `answer_question`, `set_question_status`, `edit_question`, `delete_questions`). Returns a `preview_token`; save exactly that change with `bim_apply_views_sheets_edit`. Lengths in mm, paper positions and note widths / text heights in paper mm |\n| `bim_apply_views_sheets_edit` (approval) | Save exactly the change `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` returned: same `command`, its `base_revision` and `preview_token`. A different command or revision is refused before anything is sent; a retry never saves twice. This is how a whole batch of sheets, viewports or annotation edits saves in one approval |\n\n### Parts, any discipline (each needs approval)\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_place_part` | Place equipment, terminals, receptacles, panels, lights, fixtures… on a level at a plan position. Values are checked against the type. **No `level_id`, `x_ft`, `y_ft`: the part is planned** (exists for circuits, schedules and the one-line, waits in Not placed yet; optional `level_hint`, `space_hint`, `note`). `part_id` instead of `type_id` with a location draws that planned part (same id, circuits and values kept); `part_id` with `unplace: true` takes a placed part off the drawing (disconnect its runs first). A firm-library size (`bim_list_firm_parts`, in `mutinybim-parts`) needs `from_firm_library: true`. `mount: \"turned_over\"` (a floor register: neck down) or `\"on_side\"` (a sidewall register, facing `rotation_deg`); `on_plane_id` puts it on a mounting plane at the plane's height; `intakes` sets a hood / louver / screen on an outdoor-air opening. **`equipment_list` (alone): a pasted vendor or mechanical schedule** becomes planned items, one per unit (K-14 ×2 → K-14.1, K-14.2) with each row's volts, phase, load, gas MBH and water / waste sizes, in one approval, exactly as Equipment list › Add. A row the reader can't use refuses the whole list and names the row: tell the person, never guess the value |\n| `bim_move_elements` | Move parts by an offset (also up/down); equipment drags its connected ducts; tags follow |\n| `bim_rotate_elements` | Rotate about the insertion point, a pivot, or the group centre |\n| `bim_copy_elements` | Copy by an offset; copies keep type and values, start without a mark |\n| `bim_delete_elements` | Delete. Default `refuse` lists dependents; `disconnect` cuts connections; `cascade` removes dependents too. Preview first and say what goes |\n| `bim_set_parameters` | Set instance values (this part) or type values (every part of that type); a value may be `{ value, unit }`. Instance scope also takes `mount` (disconnect its runs first) and `intakes` |\n| `bim_move_wall` | Move a wall; its doors, windows, tags and room dimensions follow |\n\nNew levels are `mutinybim-coordination`; new part types are `mutinybim-parts`.\n\n### Print and issue the set\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_print_set_pdf` (read) | Every sheet (or the `sheets` given, in that order) from the last saved revision to **one** vector PDF with linked backgrounds, in binding order (G, H, V, B, C, L, S, A, I, Q, F, FP, P, D, M, E, T…, then number). Returns pages, revision, sha256 and a 5-minute link, and records how each sheet drew for the issue check. Does not issue |\n| `bim_issue_set` (approval) | Issue (live blocks on the issued sheets are frozen as issued; afterwards each reads \"Matches\" or \"Changed since\" that issue): optional `add_revision` (one row on every issued sheet, one edit), then capture the model at that revision, draw the set **only from the capture**, store the snapshot, PDF and manifest write-once and hand the PDF to the host to file with the project's issued drawings. `expected_revision` = the revision you checked; if anything was saved since, **nothing is issued**. Refused as `needs_print` when a sheet draws differently than in the last `bim_print_set_pdf` (live schedules): print again, or `accept_changes_since_print: true` after checking those sheets. Check first with `bim_set_issue_status` (read only, in `mutinybim-views-sheets`; it gives the `revision` to pass as `expected_revision`). A retry returns the same issue |\n\nIssue only when the person says to issue, with `expected_revision` = the\nrevision you printed and checked. If it says the model moved, someone saved\nsince you checked: review, print, and ask again. Never issue a set you didn't\nprint and check.\n\n**Locked groups.** Nothing in a locked group can be created, changed, moved,\ndemolished or deleted, by anyone, agents included; an edit that would touch\none is refused and nothing changes. Report the refusal word for word and ask\nthe person whether to unlock the group (`mutinybim-groups-phases`). Never\nunlock a group yourself unless the person asked for exactly that, and never\nroute around a lock.\n\n## Workflows\n\n**Place a part:** `bim_list_part_types` (category or search) → pick type and\nlevel → `bim_preview_part_edit` with `tool: \"bim_place_part\"` → describe →\n`bim_place_part` with `base_revision`.\n\n**List first, draw second.** A part can exist before it is drawn: plan it\nwith `bim_place_part` and no location, circuit it, schedule it, put it on the\none-line, then place it when the drawing is ready. Check the Not placed yet\nlist (`bim_model_summary`) before saying a job is drawn, and never invent a\nlocation to get a part onto the drawing: ask where it goes. People see the\nsame list in the MutinyBIM editor (Electrical, Mechanical or Plumbing tab ›\nNot placed yet) and can place each one in the view they are in.\n\n**Kitchen equipment list → circuits → schematic design.** First turn on\nevery family the job needs: `mutinybim-electrical` (it has\n`bim_electrical_report`, `bim_place_service_layout` and `bim_create_circuit`)\nand, for the schematic design issue, `mutinybim-views-sheets`. The person\npastes the vendor's schedule: `bim_place_part` with `equipment_list` (one\napproval; the items wait in Not placed yet). Once it saves, go straight on to\nthe panel and the circuits in the same turn; the list is the first step, not\nthe end. No panel yet (`bim_electrical_report` lists\nnone): plan the main panel the way \"Add a planned panel (MDP)\" does,\n`bim_place_service_layout` with `plan_only: true`, the service size and system\nthe person gives, distribution name MDP (one approval). Then\n`bim_create_circuit` with `source_id` the panel, `members: []` and\n`one_circuit_each: true` is Circuit all: every item waiting for a circuit gets\nits own, breaker sized by the project's NEC edition (one approval). Read back\n`circuits` and `not_circuited` (an item on the wrong voltage says so: report\nit, never move it to another panel yourself). Issue the schematic design set only when asked\n(`mutinybim-views-sheets`).\n\n**Loads list → building drain.** Turn on `mutinybim-schedules` and\n`mutinybim-calcs` first. `bim_schedule_define` with\n`template: \"loads\"` and a `name` previews the built-in Loads list (nothing\nsaved, a `preview_token`); call it again with the same arguments plus\n`base_revision`, `preview_token` and `user_confirmed: true`, which waits on the\nApprove card. Then `bim_schedule_read` for its totals, and `bim_run_calc`\n(`plumbing.size_drain`, role `building_drain`) for the total drainage fixture\nunits. Report the DFU total and the size with the clause it cites.\n\n**Questions and assumptions.** When the engineer says \"ask the architect\nwhether …\", pin a question to the elements it is about: `add_question`\n`{ questions: [{ questionId, text, askOf: \"architect\", anchor: { elementIds },\nblocksIssue? }] }` through `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` →\n`bim_apply_views_sheets_edit`. `answer_question` records the answer\n(`status: \"assumed\"` with the assumption the design goes ahead on);\n`set_question_status` withdraws (`withdrawn`) or asks again (`open`). Before\ndrafting an email, `bim_model_summary` with `questions: \"<audience>\"`: it\nskips what the model already answers and returns the text only (nothing is\nsent: the person sends it). A sheet that shows an element with an open\nholding question can't be issued: quote the hold reason the refusal gives,\nnever say only \"it can't issue\".\n\n**Loads, one concept for every trade.** A load starts as an estimate on a\nspace or zone (lighting 5 kW on the kitchen), becomes a point, then a real\ndevice once placed, and keeps its id the whole way. Everywhere a total shows,\nthe estimate is shown against the devices so far (\"estimate 5,000 VA, devices\nso far 800 VA\"); the estimate counts until the devices exceed it. Say it that\nway, never \"allocated\" alone. Create, move, name a source or set the policy\nwith `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` (`create_load`, `move_load`,\n`split_load`, `edit_load_point` with `servedBy`, `set_load_allocation_policy`)\nor, with `mutinybim-plumbing`, `bim_edit_load_point`. `bim_query_elements` on\na space or zone returns `loads` in those words.\n\n**Spaces and zones.** Most jobs have only a PDF or a CAD file, no linked\nmodel. People draw spaces by tracing over that drawing (Spaces › Draw space);\nthe area comes from the outline, never typed. You may make spaces from the\narchitect's rooms (`make_spaces_from_rooms`), type rows that have no outline\nyet (`create_space` without `outline`), group spaces into zones and say what\nserves each (`create_zone` / `edit_zone` `servedBy`, planned equipment\nincluded), and place or scale an underlay when the person asks. Never invent\nan outline. All of these go through `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` →\n`bim_apply_views_sheets_edit`. Each space says how its estimates count\n(`estimates_count` on its row); `edit_space { spaceId, estimateCounting }`\nsets `max` (estimate until devices exceed it), `actual_only`,\n`allocation_only`, or `null` (the model's setting), the same as the space's\nLoads › Estimates count field.\n\n**Live load summary on a sheet:** `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` with\n`{ type: \"add_field_block\", itemId, sheetId, at_in: { x, y } }` (no `block`:\nthe electrical load summary at the service; `block: { template, title,\nsubjectId }` for another; `block: { table: { kind: \"ventilation\" } }` or\n`\"pressurization\"` for that table, the whole model unless `scope: { kind:\n\"level\", levelId }` or `{ kind: \"zone\", zoneId }`) → approve →\n`bim_list_views_and_sheets` shows what it says now. `edit_field_block` changes it (`title: null` removes the title);\nmove it with `update_drawing_item { itemId, points: [at] }`. It updates with\nevery edit and freezes when the set is issued; `bim_set_issue_status` says\nwhether it still matches the last issue.\n\n**Rebalance airflow:** `bim_query_elements` (terminals, CFM) → activate\n`mutinybim-ducts-pipes` → one `bim_set_terminal_airflow` per terminal, each on\nthe latest saved revision → read the terminals again and report the new totals.\n\n**Produce a construction drawing set:** activate `mutinybim-views-sheets` and\nfollow its \"Produce a construction drawing set\" workflow (plan, views, sheets\nin one approval, layout, schedules, notes, verify, print, issue). Add\n`mutinybim-drafting` for dimensions, tags and details.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_schedule_read` |\n| `bim_list_part_types` |\n| `bim_list_levels` |\n| `bim_list_views_and_sheets` |\n| `bim_list_linked_models` |\n| `bim_surfaces_at` |\n| `bim_describe_commands` |\n| `bim_preview_part_edit` |\n| `bim_preview_route_edit` |\n| `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` |\n| `bim_apply_views_sheets_edit` |\n| `bim_render_view_image` |\n| `bim_render_sheet_image` |\n| `bim_print_sheet_pdf` |\n| `bim_print_view_pdf` |\n| `bim_print_set_pdf` |\n| `bim_issue_set` |\n| `bim_place_part` |\n| `bim_move_elements` |\n| `bim_rotate_elements` |\n| `bim_copy_elements` |\n| `bim_delete_elements` |\n| `bim_set_parameters` |\n| `bim_move_wall` |\n| `bim_lint_configure` |\n| `bim_lint_check` |\n| `bim_status_snapshot` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-drafting": "---\nname: mutinybim-drafting\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for drafting and annotation: dimensions and automatic dimension strings, tags, tag styles and automatic tagging, notes with leaders, editing annotations, detail linework, hatched and masking regions, detail components, batt insulation and the modify tools (trim, extend, split, fillet, offset, mirror, array) on drawn items. Use with the mutinybim core. Trigger on: dimension, tag, annotate, leader, note, detail, hatch, detail component, linework, trim, fillet, offset, mirror, array, drafting view.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the drafting family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs drafting, or /packs/drafting/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"drafting\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"drafting\"\n  token-cap: \"8200\"\n  tools: \"bim_list_view_annotations bim_add_dimension bim_auto_dimension bim_add_tag bim_preview_auto_tag bim_auto_tag_view bim_add_note bim_edit_annotation bim_set_tag_text bim_list_tag_styles bim_save_tag_style bim_apply_tag_style bim_list_detail_components bim_draw_detail bim_modify_detail bim_copy_detail_items bim_set_detail_group\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: drafting, annotation and detailing\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the four starting reads and the rules for every change: preview, say the change, edit with `base_revision`, report the server's\nanswer, look at what you drew. The views/sheets preview and apply (a\n`batch_documents` of many annotation edits saves in one approval) and the\npictures you check your drawing with (`bim_render_view_image`,\n`bim_render_sheet_image`) are the `model` family. Tags and dimensions on\nevery plan of a set are one script.\n\nNew views, crops, sheets and sheet notes, and replacing a note's words\n(`bim_edit_text_note`), are the `mutinybim-views-sheets` pack.\n\n## Drafting and annotation (dimensions, tags, notes with leaders)\n\nAnnotation belongs to the view it is drawn in (a plan, ceiling plan, section or\ndetail view) or to a sheet, and prints at paper size at any view scale. Points\nin a view are **view feet** (plan x / y; a section's distance along the cut and\nelevation; a detail view's drawing feet); on a sheet, paper inches. Offsets and\ntext heights are paper inches.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_list_view_annotations` (read) | Every dimension, tag, note, line, rectangle and cloud in a view or on a sheet: id, what it prints, points, paper box, what each dimension or tag follows (and any it lost), note leaders, and which annotations overlap. Read this before editing a detail or tidying a plan |\n| `bim_add_dimension` (approval) | linear (horizontal / vertical), aligned, angular, radial, diameter, arc_length, spot_elevation, spot_coordinate. Each point either **follows an element** (`element_id` + `anchor`: start, end, mid, edge_left, edge_right, center) or is a fixed point. Several points with `chain`: continuous or baseline. `offset_in`, `text_override`, `prefix`, `suffix`, `style` (units, rounding, tick / arrow / dot, text height) |\n| `bim_auto_dimension` (approval) | Deterministic: `each` dimensions every run, wall or grid along its length; `chain` puts one continuous string across several elements' centrelines. One edit; every dimension follows its element |\n| `bim_add_tag` (approval) | Tag one element in a plan or section. `template` auto (by what it is), duct_size, pipe_size, mark, air_terminal (mark + CFM), equipment, fixture, or fields (parameter names). The words always come from the element. `at_ft` for the head, else the first clear spot; `leader` on or off. `style` (a tag style's id or name) makes it follow that style; without it the tag takes the default style for its kind, if one is set. Returns `looks`: what it prints, its paper box, `overlaps` (other tags, notes, dimensions), `crosses_lines`, `nearby_tags`, `from_element_in`, `clear` |\n| `bim_preview_auto_tag` (read) + `bim_auto_tag_view` (approval) | Tag every untagged element of the given categories (duct, pipe, air_terminal, equipment, fixture, part): each at the first clear spot beside it, never overlapping linework or another annotation; one size tag per straight run of one size; existing tags never move. Preview, say what it will tag, then apply with the `preview_token` |\n| `bim_add_note` (approval) | A rich note (same as sheet notes: bold, italic, lists, sizes, prints as shown) in any plan, section or detail view, or on a sheet, with any number of `leaders`: `to`, optional `elbow`, `arrow` (arrow, dot, tick, none) |\n| `bim_edit_annotation` (approval) | One annotation by id: `move` (dx / dy, or `auto_place: true` for a tag: the first clear spot beside its element), `dimension` (override, prefix, suffix, offset, style, re-point it), `tag` (template, fields, leader, `tag_style`: a tag style id or name, `\"\"` takes it off its style; `elbow: \"auto\"` gives it an elbow leader, a short level shoulder then one bend, and is also \"Straighten leader\" for a bend placed by hand; `elbow: { x, y }` in view feet places the bend), `spread` (`item_id` plus `item_ids`: the tags move clear of every other tag, note and dimension, each with an elbow leader, one undo step; a bend placed by hand is kept; `need_a_hand` lists any with no clear spot), `leaders` (replace a note's leaders), `delete`. Tag moves and retypes return `looks` like `bim_add_tag`. Many at once: a `batch_documents` through `bim_apply_views_sheets_edit` |\n| `bim_list_tag_styles` (read) | The model's tag styles: each one's lines, text height, box, leader, the kinds it is the default for and how many tags use it, plus every field name a style can read (size, size_with_insulation, insulation, cfm, mark, type, system, elevation, length, and every part parameter, custom ones included) |\n| `bim_save_tag_style` (approval) | Create a style (`name`, `lines`), start one from a trade's ready-made style (`preset`, ids from `bim_list_tag_styles` `presets`), or change one (`style` + what changes): `lines` one template per printed line, words plus `{field}` (the field paths schedules and diagrams use: `[\"{size}[ {insulation}]\"]`, `[\"{mark}\", \"{cfm} CFM\"]`, `[\"{pipe.size}\", \"{pipe.length_lf}\"]`, `[\"{circuit}\"]`; `[ … ]` drops when its field is empty); `text_height_in`; `box` (true, false, or `rounded`, `circle`, `hexagon`, `diamond`); `orientation` (`along`: words turn with the run, never upside down); `prefix` / `suffix` (`\"\"` clears); `units` for lengths and heights (`ft_in`, `ft`, `in`, `mm`, `m`); `leader`; `applies_to` (kinds it may label; a tag on anything else is refused); `default_for` (duct, pipe, air_terminal, equipment, fixture, part; a kind is the default of one style at most, so taking it moves it off another style). Changing a style changes every tag that uses it. `delete: true` removes a style no tag uses |\n| `bim_apply_tag_style` (approval) | Give many tags in one plan or section a style in one edit: every tag there, or only those on `kinds`, or only `item_ids` |\n| `bim_set_tag_text` (approval) | Change a model-linked tag's text |\n\n**Tag styles.** A tag style is a named, reusable tag look. A tag that uses one\nprints the style's lines, at its text height, with its box and leader; a line\nwhose fields are all empty is left out (a bare duct prints no insulation line).\n`bim_auto_tag_view` takes `style` too, and otherwise uses each kind's default\nstyle. People manage them in Annotate › Tag styles, pick one on a selected tag\n(Style), and give it to every tag of that kind in the view with \"Use on N more\n… tags here\"; name those when you hand work back. Say \"tag style\", never\n\"tag family\".\n\nDimensions and tags are **associative**: when the model moves, they follow at\nthe next drawing. A dimension point that lost its element prints at its last\nspot and `bim_list_view_annotations` lists it under `orphaned_points`: re-point\nit or delete it. Notes' leaders point at fixed spots; move them with the note\nwhen the thing they point at moves.\n\n## Detailing (linework, regions, components, insulation, modify)\n\nDetailing draws in a detail view, over a plan or section, or on a sheet, the\nsame as people's Draft tab. Units: in a view, points are **view feet** and\nsizes **inches** (real size: a 6\" valve is `width_in: 6`); on a sheet, paper\ninches for both. A circle's `radius` and an arc's points are positions, so they\nare view feet too: in a 1½\" = 1'-0\" view, `radius: 6` prints 18 in across.\nText in a drafting view is `bim_add_note` with the view as owner, not a sheet\nnote. Line style: a named `line_style` (thin, medium, wide, heavy, hidden,\ndashed, center, phantom, dotted lines), the list people pick from;\n`weight` / `pattern` override it.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_list_detail_components` (read) | The 2D component library (ducts and pipes in section, flex duct, threaded rod, trapeze and strap hangers, strut, gate / ball / check valves, concrete anchors, beam clamps, steel angle, and the **electrical one-line symbols** `elec_*`: utility source, meter, CT cabinet, breaker, draw-out breaker, fuse, fused and non-fused disconnects, contactor, transformer, generator, motor, ATS, VFD, UPS, switchboard, panelboard, MCC, bus, busway, ground, surge protective device, equipment box), hatch patterns, line weights and patterns, drafting categories |\n| `bim_draw_detail` (approval) | Draw up to 500 items as **one** edit. Detail lines follow BIM drafting-view conventions: every line and arc is its own element, joined where its ends meet. `rect` (four joined lines), `polygon` (`sides`, `inscribed` true or false), `chain` (lines through points, `closed` to shut it; `polyline` means the same) each make one line per side, with ids `<id>-0`, `-1`, …. Also `line`, `arc` (centre, start, end), `arc_radius` (start, end, radius, `toward` the bulge side), `arc_tangent` (continues the line from `from` through `start`), `circle`, `spline`, `region` (`hatch`: concrete, earth, masonry, insulation_rigid, crosshatch, diagonal…, or `masking: true` to hide what is under it), `component` (`symbol` at a point, `width_in`, `height_in`, `rotation_deg`, `mirror`), `repeat` (a symbol from one point to another: rods, flex), `batt` (insulation `thickness_in` between two points). Each takes `line_style` (thin, medium, wide, heavy, hidden, dashed, center, phantom, dotted: the usual way to pick linework), `weight` / `pattern` (override the named style), `category`, `group` |\n| `bim_modify_detail` (approval) | One operation on drawn items, by id. `trim_extend_corner` (two items; `keep1` / `keep2` a point on the part of each to keep), `trim_extend_single` (one item to `boundary_id`, `keep`), `trim_extend_multiple` (many items to one boundary, one `keeps` point each), `split` (`at`; `delete_to` removes the piece between two points), `split_gap` (`at`, `gap`), `fillet_arc` (two lines, `radius`; `near1` / `near2` pick the parts to keep), `chamfer`, `offset` (`distance`, `toward`; pick a whole chain and its corners still meet; `copy: false` moves the originals), `move` (`disjoin` to leave joined lines behind), `copy`, `rotate`, `scale`, `mirror`, `array` / `polar_array` (`group`: each copy its own detail group), `align`, `move_end` (drag one end), `pin` / `unpin`, `delete`, `match_type` (`source_id`'s line style and category). `trim`, `extend` and `fillet` are older names that still work; prefer the ones above. Items in a detail group come along for move, copy, rotate, scale, mirror, array, align and pin. Dimensions that follow moved items update by themselves |\n| `bim_copy_detail_items` (approval) | Copy drawn items (or everything) from one view or sheet into another, shifted. Dimensions among them follow the copies; groups stay together |\n| `bim_set_detail_group` (approval) | Put items in a named detail group (they then select, move and copy together), or take them out (`group: null`) |\n\n### Joined detail lines\n\n- A rectangle is four lines, not one shape. To round one of its corners, pick\n  the two lines that meet there and `fillet_arc` them. To round every corner,\n  run `fillet_arc` once per corner (one approval each, or one\n  `batch_documents`).\n- Joined ends stay joined. Moving, rotating, trimming or dragging the end of a\n  line stretches the line joined to it that you did not pick, so the outline\n  stays closed. Say so in the plain-words preview (\"the two sides joined to it\n  stretch to follow\"). Use `move` with `disjoin: true` only when the person\n  wants the pieces pulled apart.\n- To work on a whole outline, read the view with `bim_list_view_annotations`\n  and pass every line of the chain: `offset` of all four sides of a rectangle\n  gives four new joined sides; offset of one side gives one parallel line.\n- A rectangle drawn before joined lines existed reads and prints as before.\n  Its first edit turns it into four joined lines, ids `<rect id>~s0` to `~s3`\n  (bottom, right, top, left from the first corner), in the same approval and\n  the same undo. Naming that rectangle with two `keep` / `near` points (for\n  `fillet_arc`, `chamfer`, `trim_extend_corner`) picks the two sides at that\n  corner. Dimensions that followed its corners follow the new line ends. If a\n  dimension follows its middle, the tool refuses and says so; redraw that\n  dimension first.\n- Pinned items refuse Move, Rotate, trims, splits, fillets and Delete until\n  `unpin`. Pin linework another discipline depends on before big edits.\n- Pick linework by named `line_style` (`hidden` for work above or behind,\n  `center` for centre lines, `wide` for cut outlines), the same list people\n  pick from in the options bar (`bim_list_detail_components` returns\n  `line_styles`). Use `match_type` to give lines the style of one already\n  right, instead of restyling each.\n- People do the same things by hand: Annotate › Detail line ▾ (Rectangle,\n  Polygon, Lines (chain) draw joined lines), Annotate › Fillet arc, Modify ›\n  Trim/Extend ▾, Split ▾, Offset (Copy, Numerical / Graphical), Pin ▾, and a\n  region's Edit boundary (Finish / Cancel). When you hand work back, name\n  those so the person can carry on without an agent.\n\nPer view, `bim_set_view_properties` (`mutinybim-views-sheets`)\n`hidden_drafting_categories` hides detail_lines, hatching, masking, components,\ninsulation, text, dimensions, tags or clouds there only (a coordination copy of\na detail with hatching off). Draw order is the order in the batch: put masking\nregions after what they cover and before the note that sits on them.\n\nNever build an electrical one-line or riser by hand from `bim_draw_detail`\ncircles and rectangles, and never draw on a live diagram view: it is drawn\nfrom the model by `mutinybim-electrical` (`bim_layout_one_line` with\n`from_model: true`); change its layout with hints there.\n\n### Charts and pictures on a sheet\n\nA `figure` item in `bim_draw_detail` (sheets only; `corner` / `opposite` are\nthe frame's corners in paper inches) holds either a live chart, `chart:\n\"plumbing.hunter_curve\"` (redrawn from the model at every show and print; it\nmarks each water system's design point), or a picture, `file_id` of a PNG or\nJPEG already stored with the project plus its `file_name`. Never both. Move\nor resize it with `bim_modify_detail` (move, scale) like any drawn item;\npeople use Sheets › Chart / Picture and drag the corners. A picture that can't\nbe read stops the sheet from printing with the picture's name: say so rather\nthan printing without it.\n\n## Workflows\n\n**Make a detail.** `bim_create_view` (`mutinybim-views-sheets`) with\n`view_type: \"detail\"` and a detail scale (1 1/2\" = 1'-0\" is 8; 3\" is 4) →\n`bim_list_detail_components` → draw the whole detail as one `bim_draw_detail`\n(the slab as a concrete `region`; outlines as `rect`, `polygon` or `chain` so\ntheir corners are joined; rods as `component` threaded_rod or a `repeat`; the\nhanger, the duct section, `batt` insulation, a `masking` region under each\nnote; hidden or centre lines where they belong) → round corners with\n`bim_modify_detail` `fillet_arc`, meet lines with `trim_extend_corner`, cut\nopenings with `split` and `delete_to` → dimensions that follow the linework\n(`bim_add_dimension` with `element_id` = a line's id and `anchor` start / end)\n→ notes with leaders (`bim_add_note`) → `bim_set_view_properties` with\n`fit_crop: true` (a detail's crop is set when it is empty; fit it so nothing\nyou drew is clipped) → `bim_render_view_image` and look (clipped notes,\noverlaps, open corners) → `bim_place_viewport` on the details sheet →\n`bim_print_set_pdf` and check it. Describe the detail in plain words before\nthe batch (\"a 2'-0\" wide trapeze hanger: two 3/8\" rods, a strap, the duct;\nwidth and maximum drop dimensioned; rod and strap noted\").\n\n**Edit a detail.** `bim_list_view_annotations` on the view (ids, what prints,\nwhat follows what) → one `bim_modify_detail` per operation (groups come along\nfor move, copy, rotate, scale, mirror, array, align, pin; the trims, splits,\nfillet arc, chamfer, offset, `move_end`, `match_type` and `delete` act on\nexactly the items named; joined ends follow) → start a similar detail from\nthis one with `bim_copy_detail_items` → change words with `bim_edit_text_note`\n(`mutinybim-views-sheets`, with its `expected_content`) → re-point or restyle\ndimensions with `bim_edit_annotation` `dimension` → read again and say what\nchanged, including every line that stretched and every dimension value that\nmoved.\n\n**Make tags say what the firm wants** (\"tags should show the insulation\",\n\"one line: size and CFM\"). `bim_list_tag_styles` (reuse a style if one fits) →\n`bim_save_tag_style` with the lines in plain fields (and `default_for` so new\ntags take it) → `bim_apply_tag_style` on each plan with the kinds it covers →\n`bim_list_view_annotations` and `bim_render_view_image`: every tag prints the\nnew words; fix overlaps the longer text made (`bim_edit_annotation` `move`\n`auto_place`). To change all of them later, change the style once. A trade's\nusual tag (duct size along the run, pipe size, equipment mark in a hexagon,\ncircuit) is a `preset`: start from it rather than typing the lines.\n\n**Place a tag where it reads.** `bim_add_tag` (no `at_ft`: it picks the first\nclear spot) → read `looks`: if `clear` is false, say what it overlaps or\ncrosses and `bim_edit_annotation` `move` `auto_place: true`, or give `dx` /\n`dy` away from the `nearby_tags` → if `from_element_in` is large, add a leader\n(`tag`, `leader: true`). Never leave a tag sitting on another tag.\n\n**Annotate a plan.** `bim_preview_auto_tag` with the categories the sheet needs\n(duct sizes, air terminals with CFM, equipment marks) → say how many and any it\ncouldn't place → `bim_auto_tag_view` → `bim_auto_dimension` for the runs or\ngrid lines that need locating → spot elevations on duct bottoms or tops where\nheights matter (`bim_add_dimension` kind `spot_elevation`, first point the run's\n`mid`) → general and specific notes with leaders → `bim_list_view_annotations`:\nfix anything in `overlaps` (move one of the two) → `bim_render_view_image` and\nlook → print and check.\n\n**Drafting conventions** (general practice; the firm's own standards win when\ngiven):\n\n- **Text** 3/32\" (about 2.4 mm) for notes, tags and dimensions; titles larger.\n  Never smaller to make something fit; move it or split it.\n- **Line weights**: cut and outline heavier than detail linework, which is\n  heavier than dimension and leader lines (the thinnest).\n- **Dimensions**: dimension to things that are built (faces, centrelines,\n  grids), not to other dimensions; a continuous string rather than a pile of\n  separate ones; one overall dimension outside a string; don't dimension the\n  same thing twice on one sheet. Ticks for architectural-style drawings,\n  arrows for mechanical details, as the set already does.\n- **Tags**: one per element per view; size tags on every straight run of a\n  new size; leaders only when the tag can't sit next to what it labels.\n- **Notes and keynotes**: short, imperative, uppercase; a general note applies\n  to the whole sheet or set, a specific note points at one thing. Don't paste\n  text from code books or published standards.\n- **Callouts**: a box round the enlarged area, bound to the enlarged view\n  (`add_drawing_item` `shape: \"callout\"`, `callout.viewId`). Never type a\n  sheet number into a note for it: the callout's head reads the view's number\n  and sheet live and follows it to another sheet.\n\nIf a tool here is missing after turning this family on, say exactly which\ntool is missing; don't substitute another.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_list_view_annotations` |\n| `bim_add_dimension` |\n| `bim_auto_dimension` |\n| `bim_add_tag` |\n| `bim_preview_auto_tag` |\n| `bim_auto_tag_view` |\n| `bim_add_note` |\n| `bim_edit_annotation` |\n| `bim_set_tag_text` |\n| `bim_list_tag_styles` |\n| `bim_save_tag_style` |\n| `bim_apply_tag_style` |\n| `bim_list_detail_components` |\n| `bim_draw_detail` |\n| `bim_modify_detail` |\n| `bim_copy_detail_items` |\n| `bim_set_detail_group` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-ducts-pipes": "---\nname: mutinybim-ducts-pipes\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for duct and pipe runs: route and connect runs, flex, resize runs and stretches, slide branches, terminal airflow, move equipment, risers, fittings and offsets, elevation changes, splitting and dragging runs, system design, duct insulation, drain slope and inline pipe accessories. Duct size advice and the pressure and pipe reports are in the calculations pack. Use with the mutinybim core. Trigger on: route duct, route pipe, connect, resize, fitting, offset, elevation, slope, insulation, system design.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the ducts-pipes family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs ducts-pipes, or /packs/ducts-pipes/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"ducts_pipes\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"ducts-pipes\"\n  token-cap: \"10800\"\n  tools: \"bim_route_run bim_connect_runs bim_disconnect_run bim_route_flex bim_resize_run bim_slide_branch bim_set_terminal_airflow bim_move_equipment bim_resize_riser bim_list_fitting_types bim_swap_fitting bim_offset_run bim_change_elevation bim_split_run bim_resize_stretch bim_drag_run bim_set_system_design bim_set_duct_insulation bim_set_slope bim_place_pipe_accessory\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: duct and pipe\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the four starting reads and the rules for every change (preview, say the change, edit with `base_revision`, report the server's\nanswer). `bim_preview_route_edit`, which previews every edit here, is the\n`model` family. Many runs at once (a branch to every diffuser, every fixture\nto a drain) are one script with `routes.*`; the tools here are for one\nchange.\n\nSizes come from engineering: duct size advice (`bim_suggest_duct_size`), the\npressure and pipe reports and every calc with citations are `mutinybim-calcs`.\n\n## Duct and pipe (each needs approval)\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_route_run` | Route duct or pipe through plan points at a height above the level, from / to a part connector, an open run end, or `branch_from` a trunk (tap / tee / wye by the system's rules). Leave `elevation_ft` out to take the height of what the route starts (else finishes) on: a `branch_from` tap lands on the trunk's centreline, a continued open end or side-facing connector carries on at its height. A typed `elevation_ft` that isn't the trunk's is refused with the trunk's height; don't guess one. Starting on a run continues its system (no `system_id` needed). Fittings are created with it, all or nothing. Duct legs run level at **any plan angle** (or vertical, or a 45° slope), and each elbow is made to its turn (30°, 22.5°, 60°… up to 135°) with the loss for that angle; a turn sharper than 135° is refused (make it two elbows). Elbow types made only for some turns (mitred with turning vanes: 45° / 90°) refuse other angles with the reason. Pipe legs stay E-W, N-S or 45° and turn 45° or 90°. The person's Route tool snaps duct to 15° and 22.5° steps from the working angle (Alt / Option, or Ribbon › Any angle, draws free); give the angle the person asked for. Rise or drop mid-run: `elevation_ft` on a point on a straight stretch (not at a turn; rectangular duct can't twist), made as `rise_style` says (`vertical` 90° elbows, or `45`). Duct: `elbow_family` / `takeoff_family` (from `bim_list_fitting_types`) pick this route's elbows and branch takeoff without changing the system's default. Duct: `insulation` (else the system's default) and `airflow_cfm` (with `duct_shape`, `height_in`) to size from airflow instead of giving a size; see Duct insulation and sizing. `use_my_defaults: true` fills whatever you leave out (size and shape, insulation, elbow and takeoff types, rise style) from the **person's own saved Route defaults** (Route panel › My defaults: this project's, else every project's); what you give always wins. Use it when the person says \"route it the way I usually do\" |\n| `bim_connect_runs` | Connect existing runs, one edit, by `mode`: `face_to_face` (default with `b_end`) joins ends that already meet; `auto_route` joins two open ends that don't line up with a square route worked out between them (out of each end, square turns, one rise or drop) or refuses with the reason; `tap_into_side` (default without `b_end`) continues `a`'s open end straight into `b`'s side with a tap at `b`'s height (rises or drops on the way); `tee_at_elbow` turns a level or pitched elbow (`a_id`; a drain at up to 1/2\" per ft counts) into a tee: the `a_end` leg runs on through the corner at its own fall and ends open there, then `bim_route_run` from that end routes the branch on. On a drain the other leg must drain into it, so pass the **downstream** leg as `a_end`; the branch fitting is the one the direction rules allow (a combination wye & 1/8 bend horizontal to horizontal), and the other way round is refused. Pressure pipe gets the system's tee. Sizes and systems must match |\n| `bim_disconnect_run` | Cut one connection; both sides stay open, nothing is deleted |\n| `bim_route_flex` | Flex from an open duct end (a tap's short branch) into an air device's neck (`to_connector` default `neck`): one round piece sized to the neck, curving from the end into it, linked both ends so airflow and pressure include it (rougher than sheet metal). The end must already be the neck's size (else route a transition first); the result warns past 6 ft, the usual limit; say it |\n| `bim_resize_run` | Resize a **whole run** (rectangular, round, flat oval, or pipe size + material); every connected same-size piece follows. Fittings not made in the new shape take the system's choice for it (a smooth radius elbow becomes the round elbow on round duct) and the result says which; report it. To resize only some pieces, or part of one piece (for example one side of a split), use `bim_resize_stretch` |\n| `bim_slide_branch` | Move a branch takeoff along its trunk |\n| `bim_set_terminal_airflow` | Set a diffuser or grille's design CFM; system totals recompute |\n| `bim_move_equipment` | Move a unit; connected ducts follow where they can |\n| `bim_resize_riser` | Resize a riser through every level it crosses, as one change |\n\nWhat `bim_route_run` does to rectangular and flat oval duct by itself:\n\n- **Every turn must be one an elbow can make.** A route that would twist the\n  duct (a riser turning the other way from the leg it meets, a rise from a leg\n  running across it) is refused with the reason. Rise from a leg running the\n  same way, or join with square or round duct; do not try random point orders.\n- **A riser that turns flat lays the duct on its side**, so the route adds a\n  short straight and a transition back to the size you asked for. If that leg\n  is too short for the transition, it is refused: make the leg longer.\n- **A branch wider than its trunk allows** is tapped at the widest size the\n  trunk takes, with a transition up to the size drawn. Refused only when even\n  the narrowed tap doesn't fit: make the branch smaller.\n\nReport the added transitions when you describe the change.\n\n## Fittings and pressure\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_list_fitting_types` | Read the fitting library (elbows, transitions, offsets, takeoffs, caps), what each fits, and its parameters. Use the id with the tools below |\n| `bim_swap_fitting` (approval) | Change one fitting's type, e.g. mitred elbow to radius elbow; connections stay. Refused when the new fitting doesn't fit. On the **same** type, only the `params` you give change: an eccentric transition (`duct.transition.eccentric`) moves its flat side with `params: { flat: \"top\" \\| \"bottom\" \\| \"left\" \\| \"right\" }` and keeps its wall angle. **Mirror** = the opposite side. Flat on top keeps the top of duct level under structure; left / right are as seen looking from the end that kept its size into the resized duct |\n| `bim_offset_run` (approval) | Jog a run left, right, up or down at a point with 45°/90° elbows or an ogee |\n| `bim_change_elevation` (approval) | Raise or drop a stretch of a run to a new height with 45° or 90° rises; what is connected follows |\n| `bim_split_run` (approval) | Cut a routed duct or pipe segment in two at `at_ft` (feet from its start) with a plain joint. Both pieces keep size, system and connections; branches stay on the piece they sit on. Refused on a takeoff or when a piece would be too short for its fittings |\n| `bim_resize_stretch` (approval) | Resize or reshape **only the segments you name** in one change (rectangular, round, flat oval, or pipe), or only `from_ft`–`to_ft` on one segment. Elbows between resized pieces follow; a `transition` (system default, concentric, eccentric flat top / bottom / left / right, rect-to-round) goes where a neighbour keeps its size, with the insulation of the duct it joins. Elbows, transitions and taps not made in the new shape take the system's choice for it (resizing a rectangular run with smooth radius elbows to round gives round elbows), and the result says which. Refused when the transition doesn't fit or a branch would be as wide as its trunk. This is what the MutinyBIM editor does when a person resizes a selected piece or a Tab-selected stretch; `bim_resize_run` is the whole run |\n| `bim_drag_run` (approval) | Move one straight routed segment or in-line elbow / transition by `east_ft`, `north_ft`, `up_ft` (negative lowers), exactly as dragging it in the view does. What it meets follows: legs along the move stretch, legs square to it move with it, fixed parts refuse. A 45° or sloped piece slides so its neighbours only stretch. `end: \"start\" \\| \"end\"` moves only that end (raise the top of a rise to make it taller). By default it behaves like the view's drag: a leg pulled back past its elbow turns the other way (its 90° elbows flip), and a move that can't go all the way (a leg shorter than its elbows need, an elbow that can't flip) stops at the limit; say how far it actually moved. `strict: true` never flips and stops at the elbow limit (the view's Alt drag). `reroute` is gone; `stop_at_limit` is still accepted and changes nothing. Refused only when it can't move that way at all. To move a whole run rigidly, use `bim_move_elements` on its pieces (the view's Tab, then drag). Not for takeoffs (`bim_slide_branch`) or whole units (`bim_move_equipment`) |\n| `bim_set_system_design` (approval) | `name` renames a system (unique in the model, e.g. `DCW-1`; a new one is named by `bim_route_run` `system_name`). Choose the fittings routing places on a system (per slot) and its design altitude, air temperature and duct material; for duct also `default_insulation` (what new runs get) and `sizing` (friction rate, velocity limit, aspect ratio a size suggestion aims for; `null` = typical values). Pipe: `pipe_design` (below) |\n\nMounting planes (`bim_set_mounting_plane`) are in `mutinybim-parts`; an engineer's own loss coefficient on a fitting (`bim_set_fitting_coefficient_override`) is in `mutinybim-calcs`.\n\nRouting refuses a leg too short for its fittings and says how much room they need: move the point or pick a shorter fitting with `bim_swap_fitting` / `bim_set_system_design`. Never present formula coefficients as final for permit work.\n\n## Duct insulation and sizing from airflow\n\nA run's size is always the **sheet-metal** size. Insulation goes on one side of it:\na **wrap** is outside (the outside size grows by the thickness on every side; that\nis what has to clear structure and other trades), a **liner** is inside (the\nairway shrinks by the thickness on every side, so velocity and friction rise;\nthe pressure report already uses the inside size). A liner's `surface` is\n`faced` (the default: smooth facing, 1.5 mm roughness) or `spray_coated`\n(4.5 mm, noticeably more friction); the air sees the liner, not the duct\nmaterial. A system's default insulation carries its liner surface too. Fittings carry\nthe insulation of the duct they join. Plans draw it as thin dashed lines beside\n(wrap) or inside (liner) the duct, sections as a thin dashed outline at the cut,\nthe 3D view as a see-through skin over wrapped duct. A schedule of category\n`duct` has `insulation`, `liner_surface`, `inside_size`, `outside_size`, `cfm`,\n`velocity_fpm` and `friction_in_per_100ft`; tag template `duct_size_insulation` reads `24\"x12\" (1\" liner)`.\nPipe insulation is a wrap outside the pipe: drawn the same way, a `pipe` schedule\ncategory has `system`, `size`, `material`, `insulation`, `insulation_thickness`,\n`outside_size`, `length`, and tag template `pipe_size_insulation` reads `2\" (1\" ins.)`.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_set_duct_insulation` (approval) | Put a wrap or liner on routed duct, or a wrap on pipe, or take it off (`insulation: { kind: \"none\" \\| \"wrap\" \\| \"liner\", thickness_in, surface?: \"faced\" \\| \"spray_coated\" (liner only, default faced), material?, r_value? }`), for `scope` `piece` (the segment and its end fittings), `run` (default: same-size pieces through elbows, stopping at transitions, branches and parts) or `system`. A liner leaving less than 2\" clear inside, or any liner on pipe, is refused with the reason. Pipe: `from_schedule: true` (no `insulation`) gives every pipe of the element's system the project's pipe insulation schedule thickness for its size, one edit |\n\n- **Defaults.** Each duct system has a default insulation (`bim_set_system_design`\n  `default_insulation`). `bim_route_run` without `insulation` uses it; with\n  `insulation` it uses that (`kind: \"none\"` for bare duct). A **new** system takes\n  its first route's choice as its default, or else starts like an existing system\n  of the same kind. When the person says \"draw supply with 1\" liner by default\",\n  set the system default; don't add `insulation` to every route.\n- **Sizing.** Ask `bim_suggest_duct_size` and show the person the size, velocity\n  and friction before resizing. Typical criteria (0.08\" per 100 ft; 2,000 fpm\n  supply, 1,500 fpm return / exhaust / outside air; 4:1) are general values: say\n  \"typical, verify for the project\" unless the system has its own. Apply with\n  `bim_resize_run` / `bim_resize_stretch`, or route with `airflow_cfm` (and\n  `height_in` for the depth) instead of a size. Never resize without the\n  person's approval of the size. A liner eats depth: 1,200 CFM with a 1\" faced\n  liner is 19\" round or 22x14, but 36x10 when held to 10\" deep (8\" inside);\n  say so when the person's depth forces a very wide duct.\n\n## Pipe: fittings, slope and hydraulics\n\nLoads on open pipe ends without fixtures (load points, connection types,\nfixture units) are in `mutinybim-plumbing`; `bim_pipe_report` reads them.\n\nPipe sizes take `nominal_in`, `material` and optionally `spec` (`type_k` /\n`type_l` / `type_m` copper, `sch40`, `no_hub` or `service_weight` cast iron…).\nFittings come from the library by service (domestic water, drainage and vent,\nfuel gas) with the pipe's usual joint (sweat, press, threaded, no-hub, solvent\nweld, PEX crimp / expansion, fusion, CSST).\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_set_slope` (approval) | Pitch one drain segment to inches per foot (default: the system's minimum, 1/4\" per ft up to 3\", 1/8\" larger); what is connected follows |\n| `bim_place_pipe_accessory` (approval) | Put a valve, union, cleanout, test tee, P-trap, gas cock or drip leg inline on a pipe |\n\n`bim_list_fitting_types` with `domain: \"pipe\"` and `service` (`water`,\n`drainage`, `vent`, `gas`) lists families, joints, materials, take-out, and for\ndrainage the direction changes each may make. `bim_set_system_design`\n`pipe_design` sets water supply pressure, friction method, demand table,\nvelocity limit; drain slopes, capacity table, direction-rule overrides; gas\nequation, specific gravity, allowable drop.\n\nDrainage rules the model server enforces (from general practice; the project\ncan override any of them in `pipe_design.directionRules`):\n\n- `bim_route_run` slopes level drain legs by itself (`slope_in_per_ft` to\n  choose steeper) and picks the fitting for each change of direction:\n  a sanitary tee only where a horizontal branch enters a stack; a combination\n  wye & 1/8 bend where a branch joins a horizontal drain; a long sweep where a\n  stack turns horizontal. A branch into a stack finds its own height from the slope.\n- A disallowed fitting, a drain flatter than the minimum, a branch sloped away\n  from the run it joins, or a drain that gets smaller downstream is **refused\n  with the reason and the fitting to use**. Explain it; do not try random types.\n- No plumbing or gas code sizing table is bundled. If the user wants a code\n  check, ask for the firm's values and enter them as `capacityTable`,\n  `demandTable` or `fixtureTable`; never type in values from a code book.\n\n## Workflows\n\n**Route a branch duct to a diffuser:** `bim_query_elements` on the level (the\ntrunk run id, the diffuser's connector id) → `bim_preview_route_edit` with\n`tool: \"bim_route_run\"`, `branch_from` the trunk, `to` the diffuser connector,\nno `elevation_ft` (the tap takes the trunk's height and system) → fix any\n`would_refuse` → describe → `bim_route_run`.\n\n**Rooftop unit to ceiling diffusers on the floor below** (a downflow RTU):\n1. `bim_list_levels` (the roof and the floor below; their elevations) and\n   `bim_list_part_types` `search: \"rooftop\"`: place the current RTU\n   (`builtin:rtu-5-ton@2`, with its outdoor-air hood) on the **roof** level at\n   its curb height.\n2. Diffusers on the floor below: on the ceiling (`height_above_level_ft`),\n   or where there is no ceiling, `bim_set_mounting_plane` (`mutinybim-parts`) and\n   `bim_place_part` with `on_plane_id`. A ceiling diffuser is drawn face down,\n   neck up: leave `mount` out. A floor register is `mount: \"turned_over\"`, a\n   sidewall register `mount: \"on_side\"` with `rotation_deg` facing the room.\n3. The trunk: `bim_route_run` with `level_id` = **the roof**, `from` the\n   RTU's down-facing supply, and `elevation_ft` **from the roof's floor**, so\n   a plenum 2.5 ft below the roof deck is `-2.5`. The run drops through the\n   roof; the piece above the roof is drawn on the roof plan and the rest on\n   the floor below. `to` may also be a connector on the floor below directly.\n4. Branches off that trunk: `bim_route_run` with the **trunk's level** (the\n   roof) and `branch_from`; heights are still from the roof's floor. Stop the\n   branch a few feet short of the diffuser and `bim_route_flex` into its neck.\n5. `bim_pressure_report` and `bim_query_elements` (terminals): report CFM per\n   diffuser and the static the RTU needs.\n\n**Hood, louver or screen on an outdoor-air or exhaust opening:**\n`bim_set_parameters` `scope: \"instance\"`, `intakes: { outdoor_air: \"louver\" }`\n(`hood`, `louver`, `screen`; `none` takes the type's off, `null` puts it back).\nIt is drawn in 3D, on prints and in plan. The built-in RTU and makeup air unit\nship with a weather hood.\n\n**Move or raise a piece of duct:** `bim_inspect_network` on the piece (its id\nand neighbours) → `bim_preview_route_edit` with `tool: \"bim_drag_run\"` and the\nmove (`east_ft`, `north_ft` or `up_ft` alone, or `up_ft` with one of the others,\none step; east and north together on a level piece is at an angle and refused,\nso move one way at a time; `up_ft` alone just raises or lowers it) → read where the preview leaves it (flipped elbows, or stopped at the\nlimit) and say that, or pass `strict: true` if the elbows must keep their\ndirection → `bim_drag_run`. Whole unit: `bim_move_equipment`; a takeoff: `bim_slide_branch`.\n\n**Connect to existing duct:** a branch off a trunk is `bim_route_run` with\n`branch_from` (no `elevation_ft`); an open end that should run into a trunk's\nside is `bim_connect_runs` `tap_into_side`; two open ends that don't line up\nare `bim_connect_runs` `auto_route`; a branch from an elbow's corner is\n`bim_connect_runs` `tee_at_elbow`, then `bim_route_run` from the open end it\nleaves. Preview each with `bim_preview_route_edit` first.\n\n**Resize part of a run:** `bim_split_run` where the size changes (if it changes\nmid-segment) → `bim_resize_stretch` on the pieces that change. Use\n`bim_resize_run` only when the person means the whole run.\n\n**Make a reducer flat on one side, or mirror it:** `bim_inspect_network` on the run (the transition's id) → `bim_preview_route_edit` with `tool: \"bim_swap_fitting\"`, `family: \"duct.transition.eccentric\"`, `params: { flat: \"top\" }` (Mirror: the opposite side) → `bim_swap_fitting`. Or choose it while resizing: `bim_resize_stretch` `transition: \"eccentric_flat_top\"`.\n\n**Rebalance airflow:** `bim_query_elements` (terminals, CFM) → one\n`bim_set_terminal_airflow` per terminal, each on the latest saved revision →\nread them again and report the new totals.\n\n**Add a wye at a drain bend (the person's \"+\"):** `bim_inspect_network` on the\nbend (its id, which port links which leg, which leg falls away from it) →\n`bim_preview_route_edit` with `tool: \"bim_connect_runs\"`, `mode: \"tee_at_elbow\"`,\n`a_id` the bend, `a_end` the port of the leg that drains **away** → describe\n(the trunk runs on upstream at the same fall; the other leg joins through a\ncombination wye & 1/8 bend) → `bim_connect_runs` → `bim_validation_issues`.\n\n**Create similar (the person's C S):** read the selected element\n(`bim_inspect_network` for a run, `bim_query_elements` for a part), then make\nanother with the same values; nothing is guessed. A pipe or duct:\n`bim_route_run` with its `kind`, size (`nominal_in` + `material` + `spec`, or\nthe duct size), `system_id`, and `elevation_ft` (its height), from where the\nperson says, or `from` / `to` its open end (`run_id` + `end`) to continue it.\nA part: `bim_place_part` with its `type_id`, `level_id` and\n`height_above_level_ft`, at the new point. Say what was copied.\n\n**Lay out a drain and check it:** `bim_query_elements` (the stack or main id,\nfixture connector ids) → `bim_preview_route_edit` with `tool: \"bim_route_run\"`,\n`kind: \"pipe\"`, the fixture `from` and `branch_from` the stack or main → fix any\n`would_refuse` → `bim_route_run` → `bim_pipe_report` and report fixture units,\ncapacity and anything flagged.\n\nIf a tool here is missing after turning this family on, say exactly which\ntool is missing; don't substitute another.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_route_run` |\n| `bim_connect_runs` |\n| `bim_disconnect_run` |\n| `bim_route_flex` |\n| `bim_resize_run` |\n| `bim_slide_branch` |\n| `bim_set_terminal_airflow` |\n| `bim_move_equipment` |\n| `bim_resize_riser` |\n| `bim_list_fitting_types` |\n| `bim_swap_fitting` |\n| `bim_offset_run` |\n| `bim_change_elevation` |\n| `bim_split_run` |\n| `bim_resize_stretch` |\n| `bim_drag_run` |\n| `bim_set_system_design` |\n| `bim_set_duct_insulation` |\n| `bim_set_slope` |\n| `bim_place_pipe_accessory` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-electrical": "---\nname: mutinybim-electrical\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for electrical design: panels and their schedules, circuits and feeders, rebalancing, connection and feeder types, room circuit templates, receptacle proposals by code spacing, service layout, and live one-line, electrical riser and plumbing riser diagrams. Use with the mutinybim core. Trigger on: panel, circuit, feeder, breaker, load, receptacle, service size, one-line diagram, riser diagram, plumbing riser.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the electrical family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs electrical, or /packs/electrical/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"electrical\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"electrical\"\n  token-cap: \"9300\"\n  tools: \"bim_electrical_report bim_read_panel bim_electrical_catalog bim_propose_links_from_text bim_preview_circuit_edit bim_create_circuit bim_edit_circuit bim_add_to_circuit bim_remove_from_circuit bim_feed_panel bim_rebalance_panel bim_save_electrical_type bim_apply_circuit_template bim_preview_one_line bim_layout_one_line bim_propose_receptacles bim_accept_receptacles bim_place_service_layout\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: electrical distribution and one-line diagrams\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the reads and the rules for every change: preview, say the\nchange, edit with `base_revision`, report the server's answer, look at what\nyou drew. The pictures you check a diagram with (`bim_render_view_image`,\n`bim_render_sheet_image`), the PDFs and part placement are the `model`\nfamily. Many circuits at once (a whole floor's receptacles on named circuits)\nare one script; the tools here are for one change or a proposal to review.\n\nSize what the diagram shows with `mutinybim-calcs` (`bim_run_calc`). Fixing a\ncrowded diagram by hand uses `mutinybim-drafting` (`bim_modify_detail`) and\n`mutinybim-views-sheets` (`bim_edit_text_note`, `bim_create_sheet`).\n\n## Circuits, feeders and panel totals\n\nA circuit is not text. \"R-1 is on L1-3\" is a link in the model from circuit 3\nof panel L1 to R-1's power connector, and panel L1 is fed by a link from a\nbreaker in transformer T1 (or straight from a board's breaker). Loads are read\noff the devices, so every panel's totals, its \"Supply from\" and its capacity\nare live. Nothing has to be drawn for a circuit to exist.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_electrical_report` (read) | Every panel, switchboard and transformer (or one: `panel` by id or name) with `supply_from`, `connected_va`, `demand_va` (NEC Article 220 calculated load, largest motor +25% included), `sizing_va` / `sizing_a` (continuous loads at 125%: what conductors and the breaker are sized for), `per_phase` VA and amps, `demand_by_class` with the clause for each line, `capacity` (`limit_a`, `limited_by`, `headroom_a`, `reason`), and `findings`. One panel also lists its `circuits` with members. The code edition is `nec_edition`, else the project's Code Study, else `state`; with none, `rules_placeholder: true` and the totals are connected loads only |\n| `bim_read_panel` (read) | One panel as the MutinyBIM editor's panel view draws it: each used slot's circuit (`circuit_id`, name, breaker, poles, members; `existing` on a renovation's existing circuits, which keep their panel and slots), the free slots, what feeds it, and `fault_current` (available vs the panel's rating, NEC 110.9, with the point-to-point `trail`). No `panel`: the devices on no circuit yet. Totals are in `bim_electrical_report` (which also gives `fault_current` per panel) |\n| `bim_electrical_catalog` (read) | The project's connection types, feeder types (with their schedule line) and room templates. `size_feeder_for: \"H1\"` (+ `length_ft`, `material`, `conduit`) proposes a feeder type from that panel's NEC load (breaker, conductors with voltage drop, parallel sets, EGC, conduit, the steps) with the exact `bim_save_electrical_type` call. `check: true` adds the Electrical tab's check list (each item with the ids to select) |\n| `bim_preview_circuit_edit` (read) | Dry run of any edit below with its arguments: what it changes (a rebalance's moves and phases before and after; a template's circuits per unit), or the plain reason it would be refused. Free |\n| `bim_propose_links_from_text` (read) | Turn circuit numbers people typed on devices (\"L1-3\", \"HP1-43,45\") and \"Fed from\" typed on panels into proposals: what would be made, `ready` or `refused` (with the reason), `unmatched`, `ambiguous`, and `to_make_it` (the exact `bim_create_circuit` / `bim_add_to_circuit` call). Changes nothing |\n| `bim_create_circuit` (approval) | Serve the listed `members` from `source_id`: devices on a panel's circuit, or a panel / transformer from a breaker (a feeder). `positions` are circuit numbers, one per pole (1,3,5 for three poles); leave them out for the free ones that best balance the panel's phases. Optional `breaker_a` (default: one unit's MOCP, else 20 A; a feeder: the fed panel's main, else its bus), `description`, `load_class`, `conductors` (a voltage-drop upsize is a real value here, never \"SEE SLD\"), `length_ft`, `feeder_type`, `circuit_id` (from a proposal). **`one_circuit_each: true`**: each member on a circuit of its own in the slots that best balance the phases, breaker the next standard size for its load (the project's NEC edition; none: the panel default, and it says so), planned items included; `members: []` = every Not placed yet item waiting for a circuit (the MutinyBIM editor's Circuit all). Returns `circuits` (item, circuit, poles, VA, breaker) and `not_circuited` (item and reason, e.g. wrong voltage for this panel); one approval, one undo step. With no panel yet it says to plan the MDP first (`bim_place_service_layout` `plan_only: true`) |\n| `bim_edit_circuit` (approval) | Move a circuit or feeder to other positions or another panel, or change its breaker, conductors, load class, length, feeder type or description (`null` clears) |\n| `bim_add_to_circuit` (approval) | Put more devices on an existing circuit |\n| `bim_remove_from_circuit` (approval) | Take devices off a circuit; it stays as a spare (delete it with `bim_delete_elements`) |\n| `bim_feed_panel` (approval) | Feed `panel_id` (a panel or transformer) from `source_id`: a board or panel's breaker (`positions`, `breaker_a`, `feeder_type`) or a transformer's secondary. A feeder it already has moves instead of a second one. A transformer has no main to default its breaker from: ask for it. A generator feeds a transfer switch's emergency side: `bim_create_circuit` with `members: [{ element_id: ATS, connector: \"emergency\" }]` |\n| `bim_rebalance_panel` (approval) | Move or swap circuits on one `panel` so A, B, C carry more even load. Same model, same moves; three-pole breakers and `keep_circuits` stay; what is on a circuit never changes |\n| `bim_save_electrical_type` (approval) | Save one `entry` (`connection_type`, `feeder_type` or `circuit_template`); `replaces` renames it and what names it follows; `delete: { type, name }` removes one nothing uses. Devices take a connection type by `bim_set_parameters` `values.connection_type` (their load, voltage, poles and class then come from it). Plumbing, gas and air connection types (and one record with both, a dishwasher's hot water and 120 V) are `bim_save_connection_type` in `mutinybim-plumbing` |\n| `bim_apply_circuit_template` (approval) | Circuit the devices in each unit's rooms by a room template: `units: [{ panel, rooms }]`, both as the person names them. Devices already on a circuit are left alone; refused circuits are reported, the rest made |\n| `bim_place_service_layout` (approval) | Start the one-line from a service size: meter, optional fused main disconnect, main distribution and the panels and transformers the person lists, each `fed_from` another, as one edit. `plan_only: true` draws nothing: every piece is planned and waits in Not placed yet (workflow: starter service layout) |\n\nThese are the same edits as the MutinyBIM editor's **Electrical** tab and the\n**Circuit** tab (Add to circuit, Edit circuit, Feed panel, Drag to panel,\nPanel view, Check, Balance phases, Electrical types, Room templates), so a\nperson sees the agent's circuits in the panel view at once. Place parts with\n`bim_place_part` (model family). `bim_list_part_types` with an electrical\n`category` or `search` lists the starter types (panelboards with and without\na main, switchboards and switchboard sections, transformers, generators,\ntransfer switches, meters, CT cabinets, tap boxes, fused and non-fused\ndisconnects, receptacles, lights, switches, junction boxes and hardwired\nequipment connections) with their voltages. Disconnects, transfer switches,\nmeters, CT cabinets, tap boxes and generators take no slots: what they feed\nhas no `positions`.\nMechanical equipment with a nameplate voltage and MCA / MOCP (built-in, cut\nsheet or drafted) is circuited directly: 3 poles for three-phase, breaker from\nMOCP, load from MCA.\nName panels with `values: { panel_name: \"L1\" }` so circuits read `L1-3`; give\ndevices a `mark`.\n\nRules (these are firm):\n\n- **Members are exactly what the person named**, or what they confirmed from a\n  proposal. Never choose which devices go on a circuit by where they are, never\n  split or regroup devices into circuits yourself, and never pick a panel for\n  them by distance. Ask \"which panel, and which devices?\" when it isn't said.\n  This is a fixed product rule, not a default.\n- A refusal is the answer: \"MUA-1 needs 480 V 3-phase; L1 is 120/208 V 3-phase\n  4-wire, so it can't serve it.\" Repeat it, and suggest the right kind of\n  panel; don't retry with other positions to get around it.\n- A device already on a circuit is refused by `bim_create_circuit`: take it\n  off with `bim_remove_from_circuit` first, and say that you moved it.\n- A 120/208 V panel can't come straight off a 480 V board: a transformer goes\n  between. Never place or feed one on your own; ask.\n- Findings never block edits. After a change, read the panel again and say\n  what the findings say (overloaded, unbalanced phases, a member whose voltage\n  no longer matches). Fix only what the person agrees to.\n- Demand is computed at each panel from every load below it, so 220.56\n  kitchen equipment counted at the MDP differs from the panel that serves it.\n  Never add up panels' demands to get the MDP's; read the MDP.\n- Quote numbers with their basis: \"L1 can carry 208 A, limited by what is left\n  in T1\", \"kitchen 4 units at 80% (NEC 2023 Table 220.56)\". If\n  `rules_placeholder` is true, say the totals are connected loads, not a code\n  calculation, and ask for the NEC edition (or to record it in the Code Study).\n\n### Workflow: circuit a floor\n\n(e.g. \"circuit the office receptacles on level 2 to L1, and feed L1 off the\nMDP through T1\"; one circuit is the same steps with one group)\n\n1. `bim_electrical_report` for the panels, what feeds each and their free\n   capacity; `bim_read_panel` with no panel for the devices on no circuit;\n   `bim_query_elements` (core, kinds `part`, the level) for ids and marks. Ask\n   for what the person didn't say: which panel, which devices go together,\n   breaker size if not the default.\n2. Distribution first, top down: `bim_feed_panel` each transformer from its\n   board's breaker, then each panel from its transformer or board. Say the\n   change first (\"feed T1 from MDP, 3-pole 125 A breaker on the lowest free\n   circuits\"); preview with `bim_preview_circuit_edit` when unsure.\n3. Each circuit: `bim_create_circuit` with `source_id`, exactly the `members`\n   the person grouped (or their room convention named), and a `description`\n   (\"Office 106 receptacles\"). Report the name the server gives back\n   (\"L1-3\") and its phase.\n4. Refused? Say the reason as written and stop for that circuit; carry on\n   with the others only if the person agrees.\n5. `bim_electrical_report` with `panel` for each panel you touched: say the\n   connected and NEC demand loads, the phases, `capacity.reason` and any\n   finding. An unbalanced phase: preview `bim_rebalance_panel` and offer the\n   moves; make them only if the person agrees.\n\n### Workflow: rebalance a panel\n\n`bim_preview_circuit_edit` with `tool: \"bim_rebalance_panel\"`, `args: { panel }`\n→ say the moves (\"L1-7 Office receptacles to L1-5, A → C; phases 720 / 180 /\n0 VA become 540 / 180 / 180\") → `bim_rebalance_panel` → read the panel again.\nIf the person wants a circuit left alone, pass it in `keep_circuits`.\n\n### Workflow: circuit dwelling units by a room template\n\n(e.g. \"circuit units 101–124 like our two-bedroom convention\")\n\n1. `bim_electrical_catalog` `kind: \"templates\"`. No template? Ask for the\n   convention (\"bedroom → 1, living → 3, kitchen counters → 5 and 7…\") and save\n   it with `bim_save_electrical_type` (`circuit_template`, rules top to bottom;\n   the first that matches a room's name wins).\n2. Get each unit's rooms (`bim_query_elements` kind `room`, by number) and ask\n   which panel serves each unit. Never pick a panel or rooms yourself, and\n   never group devices by distance.\n3. Preview `bim_apply_circuit_template` with `bim_preview_circuit_edit`; show\n   the circuits per unit, rooms no rule matched, devices left alone and any\n   refusal → `bim_apply_circuit_template`.\n\n### Workflow: connection types and feeder types\n\n- A designator (E.05, K-10) with its load, voltage, poles and class:\n  `bim_save_electrical_type` `connection_type`, then `bim_set_parameters`\n  `values.connection_type: \"E.05\"` on each device the person names.\n- A feeder type for H1: `bim_electrical_catalog` `size_feeder_for: \"H1\"` (with\n  the length) → show the proposal and its steps → `bim_save_electrical_type`\n  with it → `bim_edit_circuit` on H1's feeder (`supply_from.feeder_id` in\n  `bim_read_panel`) with `feeder_type` and the same `breaker_a`. Never invent\n  sizes; a feeder naming a missing mark is a finding.\n\n### Workflow: what needs attention?\n\n`bim_electrical_catalog` with `check: true`: errors first (an overload, a\nbreaker above a unit's MOCP), then loads on no circuit, unbalance, missing\ntypes. Offer the fix for each; make none unasked.\n\n### Workflow: what's the true capacity of H1?\n\n`bim_electrical_report` with `panel: \"H1\"`. Answer with `capacity.reason`\n(the lowest of its bus, its main, the breaker and conductors feeding it, and\nwhat is left upstream), `headroom_a`, and the sizing load it's compared with.\n\n### Workflow: wire up a model whose circuits are only typed text\n\n1. `bim_propose_links_from_text`. Show the person the `ready` proposals as a\n   short list (\"L1-3: R-1, R-2 (typed L1-3)\"), and the `refused`,\n   `unmatched` and `ambiguous` ones with their reasons.\n2. Make only the ones they confirm, feeders first, one `to_make_it` call each\n   (each pauses for approval). Never run them all because they are ready.\n3. `bim_electrical_report` to show the totals now that the panels are fed.\n\nLint `fed-from-text-mismatch` also fires when a panel's typed Fed from names a\nsource but nothing feeds it (\"says MDP but nothing feeds it\"): feed it with\n`bim_feed_panel` once the person confirms the source, or clear the text.\n\n## Schedules and live tags\n\nElectrical schedules are schedule views whose every value is read from the\nmodel when the sheet is drawn: circuits, breakers, loads, per-phase kVA,\ndemand, \"Supply from\". **Never type a load or a circuit into a schedule or a\nnote**; change the model (circuits above, `bim_set_parameters` for a load) and\nevery schedule and tag follows in the same revision.\n\nMaking, changing and reading them is the `mutinybim-schedules` pack\n(activate it): `bim_schedule_define` with `form: { kind, panel, template_name,\nnec_edition }` (kinds `panel`, `switchboard`, `feeder`, `connection_types`,\n`equipment_connections`, `gas_equipment`), `bim_library` for the built-in and\nfirm templates (`read` id `form:panel`: the template, every field path and the\ntemplate language), and `bim_schedule_read` (model family) to read one in full.\n\nA new schedule starts from `template_name`, else the firm default for its\nkind, else the built-in one. The NEC edition is `nec_edition`, else the\nproject's Code Study; with neither the schedule prints connected loads only\nand says so on the sheet: tell the person and ask for the edition. A schedule\nkeeps its own copy of its template, so a new firm default never changes\nschedules already drawn.\n\nField paths (`{path}` in a template): on a device `circuit` (`HP1-43,45`),\n`circuit.number` (`43,45`), `circuit.breaker`, `circuit.conductors`, `panel`,\n`load`, `load_class`, `voltage_phase`; on a panel `supply_from`\n(`MDP via 225A/3P via F225`), `feeder.source.name`, `feeder.breaker`,\n`feeder.type`, `feeder.type.conductors`, `bus`, `mains_text`, `volts`,\n`voltage_system`, `phases_wires`, `kaic`, `connected_kva`, `demand_kva`,\n`capacity_a`. The live one-line reads the same paths (`bus_rating`,\n`supply.text`), so a schedule, a tag and the diagram never disagree. The full\nlist is in `bim_library` (`read` id `form:<kind>`). `[ ... ]` prints only when every\nfield inside has a value: `{feeder.source.name}[ via {feeder.breaker}][ via {feeder.type}]`\nnever ends in \"via\" before a feeder type is chosen. `\\{` prints a brace.\n\nTags use the same paths: a tag style made with `bim_save_tag_style`\n(`mutinybim-drafting`) with the line `{circuit}` prints `HP1-43,45` on a\ndevice, `{circuit.number}` the number beside it, and `[FED FROM {supply_from}]`\non a panel. They update when the circuit moves.\n\n### Workflow: put L1's panel schedule on E-601\n\n1. Activate `mutinybim-schedules`. `bim_library` `list` (type\n   `schedule_template`): say which panel template it will use.\n2. `bim_schedule_define` with `form: { kind: \"panel\", panel: \"L1\" }`,\n   `place_on: { sheet: \"E-601\" }` (create the sheet first with\n   `bim_create_sheet` in `mutinybim-views-sheets`). Report the code and the\n   totals from the preview's `prints`, then commit it (approval).\n3. **Look:** `bim_render_sheet_image` on E-601 (zoom with `region_in`).\n4. Wording or columns the firm wants differently: `bim_schedule_define` with\n   `schedule` and `form.template` (a whole template from `bim_library`).\n\n## One-line and riser diagrams: live, from the model\n\nWhen the model has the distribution (panels, switchboards, transformers fed\nby `bim_feed_panel` / `bim_create_circuit`), a one-line is a **live diagram**:\na drafting view that stores only which diagram it is and the person's layout\nhints, and is drawn from the model's feeders every time anyone looks at it or\nprints it. Change H1's bus from 225 to 400 A and the one-line says 400A BUS\nand warns \"400A BUS FED BY 225A BREAKER\" in the same save, with no edit to the\ndiagram. Text blocks read live fields (bus, MLO/MCB, voltage system, phases and\nwires, NEMA enclosure, kAIC); feeder breakers and feeder type ovals (F225) come\nfrom the feeders. Nothing on it is dummy text, and nothing is stored as line\nwork, so never edit one with `bim_draw_detail` or `bim_modify_detail`.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_preview_one_line` (read) | `from_model: true` (+ `kind`: `one_line` default, `electrical_riser`, `plumbing_riser`) with `new_view_name`, or `view` for an existing drafting view: what it draws (`equipment`), `warnings` (bus vs feeding breaker, unfed panels, feeder loops), size, `would_save` + `preview_token` |\n| `bim_layout_one_line` (approval) | Save what was previewed: the live view (or its new hints), and `place_on: { sheet, center_in }`. Same arguments plus `base_revision` and `preview_token` |\n\n`bim_list_views_and_sheets` (model family) reports each live diagram's\n`live_diagram`: kind, hints, equipment drawn now, warnings.\n\nLayout is **hints**, merged over the view's own: `orientation` (`vertical`,\n`horizontal`), `style` (`box3d` for the 3D-box look), `detail` (`schematic`:\nnames, buses and systems only, for early design before schedules exist),\n`order` (`{ \"<source id>\": [\"<child id>\", …] }`, `$roots` for the services),\n`pins` (`{ \"<id>\": { dx, dy } }` mm: move a piece and what it feeds),\n`textOffsets` (`{ \"<key>\": { dx, dy } }` mm: move one block of text only; keys\nfrom the preview's `texts`, e.g. `h1#equipment#0` for H1's name block; it keeps\nreading the model, with a leader home when moved well away),\n`sections` (`[{ id, label, elementIds }]` dashed boxes), `futureSpaces`\n(`{ \"<bus id>\": n }` dashed SPACE FOR FUTURE), `hidden`, `roots`, `utility`\n(utility source above each service, default on), `showWarnings` (default on),\n`showLoads` (motors and units on branch circuits), `labels` (text lines per\n`bus` / `transformer` / `equipment` as field templates, e.g.\n`\"{supply.source.name} VIA {supply.breaker}\"`), `title`, `notes`,\n`systemKinds` (plumbing riser). A separately metered service (fire pump,\nhouse) is drawn in its own dashed box when its equipment's `service` value\nsays so. To change the layout, call the same tools with `view` and only the\nhints that change.\n\nA `bus_above_feeding_breaker` or `breaker_above_bus` warning is also a finding\nin `bim_electrical_report`. Say it to the person; never \"fix\" it by changing a\nbreaker or bus they didn't ask you to change.\n\n### Static diagrams: only when there is no model\n\n`diagram` (instead of `from_model`) draws a distribution described by hand\ninto a drafting view as symbols, conductors and notes. Use it only when the\nperson describes a service that is not in the model (a quick sketch, an\nexisting building nobody has modeled). Never use it for equipment that is in\nthe model: that is a live diagram. To turn an old static one-line into a live\none, give its `view` with `from_model: true` and `replace_existing: true`.\n\nThe static `diagram`: `title`, `notes` (numbered under it), and `roots` (sources:\n`utility` or `generator`). Each node: `id` (letters, digits, `_ . -`), `kind`,\n`name`, `ratings` (up to 6 lines, as they print), `children`, `placeholder`\n(a dashed future space), and `feeder` — how its parent feeds it: `device`\n(`breaker` default from a bus, `drawout_breaker`, `fuse`, `fused_switch`,\n`switch`, `none`), `device_label` (e.g. `400AF/400AT 3P`), `sets`,\n`conductors`, `conduit`, `length_ft`, `type_mark` (F225 oval). Kinds: `utility`, `meter`,\n`ct_cabinet`, `switchboard` / `panelboard` / `mcc` (bus equipment; `main: { device, label }`\nfor a main, leave it out for MLO and say MLO in the ratings), `transformer`,\n`generator`, `ats` (`emergency`: its generator node, drawn beside it),\n`disconnect`, `fused_disconnect`, `vfd`, `ups`, `motor`, `equipment` (a load box).\n`spd: true` hangs a surge protective device and `ground: true` a ground on bus\nequipment, a transformer or a source.\n\nA **meter center** (several tenants, units or lots on one service) is a\n`switchboard` node for the meter bus (its main as `main`, `ground: true`) whose\nchildren are `meter` nodes with `feeder: { device: \"none\" }`, each with one\nchild: the unit's `panelboard`, fed through its own `feeder` (`device: \"breaker\"`,\n`device_label: \"200A 2P\"`, conductors, conduit), `ground: true` where each\nservice has its own electrode. Eight lots draw about 26 in wide.\n\nFor a static diagram the preview tells you when the view already has drawn\nitems (`drawn_over_existing_items`); redraw it with `view` and\n`replace_existing: true` instead of deleting items one by one.\n\nNever build a one-line by hand from `bim_draw_detail` circles, rectangles and\nsheet notes, and never hand the person an HTML or SVG file when they asked for\nthe drawing in the model. If `bim_preview_one_line` or `bim_layout_one_line` is\nnot in your tool list, turn this family on; if they are still missing, tell\nthe person exactly which tools are missing. Don't substitute.\n\nConventions (general practice; the firm's standards win when given):\n\n- Ratings on every piece: amps, voltage, phase and wires (`1600A, 480Y/277V, 3Ø, 4W`),\n  short-circuit rating (`65 kAIC`), MLO or main size. Transformers: kVA and\n  both voltages (`75 kVA`, `480Δ - 208Y/120V`). Generators: kW and voltage.\n- Feeder tags: parallel sets, conductors with ground (`4#500 KCMIL CU, 1#3 G`),\n  conduit, length. Breaker labels as frame / trip / poles (`400AF/400AT 3P`).\n- Ask for the service size, voltage, available fault current and anything you\n  would otherwise invent. Never make up ratings or conductor sizes: size them\n  with `bim_run_calc` (`mutinybim-calcs`), leave a rating out and tell the\n  person, or ask.\n- Don't paste code tables into notes.\n\n## Workflow: the one-line on E-601\n\n(e.g. \"put the one-line on E-601\", \"show the fire pump service\", \"a riser for\nthe building\")\n\n1. `bim_electrical_report`: is the distribution in the model and fed? If\n   panels are unfed or missing, say so and circuit them first\n   (`bim_feed_panel`, after the person confirms); a live diagram can only show\n   what the model says.\n2. `bim_list_views_and_sheets`: reuse an existing live one-line\n   (`live_diagram.kind`) instead of making a second; `bim_create_sheet`\n   (`mutinybim-views-sheets`) for `E-601` if it isn't there.\n3. `bim_preview_one_line` with `from_model: true`, `new_view_name` (e.g.\n   \"E-601 One-line diagram\") and `place_on` → say \"a live one-line of N pieces\n   of equipment on E-601, kept up to date as the model changes\" and read out\n   its `warnings` → `bim_layout_one_line` with `base_revision` and\n   `preview_token`.\n4. **Look:** `bim_render_view_image`, then `bim_render_sheet_image`. If it is\n   crowded, change hints (`orientation`, `order`, `pins`, `detail`), never the\n   drawing; text that runs into other text is listed in the preview's\n   `text_overlaps`: move one of the two with `textOffsets`. Look again. If no picture comes back that you can see, say you\n   checked by `bim_list_views_and_sheets` only.\n5. `bim_print_sheet_pdf` and give the link.\n\nA riser is the same with `kind: \"electrical_riser\"` (equipment by level).\nPlumbing risers and isometrics: `mutinybim-plumbing`.\n\n## Workflow: a static single-line diagram (no model)\n\n1. Gather: service voltage and size, available fault current, each piece of\n   equipment with its ratings, what feeds what, feeder sizes and lengths.\n   Ask for what's missing; never invent it.\n2. Write the `diagram` top down (utility → CT cabinet or meter → switchboard\n   with its main → each feeder's breaker, conductors and equipment → panels,\n   transformers, the ATS with its `emergency` generator, loads).\n3. `bim_preview_one_line` with `new_view_name` and `place_on` → say \"draws N\n   pieces of equipment, about W x H in, on E-601\" → `bim_layout_one_line`.\n4. **Look** as above; fix by moving a piece's detail group\n   (`bim_modify_detail`) or editing a label (`bim_edit_text_note`). Never tell\n   the person it looks right without a picture or a measurement.\n5. `bim_print_view_pdf` or `bim_print_sheet_pdf`, and give the link.\n\n## Circuiting on plans, callouts and typicals\n\nPlans draw each circuit from the model every time they print: never draw\ncircuit lines, home-run arrows or circuit numbers with detail lines or notes.\nMoving a device moves its line and home run. Electrical plans (discipline\nelectrical) draw arcs unless the view says otherwise.\n\n| What | How (all through `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` → `bim_apply_views_sheets_edit`) |\n| --- | --- |\n| Line style | `set_view_properties` `props.circuitGraphics.style`: `arcs`, `orthogonal` (square corners), `numbers` (the circuit number beside each device, multifamily), `off` |\n| Home runs, switch legs | `circuitGraphics.homeRuns` / `switchLegs` (default on). The home run is on the member nearest the panel, tagged `<panel>-<circuit>` (\"L1-1,3\"). Switch legs are dashed, switch to the fixtures with its switch letter |\n| Typical units | `circuitGraphics.allCopies: true` draws each copy's circuiting; off, the original's home runs read \"TYP.\" |\n| A line's bend | `circuitGraphics.hints[<circuit id>].bends[\"<idA>\\|<idB>\"] = {x, y}` (mm, ids sorted); `hints[id].priority` decides which line breaks where two cross |\n| Callout to an enlarged plan | `add_drawing_item` `shape: \"callout\"`, points `[corner, opposite corner, head]` (model mm), `callout: { viewId, label? }`. The head reads that view's number and sheet live |\n\n`set_view_properties` replaces the whole `circuitGraphics`: read the view's\ncurrent one (`bim_list_views_and_sheets`) and send it back with your change.\n\n**Typicals** (unit 001 repeated 24 times): put one unit's panel and devices in\ntheir own group (`mutinybim-groups-phases`), feed the unit panel from the\nhouse panel, then `bim_update_group` with `typical { count: 24, columns,\nspacing_ft }`. `bim_read_panel` on the house panel then shows the feeder's\n`typical_count: 24` and 24 times the unit's load in its totals; the unit panel\nitself still counts one unit. Copies fed through a feeder spread over a\n3-phase house panel's phases (A-B, B-C, C-A…) by default, as 24 real breakers\nwould; `typical.phase_rotation: \"same\"` keeps every copy on the original's\nphases when the engineer says so.\n\nWho is on a circuit stays the engineer's choice. The drawing only joins the\nmembers the engineer put on it, nearest first from the home run.\n\n## Proposing receptacles and sizing the service\n\nThe person picks the rooms and the receptacle type for each kind of location;\nthe proposal is code spacing only. Accepting places devices and nothing else:\nnever circuit them as part of it, and never pick a type yourself.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_propose_receptacles` (read) | `rooms` (ids or numbers), `wall_type` (and `countertop_type`, `bathroom_type`), `mode` `dwelling` (NEC 210.52: 6 ft along wall space, counters 24 in, islands, bathrooms, laundry, hallways) or `density` with the person's `preset` (`light` ≈ 25 ft, `medium` ≈ 15 ft, `heavy` ≈ 6 ft; a firm preset, not code: it needs no `nec_edition` and its `code` says no code edition was applied; ask for the edition only for dwelling). Wall devices sit with their back flush on the wall face, clear of the corners; a \"touches wall\" soft clash on the device's own wall is expected. Each room: how it is treated and why, each device's `key`, position, reason and clause, and `coverage` (worst distance against the limit). `room_rules` overrides a room's treatment |\n| `bim_accept_receptacles` (approval) | The same arguments plus `keep` (the keys the person kept; default all) and `base_revision`. Re-runs the proposal and places the kept ones as one edit |\n\nThe rooms come from `bim_trace_rooms` (`mutinybim-coordination`) and the\nearly service size from `bim_service_from_equipment_list` (`mutinybim-calcs`):\nactivate that pack alongside this one when the workflow below needs it.\n\n### Workflow: receptacles for a unit\n\n1. `bim_query_elements` kind `room`. None: trace them (`bim_trace_rooms`)\n   from the plan the person points to, after they approve.\n2. Ask which rooms, and which receptacle types if they haven't said\n   (the Electrical tab defaults: duplex on walls, countertop GFCI, GFCI in\n   bathrooms). Commercial open areas: ask light, medium or heavy.\n3. `bim_propose_receptacles`. Tell them per room how many, any room that\n   isn't covered and why, and the notes (a basin you couldn't find, counters\n   split by a sink).\n4. They keep all or name the ones to drop; `bim_accept_receptacles` with\n   `keep`. Then offer circuiting as a separate step (`bim_create_circuit`,\n   or `bim_apply_circuit_template` with the panel they name).\n\n### Workflow: service size before layout\n\n1. Get the kitchen consultant's equipment schedule and the mechanical list\n   (paste, CSV, or read the PDF and pass its text) and the service voltage.\n2. `bim_service_from_equipment_list`. Show the rows whose class you'd\n   question (word guesses) and the ones with a `problem`; ask.\n3. Re-run with `classes` (by each row's `line`; the header is a line too).\n   Give the size, the minimum ampacity (`required_a`) and the trail's key\n   lines (220.56 count and factor, 25% of the largest motor, 125% of the\n   continuous load), and say it is an early estimate.\n\n### Workflow: starter service layout (one-line skeleton)\n\n(e.g. \"start the one-line from a 400 A 208 V service\", \"lay out the service\nfrom that size\")\n\n`bim_place_service_layout` (approval) places the meter, an optional fused\n`main_disconnect`, the main distribution (`distribution.name`, default MDP)\nand the `equipment` rows the person lists (`kind` panel with `bus_a`,\n`system`, `mains`; or transformer with `kva`, `system` = its secondary),\neach with `fed_from` naming another piece, as one edit. Where it goes:\n`room` (id, number or name: the lineup runs along that room's south wall,\nclear of it) or `origin_ft` (the lineup's left end in plan; wins over\n`room`); neither puts it at the level origin. The Electrical tab's Service\nlayout sends the same command (Where it goes: In a room, At a point with\nPick in plan, or Level origin).\n\n**Plan only** (`plan_only: true`, no `room` or `origin_ft`): the meter,\nboard, panels and feeders exist (one-line, panel schedules, circuits) but are\nnot drawn; `level` is where they will go. Use it when the person wants the\none-line or a load study before the electrical room is laid out. Each piece\nis placed later from Not placed yet, by the person or with `bim_place_part`\n`part_id`. Devices can be circuited to a planned panel, and a planned device\ncan be circuited too: everything keeps its id when placed.\n\n1. Size: the person's number, or `bim_service_from_equipment_list` above.\n2. Ask for every panel and transformer: name, rating, system, main breaker or\n   lugs, what feeds it, and each transformer's primary breaker. Never fill\n   them in yourself; nothing is sized from loads. Ask which room it goes in\n   (the electrical room) and pass `room`; a room that isn't in the model is\n   refused with the rooms that are.\n3. Call it. A refusal (a 208 V panel on a 480 V board, a missing primary\n   breaker, a name already in the model) is the reason to read out; fix and\n   call again. The approval card's notes say when the lineup is longer than\n   the room or leaves under 3 ft of working space (NEC 110.26(A)(1)); read\n   them out.\n4. Then the one-line workflow above: `bim_preview_one_line` `from_model: true`\n   draws it with the utility over the meter. Circuiting devices stays a\n   separate step.\n\nPoint-to-point fault current is a preview calc (`elec.fault_current_p2p`\nthrough `bim_run_calc`) only where the deployment turns it on; otherwise use\nthe `elec-short-circuit` skill.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_electrical_report` |\n| `bim_read_panel` |\n| `bim_electrical_catalog` |\n| `bim_propose_links_from_text` |\n| `bim_preview_circuit_edit` |\n| `bim_create_circuit` |\n| `bim_edit_circuit` |\n| `bim_add_to_circuit` |\n| `bim_remove_from_circuit` |\n| `bim_feed_panel` |\n| `bim_rebalance_panel` |\n| `bim_save_electrical_type` |\n| `bim_apply_circuit_template` |\n| `bim_preview_one_line` |\n| `bim_layout_one_line` |\n| `bim_propose_receptacles` |\n| `bim_accept_receptacles` |\n| `bim_place_service_layout` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-plumbing": "---\nname: mutinybim-plumbing\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for plumbing loads and design: fixture load points on open pipe ends and taps, connection types, the plumbing catalogue (pipe types), each pipe system's look and sizing, the project's sizing and demand curve. Use with the mutinybim core; riser diagrams are in the electrical pack. Trigger on: fixture units, load point, DFU, WFU, pipe type, pipe system sizing, demand curve.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the plumbing family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs plumbing, or /packs/plumbing/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"plumbing\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"plumbing\"\n  token-cap: \"6300\"\n  tools: \"bim_place_load_points bim_edit_load_point bim_save_connection_type bim_plumbing_catalog bim_plumbing_design\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: plumbing load points and connection types\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the four starting reads and the rules for every change (say the change, edit with `base_revision`, report the server's answer).\nPart placement and the pictures are the `model` family. Draw the pipes with\n`mutinybim-ducts-pipes` (or a script); read what they carry\n(`bim_pipe_report`) with `mutinybim-calcs`.\n\n## The idea\n\nA load point is what is connected at an open pipe end (or a tap) before, or\ninstead of, a modelled fixture: \"the end of this 2\" waste is a P01 water\ncloset, 5 DFU\". It names a **connection type** (the catalog record: P01,\nK07, FD3) and may override its values. Every pipe then carries the loads\nbeyond it, live, the same numbers the pipe report uses.\n\n- **One point per pipe end, per system.** A hand sink has a cold, a hot and\n  a waste point. Give them the same `mark` (\"K01-2\"): the pipe that carries\n  both sides (the service, above the water heater) then counts the\n  fixture's **total** WSFU (2), not cold + hot (3).\n- **A fixture attached later** takes the point's connection type and its\n  loads come from the point: one record, two views. Edit either; totals do\n  not double. Detaching keeps the point.\n- **A lump** (\"whole kitchen, 30 DFU\", `schematic: true`) breaks out onto new\n  points later; what the new points take comes off the lump, so every total\n  stays the same.\n- **Ducts too**: a 150 CFM point on a tap's open branch adds 150 CFM to the\n  trunk, before any air device is placed.\n- A point belongs to the pipe end the person named. Attach a fixture only to\n  the point the person names; never attach by nearness, and never pick a\n  fixture, heater or interceptor model for them.\n\n## Tools\n\nReads in other families:\n\n- `bim_query_elements` kind `service_catalog` (core, read): The project's connection types, every trade, with their loads\n- `bim_query_elements` kind `load_point` (core, read): Every load point: what it is on, its connection type, its loads, its fixture, and `host` lost / continued when its pipe was deleted or now runs on past it\n- `bim_pipe_report` (`mutinybim-calcs`, read): What every pipe carries (`pipes[].fixture_units`), the system total, design flow, and warnings (a point with no value for its system)\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_save_connection_type` (approval) | Add or change a connection type: `designator`, `trade`, `description`, `quantities` { `wsfu_cold`, `wsfu_hot`, `wsfu_total` (the code's combined value), `dfu`, `gph_demand`, `gpm_fixed`, `mbh`, `min_inlet_pressure`, `cfm`, `sizes` { cw, hw, w, v, g }, `custom` [{ name, unit, value }] }. A bare number is in the quantity's unit (`min_inlet_pressure` is **psi**); write text with a unit to be sure: `\"7 in. w.c.\"`, `\"2 psi\"`. A gas minimum above 14 in. w.c. saves with a warning (it is nearly always in. w.c. written as psi). `replaces` renames it (points and fixtures follow); `delete` removes one nothing uses |\n| `bim_place_load_points` (approval) | One batch of points (up to 500): each `pipe_id` + `end` (\"start\" / \"end\" of a segment, \"branch\" of a tap), `connection_type`, optional `values`, `mark`, `schematic`, `note`. The end must be open. A starter type (WC-FT, WC-FV, UR, LAV, HS, SK1–SK3, MS, FD, FS, TD, HB, DF, IM, DW, G-RANGE, G-FRYER, G-GRILL…) the project lacks is added with the points, labelled \"verify for permit work\". A point whose type has no connection for the pipe's system (a gas range on a sanitary pipe) refuses the batch, naming the point; the result lists each point with its pipe, mark, type, system and loads, so check them against the plan |\n| `bim_edit_load_point` (approval) | `action`: `edit` (`connection_type`, `values` merged on, `reset` to drop own values; `part_id` instead of `load_point_id` edits every point of that fixture), `attach` (`load_point_id` + `part_id`), `detach`, `split` (`into`: new points with the values each takes), `delete` (`load_point_ids`); `served_by` / `clear_served_by` on `edit` names or drops the load's source (WH-1, the gas meter, panel P1) before anything is drawn; `create` (`loads`: each with an `anchor` — `{kind:\"space\",id}` or `zone` for an estimate, `part`, `run_end`, `point`, or none for a row — `values`, `connection_type`, `service`, `mark`); `move` (`load_point_id` + `to`: same id, estimate → point → device); `policy` (`max`, the default: an estimate counts until the devices inside it add up to more). `load_point_id` takes any load id, not only `lp:` ones |\n| `bim_plumbing_catalog` (read) | Pipe types (firm and this project's: material, spec, sizes, joint, systems, the fitting in each slot), every pipe system's colour, line pattern, weight and own sizing, the project's auto-sizing, building type, demand curve and pipe insulation schedule, and the **stacks**: each named one by number, then drawn vertical runs tall enough to be one (\"not named yet\"), with `tag` (`4\"//S/K/L//19`), `pipe_id`, size and DFU at the base, rooms served, and per floor size, DFU and offsets; `stacks_lost` says when a named stack's pipe was redrawn |\n| `bim_plumbing_design` (approval) | One change by `action`: `system_look` (`system`, `colour` #rrggbb, `pattern`, `weight`; null = standard), `system_sizing` (`system`, `sizing_mode`, `demand_curve`; null = the project's), `project` (`sizing_mode`, `building_type`, `demand_curve`, `insulation_schedule` [{ system_kinds, below_in, thickness_in, material }] with `insulation_source`: the energy code's table isn't in the code library, so use the firm's spec or the starter the Plumbing tab offers, and say where it came from), `save_pipe_type` (`pipe_type` { name, material, spec, system_kinds, min_in, max_in, joint, fittings, note }, `replaces`), `delete_pipe_type` (`name`) |\n\n**Stacks.** Name, number or rename them through the model family's\n`bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` → `bim_apply_views_sheets_edit`:\n`name_stacks { stacks: [{ stackId, pipeId, number?, name? }] }` (any vertical\npipe of the run; no `number`: the next free one), `edit_stack` (`systemCode` /\n`serves` override the tag's letters; `null` goes back to derived),\n`delete_stacks` (names only; the pipe stays). The base-of-stack check\n(`set_stack_checks { baseClearanceDiameters: 3 }`, the firm's 3-pipe-diameter\nrule) is off by default: turn it on only when the person asks.\n\n**Grease routing.** The sewer authority decides which kitchen fixtures go\nthrough a grease interceptor. It lives on the sewer connection part (the\nexisting manhole or tie-in) as `sewer_authority`: read the project's\n`code_study_plumbing_locality` and set it with `bim_set_parameters`; never\nguess one. Findings `grease-fixture-bypasses-interceptor`,\n`grease-fixture-not-routed`, `grease-authority-unknown` come back in\n`bim_lint_check`; fix a route by naming the interceptor as the fixture's\nsource (`bim_edit_load_point` `served_by`).\n\nA point's id is `lp:<pipe id>:<end>`. A run segment's `start` / `end` facts\nin `bim_query_elements` say \"open, load point …\" when one sits there.\n\n## The order an agent calls them\n\n1. Read the fixture list the person gave (a schedule, a PDF table, a paste).\n   `bim_query_elements` kind `service_catalog`: which connection types exist.\n2. `bim_save_connection_type` for each type missing, with the values from\n   the person's schedule (not from memory; starter values are placeholders).\n3. Find the open pipe ends (`bim_query_elements` kind `run_segment`, ends\n   with no link) the person means for each fixture; ask when it is unclear.\n4. `bim_place_load_points` once, with every point.\n5. `bim_pipe_report` for the sanitary, cold and hot systems: check the\n   totals against the person's schedule (the service WSFU total, the DFU at\n   the outfall) and say both numbers.\n6. Later, a fixture placed (`bim_place_part`): `bim_edit_load_point`\n   `attach` to the points the person names; the report reads the same.\n\n## Systems, sizing and pipe types (the Plumbing tab)\n\n- **Look.** Each system kind has a standard look (cold water blue dash-dot,\n  hot red, vent green dashed, gas yellow long dash, sanitary brown solid);\n  3D, plans and print all use it. Change one system's (a grease waste line\n  heavy) with `system_look`; null puts the standard back.\n- **Auto-sizing is off** (\"check\": every pipe audited, sizes suggested,\n  nothing changes) until the person asks. `project` `sizing_mode: \"auto\"`\n  turns it on; a system can keep its own (`system_sizing`). On auto, turning\n  it on and every later edit that touches the system resize its pipes (water,\n  sanitary, gas), each system's resize its own undo, and the tool result says\n  \"Auto-sized N pipes on …\". Auto needs the project's plumbing or fuel gas\n  code: without it the edit still saves, sizes don't change, and the result\n  carries `sizing: { applied: false, reason, systems, fix }`. \"Saved\" is not\n  \"sized\"; read that field, and record the code in the Code Study (ask the\n  person which edition) before relying on auto.\n- **Demand curve.** `building_type` picks it (restaurant: ASHRAE Curve A);\n  `demand_curve` overrides, per project or per system.\n- **Pipe types.** A type's fittings follow its material unless `fittings`\n  picks one (ids from `bim_list_fitting_types`). Saving a firm type's name\n  makes a project copy; deleting it brings the firm's back.\n\nOrder: `bim_plumbing_catalog` → say the change → `bim_plumbing_design` →\n`bim_plumbing_catalog` again and report what changed.\n\n## Risers and isometrics (live, from the piping)\n\nA riser is a drafting view that stores only its kind and hints; it is drawn\nagain from the routed pipe every time, so a new fixture, a resize or a moved\nbranch shows on it in the same revision. Never draw risers with drafting\nlines.\n\nWith the electrical family turned on:\n\n- `bim_preview_one_line` (`mutinybim-electrical`, read): `from_model: true`, `kind` `plumbing_riser` (mains, a branch per fixture, stacks by floor, devices on the line) or `plumbing_isometric` (every pipe projected at 30° from the model), `new_view_name` or `view`, `hints`, `place_on`. Returns `equipment` (fixtures, devices, sources), `pipes` (each model pipe: the id a click on it selects, and how many drawn `pieces` light up for it), size, `preview_token`\n- `bim_layout_one_line` (`mutinybim-electrical`, approval): Save exactly what was previewed\n\nHints: `systemKinds` (a typical set: `[\"sanitary\",\"vent\"]`, `[\"domestic_cold_water\",\"domestic_hot_water\"]`, `[\"natural_gas\"]`), `pipeLabels` (`size`, `load`, `slope`, `invert`, `length`; default: drains size + DFU + slope, water size + WFU, gas size + MBH + LF), `hidden` (a fixture's or system's id), `pins`, `textOffsets` (move one label: keys from the preview's `texts`).\n\nWhat it shows: the sources the pipe report counts from (meter, water heater,\ngas entry; `TO SEWER` at a drain's outfall; `THRU ROOF` on a vent), running\nloads where they change (the sanitary main's 20 → 26 → 59 → 88 DFU), each\nfixture's own load, sizes, slopes, gas lengths, cleanouts and shut-offs. Hot\nwater leaving the heater on the cold line is one riser. Vents print dashed.\n`NO SOURCE CONNECTED` / `NO OUTFALL CONNECTED` means that piece of pipe isn't\njoined to a source yet: say so, don't hide it.\n\n1. `bim_pipe_report` for each system: the riser prints these numbers.\n2. `bim_preview_one_line` with `kind` and `systemKinds`; check `pipes` and\n   `equipment` against what the person expects.\n3. `bim_layout_one_line` with the preview's `base_revision` + `preview_token`.\n4. `bim_render_view_image`; if crowded, change hints, never the drawing.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_place_load_points` |\n| `bim_edit_load_point` |\n| `bim_save_connection_type` |\n| `bim_plumbing_catalog` |\n| `bim_plumbing_design` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-views-sheets": "---\nname: mutinybim-views-sheets\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for views and sheets: create, rename, duplicate and delete views, view properties and crops, 3D views with camera and crop box, sheets, title block properties, viewports and their layout, sheet notes and text notes, issue status. Use with the mutinybim core, which previews and saves view and sheet batches and prints and issues the set. Trigger on: create a view, crop, 3D view, create sheet, viewport, title block, sheet layout, sheet note.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the views-sheets family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs views-sheets, or /packs/views-sheets/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"views_sheets\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"views-sheets\"\n  token-cap: \"10600\"\n  tools: \"bim_create_view bim_set_view_properties bim_rename_view bim_duplicate_view bim_delete_view bim_create_sheet bim_set_sheet_properties bim_delete_sheet bim_place_viewport bim_edit_viewport bim_preview_sheet_layout bim_auto_layout_sheet bim_set_issue_status bim_add_sheet_note bim_edit_text_note bim_create_3d_view bim_set_3d_view_camera bim_set_3d_crop_box\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: views, sheets and drawing sets\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the four starting reads and the rules for every change: preview, say the change, edit with `base_revision`, report the server's\nanswer, and look at every drawing you change. Turn on the `model` family\ntoo: it has `bim_list_views_and_sheets`, the views/sheets preview and apply\n(`bim_preview_views_sheets_edit`, `bim_apply_views_sheets_edit`), the\npictures (`bim_render_view_image`, `bim_render_sheet_image`), printing and\n`bim_issue_set`. A sheet and a plan view per level, with their viewports, is\none script (`documents.sheet`, `planView`, `place`; there is no sheet set\nfunction, so number the sheets in print order); the tools\nhere are for single changes and for laying out and checking what a script\nmade.\n\nDimensions, tags, notes with leaders and details are the `mutinybim-drafting`\npack; activate it too when the sheets need them.\n\n## Views, crops and sheets (each needs approval)\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_create_view` | Floor plan or ceiling plan for a level, section from a cut line, 3D, or detail view |\n| `bim_set_view_properties` | Scale, what a plan shows (`top_ft`, `cut_height_ft`, `bottom_ft`, `view_depth_ft`), line weights by category (`line_weights`), discipline, hidden categories, **how each linked model draws** (`linked_models`, see `mutinybim-coordination`), detail level, and the **crop box**: `crop_min_ft` + `crop_max_ft` (both), `crop_view`, `show_crop_box`, `annotation_crop`, `far_clip_ft` (sections). `fit_crop` sizes the crop to the content and must be sent on its own. A placed viewport resizes with its crop. `projection: \"perspective\" \\| \"orthographic\"` (3D views only; plans, sections and details are always orthographic and refuse it) sets how the live 3D view draws depth; sheets print 3D views orthographic either way. **`category_overrides`**: Visibility/Graphics for any category (see below) |\n| `bim_create_sheet` | New sheet with number, name, title block (ARCH D 36x24 default, or ANSI B 17x11) and title block fields |\n| `bim_place_viewport` | Put a view on a sheet at a paper position (inches from top-left). A view goes on one sheet only |\n| `bim_add_sheet_note` | Note on a sheet: a box at `at_in` (its top-left, inches from the sheet's top-left) that wraps at `width_in` (default 4\"). Plain `text`, or rich `paragraphs` (see below). Optional fixed `height_in`, `rotation_deg`, `leader_to_in`, default `text_height_in` (3/32\") |\n| `bim_edit_text_note` | Replace a note's whole text (sheet or detail view), keeping its box. Pass `expected_content` from `bim_list_views_and_sheets`; if anyone changed the note's words since, nothing is saved: read again and re-plan |\n| `bim_rename_view` / `bim_duplicate_view` / `bim_delete_view` | Rename; copy with its properties and crop (not placed); delete (`if_placed: \"remove_from_sheet\"` to take it off its sheet too, else refused when placed) |\n| `bim_set_sheet_properties` | Number, name, title block size, title block `fields` (client, address, drawn_by, checked_by, date, status, or any key; `\"\"` clears one), and the revision table: `add_revision` appends one row; `revisions` replaces it (oldest first). Rows print in the title block, newest on top. `project_name`, `project_number` and `firm_name` fill the block's fixed PROJECT, PROJECT NO. and firm slots; left out, those print the project name, number and firm the host gives the model. `bim_render_sheet_image` returns `title_block` with what printed |\n| `bim_delete_sheet` | Delete a sheet with its viewports, notes and linework; its views stay |\n| `bim_edit_viewport` | One placed view: `action` move (`center_in`), resize (`size_in`, the crop follows), scale, north_arrow (`show`), remove. Name it by its view. A live diagram (one-line, riser, isometric) or a schedule is as big as what it draws: move only; resize and scale are refused with its real size. A frame that runs past the drawing area still saves, with a warning naming its size and the sheet's |\n| `bim_preview_sheet_layout` (read) | Where every viewport on a sheet should go (packed rows or an aligned grid, inside the border and left of the title block, room for each view title, optional scale shrink to fit). Deterministic. Returns moves, what can't fit and why, and a `preview_token` for `bim_auto_layout_sheet` |\n| `bim_auto_layout_sheet` | Apply the layout `bim_preview_sheet_layout` returned (same arguments, its `base_revision` and `preview_token`) as one edit |\n| `bim_set_issue_status` (read only, issues nothing, despite the name) | Before issuing: each live block (`blocks`: `freshness` Live, Matches <issue> or Changed since <issue>, the text now and `issued_text` when it moved on), `print_check` (`matches_print`, `changed_since_print`, `not_printed`) with the sheets that draw differently than in the last `bim_print_set_pdf` (`changed_since_print`) or weren't in it (`not_in_print`), the `revision` to pass as `expected_revision`, `next_revision`, the last print and earlier issues with PDF links |\n\nMany of these in one approval: a `batch_documents` through\n`bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` → `bim_apply_views_sheets_edit` (model family).\n\n## What a plan shows, and its lines\n\n**Heights** (plans only, feet above the plan's level, on\n`bim_set_view_properties`): `top_ft` (nothing above it draws; at or above the\ncut; `null` = no limit, so ductwork above the cut still shows), `cut_height_ft`\n(walls are cut here), `bottom_ft` (at or below the cut; negative is below the\nlevel; `null` = no limit), `view_depth_ft` (how far below the bottom it still\nsees; sending it alone sets the bottom at the level). Our model and the linked\nmodels both follow them: an element wholly above the top or below\nbottom − depth is left out of the drawing. Read them back as `shows` in\n`bim_list_views_and_sheets`. Plans made before these existed have no top or\nbottom and draw everything on their level, as before. Sections use\n`far_clip_ft` instead and refuse top and bottom.\n\n**Line weights by category** (`line_weights` on `bim_set_view_properties`):\n`{\"duct\": {\"cut\": \"wide\", \"beyond\": \"medium\"}, \"walls\": {\"cut\": \"heavy\"}}`\nwith pens thin, medium, wide, heavy; `null` clears one pen or a whole category\nback to the drawing default. Categories: duct, duct_fitting, pipe,\nair_terminal, equipment, walls, doors_windows, rooms, grids, structure, tags,\ndimensions, electrical_devices, lighting_fixtures, electrical_equipment\n(panels and other gear), insulation. The same names hide categories. The linked models take their own pens under `linked_models`\n(`cut_line_weight`, `beyond_line_weight`) plus `linked_hidden_lines` and\n`linked_min_object_in`; see `mutinybim-coordination`. Read back as\n`line_weights` in the listing.\n\n**Visibility/Graphics by any category** (`category_overrides` on\n`bim_set_view_properties`): one shape for every category, not only the\nsixteen above. A category id is a view category (`duct`,\n`electrical_devices`), a class (`mep.electrical_device`, `mep.air_terminal`,\n`struct.beam`, a firm class or an add-on's class), `service:<id>`\n(`service:grease_waste`) or `drafting:<id>`. Each takes `visible`,\n`halftone`, `cut` and `beyond` pens, `pattern` (a system line pattern) and\n`colour` (`#rrggbb`):\n`{\"mep.electrical_device\": {\"halftone\": true}, \"service:grease_waste\": {\"cut\": \"wide\"}}`.\nIt merges into what the view has; `null` clears one category. An id no\nlibrary knows is refused with close ids: use one of those, never invent one.\nA model server that can't store per-category graphics yet refuses the edit in\nplain words (nothing changes); the older `hidden_categories` and\n`line_weights` still work there. The saved answer and the listing read it\nback as `category_overrides`.\n\nPeople set all of this in View properties: **What this view shows** (Top,\nCut height, Bottom, Depth below bottom) and **Lines and colours** (visual\nstyle, detail level, a line weight table by category, and the linked models:\nhalftone, Hide what is underneath, Leave out things smaller than, their cut\nand below-the-cut pens), also reached from Views › Graphics in the ribbon.\nName those when you hand work back.\n\n**Live model or printed drawing** is each person's own choice per view,\nremembered for them on every computer. There is no tool for it: never tell a\nperson you changed it, and don't ask them to switch for you; pictures and\nPDFs always draw the printed drawing.\n\n## 3D views and 3D crop boxes (each needs approval)\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_create_3d_view` | A saved 3D view, pre-cropped. `of_elements` (ids) or `region_ft` (`{min, max}` corners) fits its 3D crop box to the **storey and grid bay** around them: floor of their level to the next level up, out to the grid lines within 40 ft (else the next 10 ft line). `box_ft` (`center`, `size`, `turn_deg`) sets the box exactly. `look_from`: `south_west` (default), `south_east`, `north_west`, `north_east` (isometric corners from above), `south` / `north` / `east` / `west` (elevations), `top` (plan, north up). `projection` defaults to orthographic. `place_on: { sheet, center_in, scale }` places it on a sheet in the same approval |\n| `bim_set_3d_view_camera` | Turn a 3D view: `look_from`, or `eye_ft` + `target_ft` exactly; optional `projection`. It opens and prints from there; a placed viewport's frame resizes to the box seen from the new camera |\n| `bim_set_3d_crop_box` | `box_ft` exactly; `fit: \"selection\"` with `element_ids` or `region_ft`; `fit: \"model\"` (whole model, square to the working angle); `align: \"working_angle\"` or `align: { from_ft, to_ft }` turns the box about its middle, size kept; `crop_on` (false shows everything, box kept); `show_box` (only what editors see; never prints) |\n\n`bim_list_views_and_sheets` reports each 3D view's `crop_box_ft` (centre, size,\nturn), `camera_ft` (eye, target), `projection`, `crop_on` and how it prints.\nIn `batch_documents` the kernel steps are `create_3d_view`,\n`set_3d_view_camera` and `set_3d_crop_box`, in **millimetres**.\n\n**How 3D views print.** Vector hidden-line from the view's own camera,\northographic (a perspective view prints parallel from the same direction),\ncropped to the **whole** 3D box, heights included; where the box cuts duct,\npipe or equipment the cut prints with the cut pen and fill. The viewport is\n\"3D VIEW, NOT TO SCALE\"; its frame is the box seen from the camera, so you\ncannot drag the frame's edges: change the box or the camera. **Linked models\n(the architect's IFC) do not print in 3D views**; say so if the person expects\nwalls from the link on an isometric.\n\n## Schedules\n\nA schedule is a view: on a sheet it is as big as its rows and grows down from\nits top-left corner; it has no scale or crop. Reading one is\n`bim_schedule_read` (model family). Making or changing one (equipment, air devices,\nfixtures, panels, the `sheet_list` index), typing into its cells and the firm\nlibrary are the `mutinybim-schedules` pack: activate it for step 5 below.\n\n## Rich notes\n\nNotes print exactly as people see them on screen (one layout engine, the same\nembedded font). Send `paragraphs` instead of `text` for formatting:\n\n```json\n[\n  { \"runs\": [{ \"text\": \"GENERAL NOTES\", \"bold\": true, \"underline\": true }], \"align\": \"center\" },\n  { \"list\": \"numbered\", \"runs\": [{ \"text\": \"Seal all \" }, { \"text\": \"supply\", \"bold\": true }, { \"text\": \" joints.\" }] },\n  { \"list\": \"bullet\", \"level\": 1, \"runs\": [{ \"text\": \"SMACNA seal class A\", \"text_height_in\": 0.125 }] }\n]\n```\n\n- A run is text with optional `bold`, `italic`, `underline`, `text_height_in`.\n  `\"\\n\"` inside a run is a line break in the same paragraph.\n- A paragraph may be a list item: `list` (`bullet` / `numbered`), `level`\n  (0–4), `start` (restart numbering). Numbering continues across a list and\n  restarts after a plain paragraph. `align`: left, center, right, justify.\n- Text wraps at the box width. A fixed `height_in` drops lines that don't fit\n  (they won't print); leave it out to grow with the text.\n- Only letters, numbers, Latin-1 accents and drafting symbols print (°, ±, ½,\n  Ø, µ, –, —, •, ≤, ≥, ×). Anything else is refused with the characters named.\n- `bim_list_views_and_sheets` returns each note's `id`, full `text`, `width_in`\n  and `expected_content` (sheets, and detail views). People editing a note\n  lock it for each other, not for you: `expected_content` is what keeps your\n  edit from overwriting theirs.\n\n## Workflows\n\n**Make a sheet:** `bim_list_views_and_sheets` (reuse names and ids) →\n`bim_create_view` if needed → `bim_set_view_properties` with `fit_crop: true`\n(or an explicit crop) → `bim_create_sheet` → `bim_place_viewport` →\n`bim_render_sheet_image` and look → `bim_print_sheet_pdf` once everything\nreports saved. Read `print_notes` on every print: it names viewports that run\npast the drawing area and labels the PDF font printed as plain text (`1⅜\"` as\n`1-3/8\"`). The print still succeeds; fix the viewport before issuing.\n\n**Produce a construction drawing set.** The whole job, end to end, with the\nperson approving in batches. Plan first, then do it in a few large, clearly\ndescribed edits rather than dozens of small ones.\n\n1. **Plan.** `bim_model_summary`, `bim_list_linked_models` (the architect's\n   model in, current and lined up), `bim_list_part_types` (levels),\n   `bim_list_views_and_sheets`. Propose the sheet list in plain words and wait\n   for the person to agree. A typical mechanical set (use `P-` and `E-` the\n   same way for plumbing and electrical):\n   - `M-001` legend, general notes and sheet index (`sheet_list` schedule)\n   - `M-101`, `M-102`, … one plan per level (`M-1xx` floor plans; ceiling\n     plans or enlarged plans `M-4xx` when asked)\n   - `M-201` sections (and elevations)\n   - `M-501` details (detail views)\n   - `M-601` schedules (equipment, air devices; plumbing fixtures on `P-601`,\n     panels on `E-601` when the model has them)\n   Number within a discipline in steps the firm uses (101, 102…); never reuse a\n   number. Ask before renaming or deleting someone else's sheet.\n2. **Views.** `bim_create_view` per level (floor plans; crop is fitted to the\n   level's content), sections through what the person wants to show, detail\n   views. Check scales (see drafting guidance below).\n3. **Sheets in one approval.** Build a `batch_documents` of `create_sheet`\n   steps with the title block `fields` (client, address, drawn_by,\n   checked_by, date, status) → `bim_preview_views_sheets_edit` → say \"this\n   creates sheets M-001 … M-601 with these title blocks\" →\n   `bim_apply_views_sheets_edit` with the `preview_token`.\n4. **Place views in one approval**, same way (`place_viewport` steps), then\n   `bim_preview_sheet_layout` per sheet (`fit_scales: true` if a view is too\n   big) → `bim_auto_layout_sheet`. Report anything `could_not_place`.\n5. **Schedules and index.** Activate `mutinybim-schedules`, then\n   `bim_schedule_define` with `place_on` for each schedule (its preview shows\n   the first rows), and put the sheet index on `M-001`.\n6. **Notes and annotation.** `bim_add_sheet_note` (general notes as a numbered\n   list), revision clouds and lines as `add_drawing_item` steps (cloud: two\n   corners in paper mm around the change, on the sheet). Dimensions, tags and\n   details: `mutinybim-drafting`.\n7. **Verify.** `bim_list_views_and_sheets`, then look at every sheet with\n   `bim_render_sheet_image` (zoom with `region_in` on notes, schedules and the\n   title block), then `bim_print_set_pdf`. Fix what you see and look again\n   before asking to issue.\n8. **Issue.** Only when the person says to issue: `bim_set_issue_status`\n   first. Schedules, tags and the sheet index are live: a sheet can change\n   after your print without anyone touching it. `changed_since_print` or\n   `not_printed`: print again, look at those sheets, then check again.\n   Then `bim_issue_set` with `expected_revision` = the `revision` you checked,\n   the issue `name` (e.g. \"Issued for permit\") and `add_revision` (`number` =\n   `next_revision`, date, description). It refuses (`needs_print`) while\n   sheets differ from the last print; pass `accept_changes_since_print: true`\n   only after you have looked at exactly those sheets and the person agrees.\n   If it says the model moved, someone saved since you checked: review, print,\n   and ask again. Never issue a set you didn't print and check. People do the\n   same from Views and sheets › Print set… / Issue set… with the same checks;\n   the host files the issued PDF with the project's issued drawings.\n\nEvery edit above pauses for approval; describe each batch (\"creates 5 sheets\",\n\"places 3 views and arranges M-101 to M-201\") so the person can approve it at\nonce. A refused step names the step and the reason; fix the plan, preview\nagain. Never send a different command with an old `preview_token`.\n\n**Drafting guidance** (general practice; follow the firm's own standards when\nthe person gives them):\n\n- **Scales.** Plans of a whole floor usually 1/8\" = 1'-0\" (96) or 1/4\" (48);\n  enlarged plans 1/4\" to 1/2\"; sections 1/4\" to 1/2\"; details 3/4\" to 3\" or\n  none. Use the largest scale that fits the sheet's drawing area; one floor per\n  sheet unless the person asks for a key plan.\n- **Text.** Note and schedule text at least 3/32\" (about 2.4 mm) printed;\n  titles larger. Never shrink text to make something fit; split it.\n- **Every placed view** has a number and title under it (automatic) and a\n  scale; plans carry a north arrow (automatic).\n- **Title blocks** are filled on every sheet with the same client, address,\n  date and status; checked_by is the engineer, not the agent.\n- **Revisions.** One row per issue, numbered in order, the same on every sheet\n  it issues; cloud what changed since the last issue.\n- **Sheet index** on the first sheet of the discipline, listing every sheet\n  with its latest revision.\n- Don't paste text from code books or published standards into notes; write\n  plain project notes or use the firm's text.\n\n**Isometric of a room or a riser on a sheet.** When a plan and section don't\nmake the work clear (a crowded mechanical room, a riser, a roof curb, a\ncongested ceiling), add a 3D view: `bim_query_elements` on the level → pick the\nids of the equipment and the duct and pipe in the room → `bim_create_3d_view`\nwith `of_elements`, `look_from` the corner that shows the most (usually from the\nopen side, above), and `place_on` the detail or enlarged-plans sheet → read it\nback with `bim_list_views_and_sheets` → `bim_print_sheet_pdf` and check the cut\nedges and that nothing important is hidden; turn it (`bim_set_3d_view_camera`)\nor trim it (`bim_set_3d_crop_box` with `box_ft`) and print again. One 3D view per\nidea; name it after the place (\"Mech room 104 isometric\"). Never use a 3D view\ninstead of the plan the permit needs; it supplements it. A person's working 3D\nview (`working_view_of` in the read, named like `{3D - Name}`) is theirs: never\nplace it on a sheet (refused) or change it; duplicate it if they ask.\n\n**Write or fix general notes:** `bim_list_views_and_sheets` (the sheet, its\nnotes and their `expected_content`) → new block: `bim_add_sheet_note` with\n`paragraphs` and a `width_in` that fits the free space; changing one:\n`bim_edit_text_note` with that note's `expected_content` → on a conflict, read\nagain, merge with the newer words, and propose again.\n\nIf a tool here is missing after turning this family on, say exactly which\ntool is missing; don't substitute another.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_create_view` |\n| `bim_set_view_properties` |\n| `bim_rename_view` |\n| `bim_duplicate_view` |\n| `bim_delete_view` |\n| `bim_create_sheet` |\n| `bim_set_sheet_properties` |\n| `bim_delete_sheet` |\n| `bim_place_viewport` |\n| `bim_edit_viewport` |\n| `bim_preview_sheet_layout` |\n| `bim_auto_layout_sheet` |\n| `bim_set_issue_status` |\n| `bim_add_sheet_note` |\n| `bim_edit_text_note` |\n| `bim_create_3d_view` |\n| `bim_set_3d_view_camera` |\n| `bim_set_3d_crop_box` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-coordination": "---\nname: mutinybim-coordination\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for coordination: browse Autodesk Docs and link, update or unlink models, match levels to a linked model, create and update levels, read and set coordinates (project base point, survey point, true north, working angle), accept or retract a lint finding, and trace rooms. Use with the mutinybim core. Trigger on: linked model, architect's model, Autodesk Docs, levels from the link, coordinates, true north, survey point, accept finding, rooms.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the coordination family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs coordination, or /packs/coordination/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"coordination\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"coordination\"\n  token-cap: \"6000\"\n  tools: \"bim_browse_autodesk_docs bim_link_from_autodesk_docs bim_update_linked_model bim_unlink_linked_model bim_sync_linked_levels bim_update_level bim_create_level bim_read_coordinates bim_preview_coordinates_edit bim_set_project_origin bim_set_survey_point bim_set_true_north bim_set_working_angle bim_lint_accept_finding bim_lint_retract_acceptance bim_trace_rooms\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: linked models, levels and coordinates\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the four starting reads and the rules for every change (preview, say the change, edit with `base_revision`, report the server's\nanswer). `bim_list_linked_models`, `bim_list_levels`, the pictures and\n`bim_preview_part_edit` (which previews `bim_create_level`) are the `model`\nfamily.\n\n## Levels (each needs approval)\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_sync_linked_levels` | Use the architect's levels: one undoable change that adds a level for every storey of the linked model (default: the architectural one) at its height here (units and the link placement applied) and updates the levels made from storeys the architect moved or renamed. A level someone already drew for the same floor is tied to the storey and keeps its name. Never deletes; a second run with nothing new is refused with that reason |\n| `bim_update_level` | Move a level to a new elevation and/or rename it. Everything on it moves with it; plans, schedules and elements that name it follow a rename |\n| `bim_create_level` | Add a level with a name and floor elevation (preview with `bim_preview_part_edit`, `tool: \"bim_create_level\"`) |\n\n## Linked models: bring in, refresh, line up\n\nLinked models are the architect's model, structure, existing MEP and our own\nRevit model, shown under MutinyBIM's work. They belong to the project (no\n`model_id`). These are the Linked models panel's actions (Manage › Link a\nmodel, and the panel's row menu). Everyone on the project sees the result.\nRead them first with `bim_list_linked_models` (model family).\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_browse_autodesk_docs` | List a folder of the project's Autodesk Docs project, or search by name. Each file says `can_link` and `why_not`, and `already_linked_as`. Needs edit access and the person's Autodesk sign-in (Settings) |\n| `bim_link_from_autodesk_docs` (approval) | Bring up to 25 Docs files in as linked models, each with a `label` people see and a `discipline`. A file already linked adds a new version. Returns `queued`: Autodesk exports and converts in the background |\n| `bim_update_linked_model` (approval) | One `action` on one linked model: `check_for_new_version` / `relink_from_docs` (Docs files), `line_up` (place a Docs file again against our Revit model as it is now), `reexport_project_model` (export our own Revit model again; no `reference_id`), `retry_conversion` (a failed version), `show_version` (`version_id`), `adjust_position` (`east_ft`, `north_ft`, `up_ft`, `rotation_deg` counter-clockwise; give only what changes) |\n| `bim_unlink_linked_model` (approval) | Take a linked model out for everyone. Its files and versions are kept. Say what views stop drawing it before you ask |\n\nResults are `saved` (done now), `queued` (the linked-models worker finishes it,\nusually in a few minutes: read `bim_list_linked_models` again to see it\n`ready`), `unchanged` (nothing to do, e.g. already the newest Docs version) or\n`refused` with the reason in the person's words (no edit access, no Autodesk\nsign-in, a file Autodesk can't open). Say `queued` as \"started\", never as done.\nUploading an IFC file from the person's computer stays in the browser (Manage ›\nLink a model › Upload IFC): tell them where to click.\n\n## Linked models in a view\n\nEach view says how every linked model (the architect's model, a consultant's\nMEP model) draws in it, so MutinyBIM's own work reads clearly. Set it with\n`bim_set_view_properties` (`mutinybim-views-sheets`)\n`linked_models: { <linked model id>: { ... } }`; linked models you leave out\nkeep their setting. Read it back from `bim_list_views_and_sheets`\n(`linked_models` on each view, with the `display` it matches or `custom`).\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `display` | `normal` (full colour), `halftone` (grey linework on paper; grey and 30% see-through in 3D for architecture), `transparent` (grey, 60% see-through in 3D), `underlay` (screened lighter on paper, faint beneath our work in 3D, never hides it), `hidden` (not drawn or printed) |\n| `transparency_pct` | 3D only, 0–90. Combine with a `display` or change it alone |\n| `categories` | Per IFC category of the linked model, e.g. `{\"IFCDOOR\": {\"visible\": false}, \"IFCWALL\": {\"halftone\": false}}`; `null` clears one exception |\n| `cut_line_weight` / `beyond_line_weight` | Drawings: the pen (thin, medium, wide, heavy) for what the cut passes through (walls; default wide) and for what is seen below the cut (furniture, slab edges; default thin). `null` goes back to the default. Kept when you change `display` |\n\nTwo view-wide settings simplify every linked model on paper (also on\n`bim_set_view_properties`): `linked_hidden_lines` (default on) removes linked\nlines covered by something nearer, such as a chair tucked under a table or a\nwall behind the one a section cuts; `linked_min_object_in` leaves out linked\nfurniture, fixtures and other non-cut things smaller than that across (cups,\noutlets). Walls are always drawn. When a person says the architecture is\n\"busy\", \"I can see chairs through the tables\" or \"too much furniture\", check\n`linked_hidden_lines` is on, offer `linked_min_object_in` (6 or 12), lighten\nwith `beyond_line_weight: \"thin\"`, and show a picture.\n\nDefaults with nothing set: drawings (plans, sections, sheets, PDFs, pictures)\ndraw every linked model halftone; 3D draws architecture halftone and 30%\nsee-through and MEP models in colour. A person's **working 3D view**\n(`{3D - <name>}`, `working_view_of` in the listing) holds their own 3D\nsetting; change it only when they ask. In 3D, our own elements win a click\nover a halftone or see-through linked model even when it is in front.\nPictures (`bim_render_view_image`, `bim_render_sheet_image`) and\n`bim_print_view_pdf` draw the linked plan under the view's work with these\nsettings; `linked_models_missing` names any linked model that could not be cut.\n\nWhen a person says \"make the architect's model lighter\", \"I can't see my\nducts\", or \"ghost the architecture\", set `display: \"transparent\"` (or\n`halftone`) on the view they are looking at, then show a picture.\n\n## Coordinates: base point, survey point, true north, working angle\n\nModel coordinates (x east, y **plan north**, z up) are where geometry and\nlinked models sit. The **project base point** carries the project's site\ncoordinates (N/S, E/W, elevation) and true north; the **survey point** is fixed\nto the site; the **internal origin** is model 0, 0, 0 and never moves.\nChanging coordinates never moves geometry, views or linked models — only what\npoints read on the site.\n\nOn screen people see plain names: **Project origin** (the base point), **Site\npoint** (the survey point), **Model zero** (the internal origin). Use those\nwords with them; keep the tool argument names as they are.\n\nThe **working angle** is the direction people draw in, for a building that\nsits skewed on the model axes: degrees counter-clockwise from model east, kept\nin (−45, 45]. Route snaps (square and 45°), the placement grid, drafting\northo/polar steps and **new** 3D crop boxes follow it. It is not true north\nand never changes site coordinates or moves anything. The model has one; a\nview may carry its own.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_read_coordinates` | Read the base point and survey point (model feet and site N/S, E/W, elevation), the internal origin, true north and the placement an IFC export carries |\n| `bim_preview_coordinates_edit` | Dry run of any edit below: what would change, or why it would be refused |\n| `bim_set_project_origin` (approval) | `mode: \"move_point\"` moves only the base point on the model (`position`); nothing's site coordinates change. `mode: \"relocate_project\"` moves the building on the site: give the base point's new site coordinates (`shared`) and/or a new `position`; every element's site coordinates change by the same move and the survey point stays on the site |\n| `bim_set_survey_point` (approval) | The site point. `mode: \"move_point\"` moves only its marker on the model (`position`); nothing's site coordinates change. `mode: \"relocate_project\"` gives it the site coordinates it should read (`shared`, e.g. the civil benchmark; optionally a new `position`): the site moves under the building, every element's site coordinates change, geometry stays put |\n| `bim_set_true_north` (approval) | `angle_deg` clockwise from plan north to true north, or two plan points `from_ft` → `to_ft` along true north. Turns the building on the site about the base point |\n| `bim_set_working_angle` (approval) | Line the working axes up with a skewed building: `angle_deg`, or two plan points `from_ft` → `to_ft` along a wall or slab edge (the axes turn square to it). `view` sets it for one view only; `view` + `use_model_angle: true` drops that view's own angle. Nothing moves. Read it back with `bim_read_coordinates` (`working_angle_deg`, `views_with_own_working_angle`) |\n\nTrue north turns true-north plans (`orientation: true_north` on the view), the\n3D view's compass dial and each sheet's north arrow; plans left on plan north do not\nmove. A plan set to `orientation: \"working_angle\"` (`bim_set_view_properties`)\ndraws turned so the building's walls run square to the paper; its north arrow\nstill points at true north. A 3D crop box turns to the working angle with\n`bim_set_3d_crop_box` `align: \"working_angle\"`, or square to an edge with\n`align: { from_ft, to_ft }`; its size is kept. There is no rotate handle on the\nbox: these (or `box_ft.turn_deg`) are how it turns. Ask which mode the person means before moving the base point: \"move just\nthe marker\" and \"put the building at these site coordinates\" are different\nedits.\n\n## Coordination findings\n\nThe coordination lint itself (`bim_lint_configure`, `bim_lint_check`, the\n`lint` block on every edit, `bim_status_snapshot`) is in the `mutinybim`\ncore. Accepting a finding, and taking an acceptance back, are here because\nthey are rare and always the person's decision.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_lint_accept_finding` (approval) | Record that a finding is acceptable, with the person's reason in their words. `finding_id` is a finding's `id` from `bim_lint_check` run now; any other id (made up, from an old check, a rule name) is refused as `lint_finding_not_current`, so run `bim_lint_check` first and copy the id. A refused id comes back refused before any Approve card shows; tell the person, don't retry it. It stops showing for everyone, people and agents, until one of its elements changes |\n| `bim_lint_retract_acceptance` (approval) | Take an acceptance back with the person's reason: one made by mistake, or one that is no longer true. The finding shows again; who accepted it, who retracted it and why are kept. `lint_acceptance_not_found` lists what is accepted now. People do the same with Retract… in Manage › Coordination |\n\nNever call either tool to test or inspect it: each one saves for the whole\nproject once approved.\n\n## Rooms from the architect's plan\n\nWhen the model has no rooms to measure (receptacle proposals in\n`mutinybim-electrical` need them), trace them from the plan the person points\nto, after they approve.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_trace_rooms` (approval) | Add rooms the proposers can measure, from the architect's plan: `level`, `rooms [{ number, name, outline_ft }]`, `openings [{ kind door\\|window, at_ft, width_ft }]` (centre on a room edge), `casework [{ type_name, at_ft, size_ft }]` (counters, \"Kitchen island countertop\", sinks, ranges, lavatories, washers). A wall goes on every room edge. Trace what the plan shows; ask when a dimension is missing |\n\n## Workflows\n\n**Square the work to a skewed building:** `bim_read_coordinates` → pick a long\nstraight wall or slab edge (read two of its plan points from the model or the\nperson) → `bim_preview_coordinates_edit` with `tool: \"bim_set_working_angle\"`\nand `from_ft` / `to_ft` → say the angle → `bim_set_working_angle` → optionally\n`bim_set_view_properties` with `orientation: \"working_angle\"` on the plans\nthey draw in, and `bim_set_3d_crop_box` `align: \"working_angle\"` on an existing\n3D view. Never change true north for this: true north is the real compass.\n\n**Set the site coordinates from a survey:** `bim_read_coordinates` →\n`bim_preview_coordinates_edit` with `tool: \"bim_set_project_origin\"`,\n`mode: \"relocate_project\"` and the surveyed `shared` values → say what every\npoint's coordinates become → `bim_set_project_origin` → `bim_set_true_north`\nif the survey gives a bearing → read again and report. When the survey ties\nthe site to a benchmark rather than the base point, put the benchmark on the\nsurvey point instead: `bim_set_survey_point` with `mode: \"relocate_project\"`\nand its `shared` values.\n\n**Bring in the architect's model:** `bim_list_linked_models` → if it is not\nthere, `bim_browse_autodesk_docs` (search by name, e.g. \"arch\") → tell the\nperson which file you'd link and why (newest, `can_link`) →\n`bim_link_from_autodesk_docs` with a clear `label` and `discipline:\n\"architecture\"` → say it started → `bim_list_linked_models` until it is\n`ready` (a few minutes; tell the person rather than waiting silently). If it\nis there but `alignment` is not placed, follow `fix`: `reexport_project_model`\nthen `line_up`, or ask the person how far it is off and `adjust_position`. If\nDocs has a newer version, `check_for_new_version`. A failed version: say the\n`error`, then `retry_conversion` only if the person agrees. Then levels from\nthe building (below), and look at a plan with `bim_render_view_image` to check\nthe architecture sits under the work.\n\n**Levels from the building:** `bim_list_levels` → tell the person the storeys\nand what syncing does to each (new, moved, tied to their level) →\n`bim_sync_linked_levels` → `bim_list_levels` again and report the levels. For a\nlevel at a surface the person names (\"the roof is 26'-6\\\"\"), use\n`bim_create_level`; to move or rename one, `bim_update_level` with its id.\nPeople do the same in Manage › Levels (Add level, Pick a surface, Architect's\nlevels) and by dragging a level line in a section.\n\nEquipment that stands on the architect's roof or slab, and the clearance zones\naround it, are set with `bim_set_part_host` and `bim_set_clearances` in the\n`mutinybim-parts` pack.\n\nIf a tool here is missing after turning this family on, say exactly which\ntool is missing; don't substitute another.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_browse_autodesk_docs` |\n| `bim_link_from_autodesk_docs` |\n| `bim_update_linked_model` |\n| `bim_unlink_linked_model` |\n| `bim_sync_linked_levels` |\n| `bim_update_level` |\n| `bim_create_level` |\n| `bim_read_coordinates` |\n| `bim_preview_coordinates_edit` |\n| `bim_set_project_origin` |\n| `bim_set_survey_point` |\n| `bim_set_true_north` |\n| `bim_set_working_angle` |\n| `bim_lint_accept_finding` |\n| `bim_lint_retract_acceptance` |\n| `bim_trace_rooms` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-groups-phases": "---\nname: mutinybim-groups-phases\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for groups, phases and typed elements: create, update, lock and delete groups, assign elements, the active group and phase, phases and phase filters, a view's phase and groups, and the class, type and values of typed elements (walls, slabs, beams, rooms). Use with the mutinybim core. Trigger on: group, lock, phase, existing, demolition, new construction, phase filter, element type, element values.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the groups-phases family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs groups-phases, or /packs/groups-phases/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"groups_phases\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"groups-phases\"\n  token-cap: \"5200\"\n  tools: \"bim_list_groups_and_phases bim_preview_organization_edit bim_create_group bim_update_group bim_delete_group bim_assign_to_group bim_set_active_group_and_phase bim_create_phase bim_update_phase bim_set_element_phases bim_set_view_organization bim_set_phase_filter bim_delete_phase bim_delete_phase_filter bim_set_element\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: groups and phases\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the four starting reads and the rules for every change (preview, say the change, edit with `base_revision`, report the server's\nanswer). The pictures and printing are the `model` family.\n\nEvery MutinyBIM element (duct, pipe, part, wall…) is in one **group** and is\n**built in** one **phase**, and may be **demolished in** a later one. Elements\nwith no group are in General; with no phase, New construction. Built-in\ngroups: General, Existing to remain, New work, Demolition, Coordination.\nBuilt-in phases: Existing, New construction. A view shows the model as of its\n**phase** through its **phase filter** (Show all, Show new, Show existing +\ndemo, Show complete), and shows / halftones / hides each group. Views, sheets,\nlevels and types have no group or phase.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_list_groups_and_phases` (read) | Groups (locked?, default visibility), phases in time order, phase filters, the active group and phase, and each view's phase, filter and group visibility. `include_counts` adds elements per group (reads the whole model) |\n| `bim_preview_organization_edit` (read) | Run one groups/phases kernel command without saving: what would change or the reason it would be refused |\n| `bim_create_group` / `bim_update_group` / `bim_delete_group` (approval) | Add, rename, recolour, set default visibility, **lock / unlock** (on its own call), delete (`move_to` when it has elements; General can't be deleted) |\n| `bim_assign_to_group` (approval) | Put `element_ids` (a run, a branch, a box selection) in a group |\n| `bim_set_active_group_and_phase` (approval) | Where new work goes: the active group, or the phase new elements are built in (one per call) |\n| `bim_create_phase` / `bim_update_phase` (approval) | Add a phase (`after` a phase, `null` = first, absent = last), rename, or move it in time |\n| `bim_set_element_phases` (approval) | `built_in` / `demolished_in` (null = not demolished), or `action: \"demolish\"` (in `phase`, default the active one) / `\"restore\"` |\n| `bim_set_view_organization` (approval) | A view's `phase`, `phase_filter`, and `groups` (name → show / halftone / hide; other groups keep theirs). Reaches the canvas, printed drawing, sheets and PDF |\n| `bim_set_phase_filter` (approval) | Create a filter (name + all four of new, existing, demolished, temporary) or change one; each is show, halftone, dashed or hide |\n| `bim_set_element` (approval) | **Typed elements**, one change per call on walls, slabs, openings, rooms, grids, columns, beams and components (see below): `class`, `type_id`, `values`, `place`, `lock` / `lock: false`, or `new_type`. A part takes its class and its add-on parameters (`<pack>.<name>`) here too |\n| `bim_delete_phase` / `bim_delete_phase_filter` (approval) | Remove a phase or phase filter nothing uses; otherwise refused with what uses it (elements, views, the default phase, the last one) |\n\n**Locked groups.** Nothing in a locked group can be created, changed, moved,\ndemolished or deleted, by anyone, agents included; an edit that would touch\none (a part move that would push a locked run, too) is refused and nothing\nchanges. Report the refusal word for word and ask the person whether to unlock\nthe group. Never unlock a group yourself unless the person asked for exactly\nthat, and never route around a lock.\n\n## Workflows\n\n**Demolition plan:** `bim_list_groups_and_phases` → `bim_set_element_phases`\nwith `built_in: \"Existing\"` on what was there before the project, then\n`action: \"demolish\"` on what is removed (default: the active phase) →\n`bim_create_view` (or reuse one; `mutinybim-views-sheets`) →\n`bim_set_view_organization` with `phase_filter: \"Show existing + demo\"`:\ndemolished work prints dashed, new work is hidden → sheet and print as usual.\n\n**Organise work by group:** `bim_inspect_network` or `bim_query_elements` for\nthe run's ids → `bim_assign_to_group` → `bim_set_view_organization` with\n`groups` to hide or halftone it per view. Lock a finished group only when the\nperson asks.\n\n**Typical unit or bay:** put one unit's elements in their own group\n(`bim_create_group` → `bim_assign_to_group`), then `bim_update_group` with\n`typical { count, columns, spacing_ft: { x, y }, mark? }` (count includes the\noriginal; negative `y` goes down the plan) on its own call. Copies draw on\nplans and count in every trade's totals (drain fixture units, service,\nmeter, project), once per copy. `typical: null` stops repeating. General\ncan't be a typical.\n\n**Contract cap on typicals:** `bim_set_active_group_and_phase` with\n`typical_cap` alone sets how many typical types the contract covers;\n`bim_list_groups_and_phases` reads `typicals { count, contract_covers }`.\nMore typicals than the cap is a coordination warning, not a refusal: say it\nand let the person decide. A finding id like `<group>#<mark>/<element>` is\ncopy `<mark>` of that group: explain it as \"copy 002 of Unit A\" and point at\n`<element>`.\n\nIf a tool here is missing after turning this family on, say exactly which\ntool is missing; don't substitute another.\n\n## Typed elements (`bim_set_element`)\n\nBuilding and structure elements carry what the engineer says about them, the\nsame way in every trade. Preview with `bim_preview_part_edit`\n(`tool: \"bim_set_element\"`), say the change, then call it (one Approve card).\nGive `element_ids` (from `bim_query_elements`) and exactly one of:\n\n- `class`: a class id from the model's registry (`arch.wall`, `struct.beam`,\n  an add-on's or the firm's class; `bim_schedule_fields` in\n  `mutinybim-schedules` lists them). `null` clears it so the class is\n  recognised from the element again. A class that doesn't admit the kind is\n  refused with the reason.\n- `type_id`: a shared type (`null` clears it). Make one first with\n  `new_type: { name, class?, for_kinds? }` (no `element_ids`); its id comes\n  back in the saved answer. Type values read under the element's own.\n- `values`: by parameter key. A plain value goes as given; `{ \"value\": 1.3935456,\n  \"unit\": \"m2/occ\" }` converts to the unit the parameter stores and is refused,\n  in words, when the key has no definition for that element's class or the\n  unit is of the wrong kind. `null` removes a value. On a part, only add-on\n  parameters (`<pack>.<name>`) go here; its type's parameters stay with\n  `bim_set_parameters`.\n- `place`: geometry in plan feet on a `level`: `point` (one point, with\n  `rotation_deg`), `line` (start, end: walls, beams, grids), `path` (two or\n  more), `area` (three or more: slabs, rooms); `z_ft` above the level.\n  `null` clears it.\n- `lock: { state: \"existing\" | \"issued\", reason }` stops anyone changing the\n  elements until `lock: false`. Lock only when the person asks, and quote\n  their reason.\n\n`bim_query_elements` reads it back: `class`, `type`, `value.<key>` (in the\nparameter's stored unit) and `locked`. Built-in building parameters use\nnamespaced keys: `arch.fire_resistance` (hours) on walls, `arch.area_per_occupant`\non rooms, `arch.fire_rating` on doors, `struct.camber` on beams; the bare names\nstill read. Add-on classes bring their own `<pack>.<name>` parameters with units.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_list_groups_and_phases` |\n| `bim_preview_organization_edit` |\n| `bim_create_group` |\n| `bim_update_group` |\n| `bim_delete_group` |\n| `bim_assign_to_group` |\n| `bim_set_active_group_and_phase` |\n| `bim_create_phase` |\n| `bim_update_phase` |\n| `bim_set_element_phases` |\n| `bim_set_view_organization` |\n| `bim_set_phase_filter` |\n| `bim_delete_phase` |\n| `bim_delete_phase_filter` |\n| `bim_set_element` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-parts": "---\nname: mutinybim-parts\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for part definitions: draft a part from a cut sheet, a complete example definition, check and save definitions, read a saved part, the firm's part library, clearance zones, the slab or roof a part stands on, and mounting planes. Use with the mutinybim core. Trigger on: new part, cut sheet, part definition, firm library, clearance, working space, host, curb, mounting plane.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the parts family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs parts, or /packs/parts/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"parts\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"parts\"\n  token-cap: \"4500\"\n  tools: \"bim_draft_part_from_cut_sheet bim_read_picture_rows bim_part_definition_example bim_check_part_definition bim_read_part_definition bim_save_part_definition bim_list_firm_parts bim_save_firm_part bim_set_clearances bim_set_part_host bim_set_mounting_plane\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: make and change parts (part editor)\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the four starting reads and the rules for every change (preview, say the change, edit with `base_revision`, report the server's\nanswer), and a part script (`parts.part(…)`, then `bim_save_part_type`) is\nthe first way to make a part. `bim_list_part_types`, `bim_place_part` and\n`bim_preview_part_edit` (which previews `bim_save_part_definition` too) are\nthe `model` family.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_part_definition_example` | Get a complete, valid definition to start from (`rooftop_unit` or `floor_drain`) and a field guide. No approval. **Read this before writing a definition**: it is the reference for every field the check and save tools take |\n| `bim_check_part_definition` | Dry run: every problem in plain words (per size), overall size, connector positions and sizes, plan / front / side SVG previews. Saves nothing, no approval. A definition whose fields don't fit is answered with each field and what is wrong with it. Warnings (they don't block saving) flag a connector below the base, facing into the body, or inside a solid piece (each feature's bounding box, cuts not subtracted), with how deep: put connectors on a face, pointing out. Openings at the bottom of a hollow curb, or on the cabinet's bottom looking down into it, are fine |\n| `bim_read_part_definition` | Open a saved part the way Edit part does: a type id or a placed part's id in, `part_name` + `definition` out in exactly the form check and save take, with its sizes and `placed_count`. No approval |\n| `bim_save_part_definition` (approval) | Save a modeled part (every size in its types table) to the project library; placed parts of those types update. Refused if a run is connected to a connector it would remove or move |\n| `bim_draft_part_from_cut_sheet` | Read a cut sheet PDF uploaded to the project (`file_id` from `search_content`, or a storage link to it) and draft a rooftop unit, air terminal or water heater: the sheet's text by page, every fact found with its page and line, the assumptions made where the sheet is silent, and a checked definition (sizes, connector positions). Saves nothing, no approval |\n| `bim_read_picture_rows` | Read an equipment schedule from a picture or a PDF on the project into planned equipment rows: each row with its page, the values the add-ons' columns read, and unsure cells marked \"check this\". The first call saves nothing; commit with the rows the person kept, `type_id`, `base_revision`, `preview_token` and `user_confirmed` (pauses on the Approve card; one undo step) |\n| `bim_list_firm_parts` | Parts in the firm library (every project can place them): each size's type id, revision, where it came from. No approval |\n| `bim_set_mounting_plane` | Make a mounting plane (`level_id`, `name`, `height_above_level_ft`, two corners) to place air devices on where there is no ceiling, or change one (`plane_id`): a new height moves the parts on it and their duct follows. `bim_list_levels` lists each level's planes |\n| `bim_save_firm_part` (approval) | Save a modeled definition to the firm library. Same model id = the next revision of that part, not a second one. Decided by the host's firm library, not the model server |\n\nA part definition is parameters + a **model**: reference planes positioned by\nparameters, sketches on those planes (rectangles, lines, arcs, circles,\npolylines; corners locked to planes with `on_plane`), and features in order\n(`extrude` or `revolve`, each `join` = solid, `cut` = void, or `intersect`).\nRows of `model.types` are the sizes. Lengths are **millimetres** in the\npart's own frame (x = the way it faces, y = its left, z = up from the\ninsertion point); formulas accept `12in` / `2ft` and `if, min, max, abs,\nsqrt, round, floor, ceil, sin, cos, tan`. A parameter with a `formula` is\ncomputed. Connectors: `domain` duct / pipe / electrical, `systemKind`,\n`position` (dimensions), outward `direction`, `shape` with sizes, `flow`,\n`flowDirection` in / out / bidirectional, `widthAxis` for rectangles.\n\n## Workflows\n\n**Make a part from a cut sheet PDF** (e.g. \"here's the RTU cut sheet, make\nthe part and put it on the roof\", \"build this diffuser from its submittal\",\n\"add this water heater to the firm library\"):\n1. Find the PDF on the project (`search_content`, or the file the person\n   attached) and note its `file_id`. If it isn't uploaded, ask them to add it\n   to the project; a link to somewhere else is refused.\n2. `bim_draft_part_from_cut_sheet` with `project_id` and `file_id` (add\n   `kind` if the sheet's title doesn't say rooftop unit, diffuser / grille or\n   water heater). Read `facts` against `sheet_text`: overall length, width,\n   height (or diameter), curb height, each duct opening's size and where its\n   centre is, pipe connection sizes, voltage / phase / MCA / MOP / FLA,\n   capacity, weight, manufacturer, model.\n3. Say the `assumptions` to the person (e.g. curb wall 2\" not on the sheet)\n   and ask about any that change a shape or a connection. A cut sheet with\n   several models in one table: draft once, then add one `model.types` row\n   per model with that model's numbers from `sheet_text`.\n4. Change the `definition` where the sheet says more than the draft caught\n   (another connection, an opening on the side), then\n   `bim_check_part_definition` until `status: ok`; compare each size's\n   overall size and connector positions with the sheet and say them.\n5. Save where the person wants it, each pausing for approval:\n   - this project only: preview with `bim_preview_part_edit`\n     (`tool: \"bim_save_part_definition\"`), then `bim_save_part_definition`;\n   - every project: `bim_save_firm_part` (same `part_name` and `definition`).\n6. To place a firm-library part: `bim_list_firm_parts` → a size's `type_id`\n   → `bim_place_part` with `from_firm_library: true` (preview it the same\n   way). Placing it adds the part to this project's library too.\n7. Not drafted (`status: not_drafted`, e.g. a fan coil, a scanned sheet with\n   no text, no overall size found): write the definition by hand from\n   `sheet_text` with the workflow below. Never invent a size the sheet\n   doesn't give: ask.\n\n**Make a part from a description or a spec sheet** (e.g. \"a rooftop unit\n90 x 50 x 40 in on a 14 in curb, supply and return on the bottom, 208 V\n3-phase\", or a floor drain from a submittal):\n1. Read the numbers you need: overall sizes per model/size, connection sizes\n   and where they are, system, airflow / GPM, voltage and phase, weight,\n   manufacturer and model. Ask for anything missing that changes the shape\n   or a connection; never invent a connection size.\n2. `bim_part_definition_example` (closest example) → copy its shape.\n3. Parameters: one per size that varies (length, width, height, connection\n   sizes) plus schedule data (manufacturer, model, airflow, cooling, voltage,\n   phase, weight). Things derived from others get a `formula`.\n4. Reference planes on every face a sketch or connector must follow\n   (`front` = length / 2, `left` = width / 2, a top at height…). Lock sketch\n   corners to them with `on_plane`, so every size flexes correctly.\n5. Features: the body as an `extrude` (or `revolve` about a construction\n   line for round bodies), curbs as a rectangle with an inner rectangle\n   (a hole), openings and recesses as `cut` features after the solids.\n6. Connectors on the faces, positions as dimensions/formulas, sizes bound to\n   parameters, direction pointing out of the part.\n7. `model.types`: one row per size from the spec sheet.\n8. `bim_check_part_definition` → fix every issue → check again until\n   `status: ok`. Look at the previews and the per-size overall sizes and\n   connector positions against the spec; say them to the person.\n9. Preview with `bim_preview_part_edit` (`tool: \"bim_save_part_definition\"`)\n   → describe → `bim_save_part_definition` (approval). The sizes become types\n   `<model id>.<size>` for `bim_place_part`.\n10. People can open the same part in the MutinyBIM editor: **Place part → Edit\n    part** (pencil on the type), or select a placed part → **Edit\n    definition**. New parts by hand: **Place part → New part** (it opens on\n    the empty part's reference planes in 3D, with **Start with a box**). In\n    Plan / Front / Side they click a dimension between planes to type its\n    length or link it to a parameter; **Save to firm library** puts it in the\n    firm library. Firm-library parts show in Place part marked \"firm library\".\n\n**Change a part that is already in the model** (e.g. \"add the 12.5 ton\nsize\", \"move the return to the side\", \"the curb is 18 in\"): `bim_read_part_definition`\nwith the type id or a placed unit's id → change only what was asked in its\n`definition` → `bim_check_part_definition` → say what changes and that the\n`placed_count` placed parts update → preview and `bim_save_part_definition`\nwith the same `part_name`. Never redraw it from an example: that makes a\nsecond part. A refusal about a connector means a run is connected where the\nchange would move or remove it; say which.\n\n## What a part stands on and the space it needs\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_set_clearances` (approval) | Clearance zones: `scope: \"type\"` with `zones` (all of the type's zones, feet: `depth_ft`, optional `width_ft`, `height_ft`, `offset_ft` to slide off centre, `from_floor`), or `scope: \"instance\"` with `overrides` by zone id for one placed part (`off: true`, new sizes, `null` = back to the type; a full zone adds one). `working_space` sizes NEC 110.26 working space from Table 110.26(A)(1) for the `voltage_to_ground` and `condition` you chose (`extra_depth_multiplier: 2` for a noted double clearance); the edition is the project's unless you give `nec_edition`. `dedicated_space` adds the NEC 110.26(E) dedicated space above a panel or switchboard (equipment width and depth, 6 ft up or to structure, no ducts or pipes of other trades); at `scope: \"instance\"` give `suspended_ceiling_ft` / `structure_ft` only as the engineer states them, never guessed |\n| `bim_set_part_host` (approval) | What parts stand on: `slab_id` (ours) or `linked` (model id, name, `top_ft`, GlobalId if known), with `curb_in`. Default `seat`: each part moves onto the top plus curb and carries its connected runs exactly like a move (a riser stretches or shrinks, level runs move whole). If a run can't follow, nothing saves and the refusal names the part, the height change (mm and in), the run piece and why: then move part and run together with `bim_move_elements` and set the host with `seat: false`, disconnect, or re-route. Don't disconnect first just to seat. `remove_host` takes it off |\n\nZones (service access, combustibles, economizer access, working space, keep\nclear) sit against one face of the part (front = the way it faces), move and\nturn with it, draw dashed, and are **never solid**. A cut sheet's service\nclearances go on the type; a site condition (a wall closer on one side) is an\ninstance override. The coordination lint's `clearance-intrusion` names\nanything inside a zone: errors for working space and combustibles. Never move\nequipment or runs to clear a zone without saying so first; propose the move.\n\nA rooftop unit should **stand on** the roof: `bim_set_part_host` with the\nlinked roof (its name and top are in the lint block's `near.host` after\nplacing; the roof level from `bim_list_levels` is the fallback) and the curb\nheight from the cut sheet. `floating-equipment` then checks it against that\nhost: floating above the curb, the host deleted (\"nothing under it\"), or the\narchitect's roof moved since. People get the host automatically when they\nplace a unit by pointing at the roof; Properties › Stands on shows it.\n\n**Rooftop unit with service clearance, from the cut sheet:** draft and save\nthe part (above) → `bim_set_clearances` `scope: \"type\"` with the sheet's\nservice, economizer and combustible clearances → `bim_place_part` on the roof\n→ `bim_set_part_host` with the linked roof and curb → read the lint block:\nfix any `clearance-intrusion` or `floating-equipment` before the next edit.\nA seat or move that would pull a straight duct or pipe piece back past its\nother end is refused (\"doubles back on … where they join\"): move less, or\nre-route.\n\n**Panel working space:** `bim_set_clearances` on the placed panel with\n`working_space` (voltage to ground: 120 for 120/208 V, 277 for 277/480 V; the\ncondition from what faces it) → say the depth, width and height it returned\nand the code citation → read the lint block for what is inside.\n\nIf a tool here is missing after turning this family on, say exactly which\ntool is missing; don't substitute another.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_draft_part_from_cut_sheet` |\n| `bim_read_picture_rows` |\n| `bim_part_definition_example` |\n| `bim_check_part_definition` |\n| `bim_read_part_definition` |\n| `bim_save_part_definition` |\n| `bim_list_firm_parts` |\n| `bim_save_firm_part` |\n| `bim_set_clearances` |\n| `bim_set_part_host` |\n| `bim_set_mounting_plane` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-calcs": "---\nname: mutinybim-calcs\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for calculations: list and run code calculations against the model, duct size advice from airflow, a duct system's pressure report, a pipe system's hydraulics report, fitting coefficient overrides, a pipe system's code sizing proposal, and service size from an equipment list. Use with the mutinybim core. Trigger on: calculation, calc, duct size, pressure drop, static pressure, external static, hydraulics, fixture pressure, size pipes, gas sizing, coefficient, service size.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the calcs family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs calcs, or /packs/calcs/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"calcs\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"calcs\"\n  token-cap: \"3700\"\n  tools: \"bim_list_calcs bim_run_calc bim_suggest_duct_size bim_pressure_report bim_pipe_report bim_set_fitting_coefficient_override bim_size_pipes bim_service_from_equipment_list\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: engineering calcs with code citations\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first for the model\nreads; `bim_preview_route_edit` is the `model` family. Calcs are read-only: nothing is saved and nothing needs approval. Two\nedits live here, each behind the person's approval:\n`bim_set_fitting_coefficient_override` (preview it with `bim_preview_route_edit`)\nand `bim_size_pipes`, which applies a gas, domestic water, sanitary, vent or\nstorm system's sizing proposal.\n\nWire, breaker, ground and conduit sizes, voltage drop, NEC demand loads, duct\nfriction and fan static pressure, and pipe friction and hydraulics, all run\nthrough one calc interface. Every value comes back with the clause or method\nit rests on (`NEC 2023 Table 310.16, copper 90°C column`, `NEC 2020 250.122(B)`,\n`Chapter 9, Table 9`). Nothing is saved.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_list_calcs` (read) | See every calc: id, what it does, whether it reads a model, a worked example input and the input schema. Filter with `domain` (`electrical`, `mechanical`, `plumbing`) |\n| `bim_set_fitting_coefficient_override` (approval) | Record an engineer's own loss coefficient on one fitting (with their name and time); it wins over tables and formulas. `c = null` clears it |\n| `bim_run_calc` (read) | Run one calc with `project_id`, `calc`, `inputs`; `model_id` for calcs that read a model. The code edition is `nec_edition` / `plumbing_edition` / `fuel_gas_edition` (the same fields `bim_size_pipes` takes), else the project's Code Study, else `state`. Returns values with units and citations, the steps in plain language, issues, and `run_key` (the same request always gives the same answer) |\n| `bim_size_pipes` (edit, approval) | Apply the sizes the system's calc proposes for one system (`system_id`): gas by `plumbing.gas_system`, domestic water, sanitary, vent and storm by `plumbing.size_system`, recomputed at the approved revision: undersized pipe goes up (`allow_smaller` also reduces oversized), reducers where sizes change, one undo. `fuel_gas_edition` / `plumbing_edition` / `state` only when Code Study names no code |\n| `bim_service_from_equipment_list` (read) | `text` (pasted schedule, CSV, or text out of a PDF), `voltage_v`, `phases`: every row with its VA, how it was read and a suggested load class, then the service size with the Article 220 trail. Rows are numbered by line in the pasted text: non-blank lines from 1, the header counted, so the first data row of a CSV is line 2 (`header_line`, `lines_note`). Send the person's corrections as `classes [{ line, class }]` by those numbers; a line with no equipment row is refused with the valid lines listed, and a class they set reads `class_why: \"set by you\"`. `required_a` is the minimum ampacity: noncontinuous plus 125% of continuous (215.2(A)(1)(a) / 230.42(A)(1)); lighting and fixed electric space heating (424.4(B)) count as continuous, the heating demand taken at 100% (220.51) before the 125%. `service` / `service_a` is the next standard rating at or above it, an early selection, not the whole-building calculation: report both. Structured rows: `bim_run_calc` `nec.service_size` |\n| `bim_pressure_report` | Read a system's pressure: velocity, friction and fitting losses by section, the critical path, and the fan's required external static pressure. Coefficients carry their source; formula values say \"verify for permit work\" |\n| `bim_suggest_duct_size` (read) | The smallest standard duct for `airflow_cfm` (or what `element_id` carries) at the system's friction rate and velocity limit, with its velocity and friction; rectangular also gives the narrowest width at `height_in` (or the segment's depth) and the least-sheet-metal size. `check_width_in` / `check_height_in` / `check_diameter_in` reports a size you have in mind. Changes nothing |\n| `bim_pipe_report` | Water: gpm, velocity, friction, fitting K, pressure at every fixture and the critical one. Drainage: fixture units per pipe and stack, capacity at slope. Gas: cfh, drop, longest run. Built-in values say \"verify for permit work\" |\n\nCalcs: `nec.size_circuit` (branch circuit or feeder: device, conductor, voltage\ndrop upsizing, EGC grown in proportion, conduit), `nec.demand_load` (Article 220\ncalculated load at a panel or service), `nec.conductor_ampacity`,\n`nec.voltage_drop`, `nec.egc`, `nec.conduit_fill`, `mech.duct_friction`,\n`mech.duct_system_pressure`, `plumbing.water_friction`, `plumbing.pipe_system`.\nThe duct and pipe system calcs give the same numbers as `bim_pressure_report`\nand `bim_pipe_report` (below).\n\n- **NEC edition.** The project's adopted electrical code from its Code Study\n  is used; else pass `state` (VA, MD, DC are on file) or `nec_edition`\n  (`2020`, `2023`). With none of these the tool asks; never pick an edition\n  yourself. Say which edition the answer is under.\n- **Size a feeder:** `nec.size_circuit` with `{circuit: \"feeder\", system: \"3ph_4w\",\n  voltage_v: 480, continuous_load_a: 100, length_ft: 250}` → 125 A, `#1` Cu\n  (130 A at 75°C), 1.52% drop, `#6` EGC, 1-1/2 in EMT.\n- **Long branch circuits** (site lighting at 277 V or 480 V): keep the breaker\n  with `ocpd_a: 20`; the tool upsizes the conductor for the 3% target and grows\n  the EGC with it (20 A, 300 ft, 16 A at 277 V → `#8`, `#8` EGC). Write the\n  real size in the panel schedule, never \"SEE SLD\".\n- **Demand at a panel or service:** give `nec.demand_load` the raw list of\n  every load below that panel (not the child panels' demand). Kitchen\n  equipment (220.56) is factored by the count at that panel, so the MDP and the\n  panel serving the kitchen get different factors for the same freezer.\n- Voltage-drop targets (3% branch, 3% feeder, 5% combined) are design\n  criteria, not code; the result says so. Override with `max_voltage_drop_pct`.\n- Quote the values and citations as returned. If an `error` issue comes back\n  (device smaller than the load, beyond the tables, parallel sets needed),\n  tell the person; don't work around it by hand.\n\n## Ventilation and pressurization (per space, zone and building)\n\n- **Outdoor air (`mech.ventilation_outdoor_air`, `model_id`).** `inputs: {\n  scope: \"model\" | \"level\" | \"zone\", level_id?, zone_id?, governing?, state?,\n  project_mechanical_code? }`. Returns Σ Voz under IMC and ASHRAE 62.1, the\n  exhaust required, which standard governs (the adopted code), the arithmetic\n  per space (7.5 × 70 people + 0.18 × 1,000 ft² = 705 cfm) and an issue per\n  space that can't be calculated. Outdoor air comes from each space's use,\n  people and area: a space with no use, no occupants or no outline has no\n  number; say which and fix the input, never type a value. Both standards are\n  shown; the adopted one governs. Multi-zone system efficiency (Ev) isn't\n  calculated yet: say so when asked about a VAV system.\n- **Pressurization (`mech.pressurization`, `model_id`, `{ scope, level_id?,\n  zone_id? }`).** Exhaust out against makeup air and unit outdoor air in, per\n  zone and for the building. Kitchens stay negative to dining; the building\n  slightly positive. The numbers come from the parts' `exhaust_cfm`,\n  `makeup_air_cfm`, `outdoor_cfm` (`bim_set_parameters`; a roof fan names its\n  zone in `serves_zone`); when one is missing, say which and ask, never total\n  the rest. **Untempered makeup air (`mech.makeup_air_heat`, `{ cfm, from_f,\n  to_f }`)** adds 1.08 × cfm × ΔT to the unit serving the zone (2,400 × 1.08 ×\n  (92 − 80) = 31,104 Btu/h).\n- The same numbers print live on a sheet: the ventilation or pressurization\n  table block (`add_field_block` with `block.table`, model family).\n\n## Plumbing and fuel gas\n\n- **Building sewer and sewage pumps (`plumbing.building_sewer`,\n  `plumbing.sewage_pump`).** A floor below what drains to the sewer by gravity\n  needs a sewage pump (IPC 712.1); a fixture whose flood rim is below the next\n  upstream manhole cover needs a backwater valve (IPC 715.1). Report capacity\n  as \"394 DFU on a 4\" drain at 1/8\"/ft; that size carries 180 at that slope\".\n  Never convert DFU to gpm for a pump: say it can't be calculated from fixture\n  units and ask the engineer for the inflow in gpm. An assumed invert gets a\n  question to the contractor (model family: questions), or\n  `elevation_confidence: verified` once measured. The same checks show in\n  `bim_lint_check` (rules `sewer-floor-needs-pump`, `sewer-floor-needs-backwater`,\n  `building-drain-over-capacity`, `sewer-elevation-unverified`,\n  `sewage-pump-inflow-unknown`, `sewage-pump-undersized`).\n\nTables come from the model server's code library (Virginia Plumbing Code 2021 = 2021\nIPC as amended; Virginia Fuel Gas Code 2021 = 2021 IFGC), cited like\n`VPC 2021 Table 710.1(1)` and `VFGC 2021 Table 402.4(28)`. In Virginia\ndeliverables say **VFGC / VPC**, never IFGC / IPC.\n\n- **Edition.** Pass `state` (VA → VPC / VFGC 2021; MD, DC plumbing → their\n  2021 IPC with \"check local amendments\"). MD and DC list no fuel gas code,\n  and other states aren't on file: the tool asks; ask the engineer which\n  edition applies. Never pick one yourself.\n- **Demand (`plumbing.hunter_demand`).** Pick the curve by building type:\n  restaurant → `ashrae_a`, hospital / nursing home / dormitory / hotel /\n  motel → `ashrae_b`, apartments → `ashrae_c`, offices / schools → `ashrae_d`\n  (or pass `building_type`). IPC Appendix E is not in the library: use\n  `ipc_e_tank` / `ipc_e_valve` only with the firm's points. Restaurant\n  check: 43.25 WSFU on Curve A → 48 gpm (the old built-in fit said 23.4;\n  the result shows both). `chart` returns all four curves and the operating\n  point for the sheet's graph.\n- **Pressure loss report (`plumbing.pressure_report`).** Design minimum (or\n  flow test), meter, tap, backflow (RPZ), PRV, static head (height × 0.433,\n  or the firm's 0.43), highest fixture pressure by its Table 604.3 name →\n  psi left for friction (for example: 50 − 4.5 − 25 − 0.28 − 3.01 − 9.8 = 7.41).\n  Add `developed_length_ft` for the allowable psi per 100 ft, then\n  `plumbing.size_water` with that pressure.\n- **Drains (`plumbing.size_drain`).** `role` building_drain (by slope,\n  3 in. minimum with a water closet), horizontal_branch or stack; give DFU\n  or fixtures by Table 709.1 name. Floor sinks need a rating (709.4): ask.\n- **Gas (`plumbing.size_gas`).** Give the drawn tree as segments (`parent`\n  null at the point of delivery, `length_ft`, appliance `load_mbh` at the\n  end, fittings as `equivalent_length_ft`), gas, material (`sch40`, `copper`,\n  `csst`, `pe_pipe`, `pe_tubing`), inlet and allowable drop. `method`\n  longest_length (402.4.1, default) or branch_length (402.4.2). For a 2 psi\n  system with line regulators (402.4.3 hybrid) run each pressure zone\n  separately. Sizes come from the matching 402.4(n) table, with the\n  equation beside it; when no table is loaded the tool says so and sizes by\n  the equation.\n- **A drawn gas system (`plumbing.gas_system`, `model_id`, `system_id`).**\n  Use this, not hand-typed segments, when the gas pipe is in the model.\n  Regulators split it into pressure zones: place one with\n  `bim_place_pipe_accessory` family `pipe.gas.regulator`, params `role`\n  (line, service, appliance), `outlet_in_wc` (2 psi = 55.4), `zone_drop_in_wc`,\n  `vent`; change one with `bim_swap_fitting` (same family, new params). A\n  regulator starts its zone where it sits: every branch, load point or\n  appliance past it along that pipe is on its zone, including a load at the\n  far end of a pipe whose regulator sits partway along. To keep a load on the\n  inlet zone, put the regulator on the branch that feeds only what it\n  regulates; a branch or load at the regulator's own point stays on the inlet\n  zone. The system's own inlet and drop are its design (`gasType` propane / natural,\n  `inletPressureInWc`, `allowableDropInWc`, `gasSizing` branch_length) via\n  `bim_set_system_design`. `gasType` is also the word every label, tag,\n  schedule and riser prints: set it on a propane system, never rename pipe\n  to say so. Polyethylene below grade is material `pe` (spec `sdr11`,\n  fusion fittings) and reads the PE tables (propane 402.4(35)/(36),\n  natural gas 402.4(20)-(22)). A load point on an open gas pipe end\n  (`load.mbh`) is an outlet like an appliance. The calc returns each zone\n  (pressure, drop, input, longest run, the 402.4(n) table read), every\n  pipe's needed size against the drawn one, the gas equipment rows,\n  `connections` (each outlet's input, zone, regulator, feed size,\n  `developedLengthFt` from the source along the routed pipe, `basisText`,\n  `requiredSize`) and the total input, and\n  findings: `regulator_missing` (an appliance on a zone above its 14 in. w.c.\n  maximum), `undersized_gas_pipe`, `zone_below_appliance_minimum`,\n  `regulator_over_capacity`, `regulator_raises_pressure`. Show the person the\n  changes, then `bim_size_pipes` (one approval). Example: a restaurant cook\n  line on propane, 11 in. w.c. / 0.5 in. at 100 ft: 1-1/4\" manifold, 1\" range, 3/4\"\n  fryers and grill; 1,024,407 Btu/h total. The gas equipment schedule is\n  `bim_schedule_define` `form.kind` `gas_equipment`; the per-outlet schedule\n  with developed lengths is kind `gas_connections` (`mutinybim-schedules`).\n  Gas pipe tags (template `gas_pipe`, or auto on gas pipe) print size and\n  piece length (`1\" 3 LF`); tag fields `gas.size`, `gas.length`,\n  `gas.from_source`, `gas.zone`, `gas.fuel`.\n- **A drawn water or sanitary system (`plumbing.size_system`, `model_id`,\n  `system_id`).** Use this, not hand-typed segments, when the pipe is in the\n  model. Water: fixture units to gpm on the system's curve (its own, else the\n  project's, else the building type's; with none it refuses: ask, never use\n  the old fit), a branch capped at what its fixtures draw at once; friction\n  within (supply − fixed losses − static head − critical fixture) over the\n  longest developed length + 30% fittings, set on the system with\n  `bim_set_system_design` `pipe_design` `supplyPressurePsi`, `fixedLossesPsi`,\n  `minFixturePressurePsi` (no supply: velocity only, with a finding);\n  velocity ≤ 8 ft/s cold, 5 hot; fixture supplies ≥ Table 604.5. Example, a\n  restaurant's cold water: 43.25 WSFU → 48 gpm at the 2\" service, water closet branches 3/4\".\n  Sanitary: building drain (trunk from the outfall, anything below a stack) by\n  Table 710.1(1) at its drawn fall, branches and stacks by 710.1(2), 3\" with a\n  water closet, never smaller downstream. A `system_slope_rule` finding means\n  the system's own minimum slopes are steeper than Table 704.1: set\n  `pipe_design.slopes` from the code if the engineer agrees. Returns each\n  pipe's needed size against the drawn one (`carries`, `governs`) and\n  findings. Show the changes, then `bim_size_pipes`.\n- **A drawn vent system (`plumbing.size_system` on the vent system).** Each\n  vent pipe vents the fixtures beyond it from the open air (their vent load\n  points share the fixture's mark with its waste point, or the fixture part's\n  vent connector): `carries` gives the DFU vented, the developed length and\n  the drain it serves (where those fixtures' drains meet). A vertical vent\n  serving a stack (vent stack, stack vent): Table 906.1 on the stack's size,\n  the DFU and the developed length to the open air, never under half the\n  stack or 1-1/4\". Every other vent: half the drain Table 710.1(2) needs for\n  its DFU, 1-1/4\" least, one size up over 40 ft measured to the vent stack it\n  joins (906.2, 906.3). `no_vent_terminal`: a piece with no open end to the\n  air; say so.\n- **A drawn storm system (`plumbing.size_system` on the storm system).** Roof\n  area per drain from the load point's custom quantity \"Roof area\" (sq ft),\n  else its flow turned back into area at the rate. The rate is the system's\n  `pipe_design.rainfallInPerHr` (`bim_set_system_design`); the code library\n  holds Figure 1106.1 only as map images, so with no rate the calc refuses\n  (`no_rainfall_rate`): ask the engineer for the 100-year, 1-hour rate from\n  approved local weather data, never guess one. Leaders by Table 1106.2(1),\n  horizontal drains by Table 1106.3 at their fall (1/8 in/ft least), read in\n  the next whole in/h column, never smaller downstream. Over 12 in/h\n  (leaders) or 6 in/h (horizontal) it refuses by name.\n- **Water heater / grease interceptor / meter.** These report the required\n  gal/h, gpm, lb or psi and pass/fail against a rating the engineer gives.\n  Never suggest a make or model.\n- **Auto-sizing is off by default** (check and suggest; the system's\n  `sizingMode` is `check`). Turn it on (`bim_plumbing_design`, project or one\n  system) only when the engineer asks. When a system is on auto, every saved\n  edit that touches it is followed by its resize, each its own revision and\n  undo, and the edit's result says \"Auto-sized N pipes on …\"; don't call\n  `bim_size_pipes` again for it. On check, call `bim_size_pipes` only when the\n  engineer asks.\n- **Hunter curve chart.** `plumbing.hunter_demand` `chart` gives the curves and\n  one operating point; the Plumbing tab's Sizing › Hunter curve shows every\n  water system's design point live. To put it on a sheet, `bim_draw_detail`\n  a `figure` item with `chart: \"plumbing.hunter_curve\"` (`mutinybim-drafting`);\n  it redraws live from the model and prints.\n\nOne-line diagrams that show these sizes are `mutinybim-electrical`.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_list_calcs` |\n| `bim_run_calc` |\n| `bim_suggest_duct_size` |\n| `bim_pressure_report` |\n| `bim_pipe_report` |\n| `bim_set_fitting_coefficient_override` |\n| `bim_size_pipes` |\n| `bim_service_from_equipment_list` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
  "mutinybim-schedules": "---\nname: mutinybim-schedules\ndescription: \"MutinyBIM pack for schedules: the classes and fields a schedule can show, define and change schedules, edit cell values, and the schedule library. The core reads schedule rows. Use with the mutinybim core. Trigger on: schedule, create a schedule, schedule fields, edit schedule values, schedule template, library.\"\nlicense: Apache-2.0\ncompatibility: \"Needs the mutinybim MCP server with the schedules family turned on (bim_enable_pack, --packs schedules, or /packs/schedules/mcp under the MutinyBIM base).\"\nmetadata:\n  pack: \"schedules\"\n  mcp-server: \"mutinybim\"\n  family: \"schedules\"\n  token-cap: \"1900\"\n  tools: \"bim_schedule_fields bim_schedule_define bim_schedule_edit_cells bim_library\"\n---\n\n# MutinyBIM: schedules\n\nA family of the `mutinybim` core. Activate `mutinybim` first: it has the\nscript tools, the four starting reads and the rules for every change (read before you plan, say the change in plain words, report the\nserver's answer). `bim_schedule_read` (rows of any schedule), the pictures\nand printing are the `model` family.\n\nA schedule is a view whose every value is read from the model at the revision\ndrawn. One engine serves the grid people type in, the sheet and you, for\nevery trade: equipment, air devices, fixtures, doors, rooms, beams, sprinkler\nheads, panels. Every value carries its unit. A calculated value (a formula, a\nServes total) is locked and says where it comes from.\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| `bim_schedule_fields` (read) | No `classes`: every class a schedule can list, by discipline. With `classes`: each one's fields (`path`, label, kind, unit, `edit`: instance, type, or `locked` with why, and how it totals) and its relationships (`serves`, `source`, `fed_from`…) for aggregate and expand columns. Use these exact paths |\n| `bim_schedule_define` (preview, then approval) | Create a schedule (a `definition`, a library `template` id, a built-in list as `template`: `space_loads` (Space loads: each space's estimate against the devices so far, per trade), `panel_loads` (Panel loads: each panel with the estimates and devices that name it), `loads`, `equipment_list`, `not_placed_yet`, or a `form`). Offer the built-in lists before building load columns by hand; they are the MutinyBIM editor's Views › Schedules › Load schedules or replace an existing one's definition (`schedule`). `place_on: { sheet, top_left_in? }` places a new one in the same edit. The preview shows problems in plain words or the first rows, with a `preview_token` |\n| `bim_schedule_edit_cells` (preview, then approval) | Change cells: `edits: [{ row, column, value }]` with `row` the row key and `column` the column id from `bim_schedule_read`. Quantities take a unit (`\"199 MBH\"`, `\"10'-6\\\"\"`); a bare number reads in the unit the schedule shows. The preview lists every change, every type-wide change as `\"WH-1 type: 14 parts\"`, and every refused cell with the reason |\n| `bim_library` | `list` scopes and items (and the panel / feeder form templates), `read` an item (its body and the `pin` a schedule uses), `test` an item (schema, units, expression, every example), `publish` to the firm or project library (preview, then approval; needs firm library write access) |\n\n## Preview, then the Approve card\n\n`bim_schedule_define`, `bim_schedule_edit_cells` and a `bim_library` publish\nwork in two calls:\n\n1. Call without `user_confirmed`. Nothing is saved. Read the preview and tell\n   the person in plain words what will change, **including every\n   `type_scope` line** (\"This changes all 14 parts of type WH-1, not just this\n   row\").\n2. Call again with exactly the same arguments plus `base_revision` and\n   `preview_token` from the preview and `user_confirmed: true`. That call\n   waits for the person's approval; they approve or deny it.\n   Report the saved revision, or the refusal or conflict, word for word.\n\nIf anything changed since the preview (a part was added to the type, someone\nedited the same cell), the commit is refused: preview again. Don't commit a\nbatch with refused cells; fix or drop them first.\n\n## Definitions\n\nA definition (ScheduleDefinition v2) is JSON. Only `source` and `columns` are\nrequired; the rest default (no filters, no groups, list presentation).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Water heater schedule\",\n  \"source\": { \"kind\": \"classes\", \"classes\": [\"mep.mechanical_equipment\"], \"includeSubclasses\": true },\n  \"columns\": [\n    { \"id\": \"mark\", \"kind\": \"field\", \"path\": \"mark\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mbh\", \"kind\": \"field\", \"path\": \"own.mbh\", \"total\": true },\n    { \"id\": \"btuh\", \"kind\": \"formula\", \"expression\": \"{own.mbh} * 1000\", \"unit\": \"Btu/h\" },\n    { \"id\": \"served\", \"kind\": \"aggregate\", \"fn\": \"count\", \"relationship\": \"serves\" },\n    { \"id\": \"serves\", \"kind\": \"expand\", \"relationship\": \"serves\", \"columns\": [\"mark\", \"connection_type.mark\"], \"paper\": \"nested\" }\n  ],\n  \"filters\": [{ \"path\": \"family\", \"op\": \"contains\", \"value\": \"Water heater\" }],\n  \"sort\": [{ \"path\": \"mark\", \"direction\": \"asc\" }],\n  \"grandTotals\": true\n}\n```\n\n- **Columns.** `field` (any path from `bim_schedule_fields`), `parameter` (a\n  firm shared field), `formula` (`{path}` fields, numbers with units, `+ - * /`,\n  `if`, `round`, `min`, `max`, `coalesce`, named calculations), `aggregate`\n  (`fn` sum, count, min, max, avg or list over a `relationship`, with a\n  `path`), `expand` (a sub-list under each row, `paper` nested, summary or\n  hidden). Options: `heading`, `unit`, `precision`, `total`, `hideWhenEmpty`,\n  `widthMm`, `highlights: [{ when, tone, reason }]`.\n- **Serves is one number or a sub-list.** \"Serves WSFU\" is an `aggregate`\n  sum over `serves`; \"Served fixtures\" is `list`; the full list with each\n  fixture's data is an `expand` column. A total of a Serves column is refused\n  (\"served loads are already counted on the fixtures\"); that is correct, so\n  don't work around it.\n- **Units.** A formula that adds different kinds (feet + MBH) is refused with\n  where; fix the formula. Numbers without units only work for counts.\n- **Named calculations** from the library are used through pins: `bim_library`\n  `read` the item and add its `pin` to the definition's `pins`, then call it in\n  a formula (`PZ.GasDemandBtuh({own.mbh})`). The model keeps its own copy, so a\n  later library change never alters a drawn sheet.\n- **Templates.** A firm schedule template is a definition saved in the\n  library: `bim_schedule_define` with `template: \"<item id>\"` and a `name`.\n- **Forms.** Panel, switchboard, feeder, connection type, equipment connection\n  and gas schedules print as forms: `form: { kind, panel, template_name,\n  nec_edition }` instead of a definition. Their cells come from the model\n  (circuits, loads); never type a load into one. `bim_library` `read` id\n  `form:panel` shows the template, its field paths and the template language;\n  `form.template` replaces a schedule's template.\n\n## Workflows\n\n**New equipment schedule on M-601:** `bim_schedule_fields` (no classes, then\nthe classes you need) → draft the definition → `bim_schedule_define` with\n`place_on: { sheet: \"M-601\" }` (preview: check the first rows and say what it\nlists) → commit (approval) → `bim_render_sheet_image` on M-601 in the core.\n\n**Change values from the schedule:** `bim_schedule_read` (row keys, column\nids, which columns edit per type) → `bim_schedule_edit_cells` preview → tell\nthe person every type-wide line → commit (approval) → `bim_schedule_read`\nagain to show the new values. A locked column can't be typed in: read\n`explain` and change its source instead.\n\n**Firm calculation:** draft the item (`formula` with `key`, `expression`,\n`result` kind and unit, `appliesTo`, `examples`) → `bim_library` `test` until\nit says valid → `publish` preview to the firm scope → commit only when the\nperson says it is the firm's standard.\n\nIf a tool here is missing after turning this family on, say exactly which\ntool is missing; don't substitute another.\n\n<!-- tools: rendered from the catalogue by `npm run build:files`; edit the catalogue, not this list -->\n\n## Tools in this family\n\n| Tool |\n| --- |\n| `bim_schedule_fields` |\n| `bim_schedule_define` |\n| `bim_schedule_edit_cells` |\n| `bim_library` |\n\n<!-- end tools -->\n",
};
